Below is a **complete, minimal example** that adds  

1. **a single‑user upload queue** – only the first client that is at the head of the queue may send chunk data.  
2. **a “Start upload / Finish upload” button** – the next client in the line is allowed to start only after the current user clicks **Finish**.  
3. **download capability** – a page that lists every file that has been uploaded and serves the file on demand.

The solution uses only the Python **standard library** and Flask.  
If you want a production‑ready queue you would replace the in‑memory structures with Redis or a DB, but the idea stays exactly the same.

---

## 1. High‑level design  

| Component | What it does |
|-----------|--------------|
| **global `UploadQueue` object** | Holds the FIFO queue (`list[client_id]`), the id of the client that is currently allowed to upload, and a list of finished files. All operations are protected by a `threading.Lock` so they are safe even when Flask runs with multiple threads. |
| **client identifier** | A UUID stored in a Flask signed cookie (`session['client_id']`). It survives page refreshes and is used to recognise the client in all later requests. |
| **/join‑queue (POST)** | First thing a browser does after the page loads – it registers the client in the queue and returns its current position. |
| **/queue‑status (GET)** | Returns `{ "position": n, "allowed": true/false }`. The front‑end polls this endpoint every second. When `allowed` becomes `true` the upload button is enabled. |
| **/start‑upload (POST)** | Called when the user clicks the *Start upload* button. It simply checks that the client is still at the head of the queue and returns *200* if it may continue, otherwise *423 (Locked)*. |
| **/upload (POST)** | The Dropzone chunk handler you already have. It now checks the client id against `UploadQueue.current_uploader`. If the client is not allowed, a **423** response is sent and Dropzone will stop sending chunks. |
| **/finish‑upload (POST)** | Sent by the client after the last chunk has been uploaded (or any time the user decides the upload is “finished”). It removes the client from the queue, marks the upload as finished, and makes the **next** client in line eligible to start. |
| **/files (GET)** | A simple page that lists all finished files with a *download* link. |
| **/download/&lt;filename&gt; (GET)** | Streams the requested file from the `static/img` folder using Flask’s `send_from_directory`. |

---

