```rust
eprintln!();                     // move the cursor to the next line after the progress bar
info!("Done Mapping Reads");
Ok(())
```

The function ends with an `Ok(())`. All errors that could happen inside the worker threads are either turned into a panic (the `Err(err) => panic!` line) or propagated through the channel (`tx.send(...).expect`). If you wanted proper error handling you would replace those `panic!`/`expect` calls with `Result`s that bubble up to the caller.

---

## 9. Summary diagram

```
+----------------+      +-------------------+      +-----------------------+
|  Main thread   |      |  Worker threads   |      |  Consumer (main)      |
| (creates chan) | ---> |  (num_threads)    | ---> |  reads from rx.iter() |
+----------------+      +-------------------+      +-----------------------+

   ^                     ^        ^                ^
   |                     |        |                |
   |                     |        |                |
   |                     |        |                |
   |   (sync_channel)    |        |                |
   +---------------------+        +----------------+

Shared objects:
- Arc<Mutex<fastq::records()>>   --->  only ONE thread at a time can call .next()
- tx.clone() per worker           --->  each worker can send its own messages
- Each worker sends:
      Some((bool, id, eq_class, cov))   // normal result
      None                               // sentinel when EOF reached
```

---

## 10. Points you may want to tweak / improve

| Area | What could be changed | Why |
|------|-----------------------|-----|
| **Error handling** | Replace `panic!` and `.expect()` with proper `Result`s. | Prevent the whole process from crashing on a single corrupted FASTQ record. |
| **Sentinel handling** | Instead of counting `None`s, you could close the sending side (`drop(tx)`) after all workers exit and just break on `rx.iter().next()` returning `None`. | Simpler logic, less bookkeeping. |
| **Load balancing** | The current design is *pull‑based*: a single mutex protects the iterator, which can become a bottleneck if `num_threads` is huge. An alternative is to pre‑split the file into *chunks* (byte ranges) and give each thread its own iterator. | Better scalability on many cores. |
| **Channel capacity** | Setting capacity to `num_threads` is fine, but you could use an *unbounded* channel (`mpsc::channel`) if you prefer the workers never block on send (at the cost of higher memory usage). | Depends on the expected size of the payload per read. |
