## 5.  `enqueue` – add a value at the rear

```c
void enqueue(int value)
{
    struct node *ptr;                        // 1. Declare a pointer for the new node
    ptr = (struct node *)malloc(sizeof(struct node));
                                            // 2. Allocate memory for one node on the heap
    ptr->data = value;                       // 3. Store the user’s value in the new node
    ptr->next = NULL;                        // 4. It will become the last node, so its next is NULL

    /* 5. If the queue is empty (both front and rear are NULL) … */
    if (front == NULL && rear == NULL) {
        front = rear = ptr;                  // … the new node is both the head and the tail
    } else {
        rear->next = ptr;                    // 6. Otherwise, link the current tail to the new node
        rear = ptr;                          // 7. Update the tail pointer to the new node
    }

    printf("Node is Inserted\n\n");          // 8. Feedback to the user
}
```

### What happens internally?

* **Memory allocation**: `malloc` returns a pointer to a block of memory large enough to hold a `struct node`.  
* **Linking**: When the queue already has elements, `rear->next = ptr` attaches the new node to the end of the list.  
* **Tail update**: `rear = ptr` moves the “tail” marker so that future enqueues know where to add the next node.

---

## 6.  `dequeue` – remove the front element

```c
int dequeue(void)
{
    if (front == NULL) {                     // 1. Empty queue?  (no head node)
        printf("\nUnderflow\n");             //    Underflow = trying to remove from empty
        return -1;                           //    Return a sentinel value
    } else {
        struct node *temp = front;           // 2. Keep a temporary pointer to the node being removed
        int temp_data = front->data;         // 3. Save the data to return it later

        front = front->next;                 // 4. Advance the front pointer to the next node
        /* If the queue became empty, also reset rear */
        if (front == NULL) {                 //    (important – otherwise rear still points to freed memory)
            rear = NULL;
        }

        free(temp);                          // 5. Return the memory of the removed node to the heap
        return temp_data;                    // 6. Give the caller the value that was stored
    }
}
```

### Why do we need `temp`?

We must **free** the memory that was allocated for the removed node, but we can’t free `front` after we have already moved `front` to the next node – we would lose the address to the block we need to release. The temporary pointer preserves that address.

---

## 7.  `display` – print the whole queue

```c
void display(void)
{
    struct node *temp;                       // 1. Traversal pointer

    if (front == NULL && rear == NULL) {     // 2. Empty queue?
        printf("\nQueue is Empty\n");
    } else {
        printf("The queue is \n");
        temp = front;                        // 3. Start at the head

        while (temp) {                       // 4. Walk until we hit NULL
            printf("%d--->", temp->data);    //    Print each value
            temp = temp->next;               //    Move to the next node
        }
        printf("NULL\n\n");                  // 5. Terminator to show the end of the list
    }
}
```

The loop is a classic *linked‑list traversal*: follow `next` pointers until they become `NULL`.

