Below is a **complete walk‑through of the problems in the snippet you posted** and a **drop‑in “fixed” version** that you can copy‑paste into your driver (or use as a reference when you tidy things up).

---

## 1️⃣  What the kernel expects from a *probe* function

| What you do | What the kernel expects / best‑practice |
|-------------|------------------------------------------|
| `return -1;` on a DT‑read failure | Return a proper **negative errno** (`-EINVAL`, `-ENODEV`, `-EIO`, …).  `-1` is interpreted as `-EPERM` which is misleading and makes debugging hard. |
| No `i2c_set_clientdata()`/`dev_set_drvdata()` | Most drivers store a private data pointer for later use in callbacks (`remove()`, sysfs ops, etc.). If you never set it you lose a convenient way to get back to `struct Huawei_GPU_data`. |
| `data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group;` – no explicit terminator | `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups()` **requires** that the *array of group pointers* be **NULL‑terminated**.  Because you allocated the whole `struct Huawei_GPU_data` with `devm_kzalloc()` (zeroed memory) the extra entries are already `NULL`, but it is good practice to make the termination explicit for readability and to protect you from later modifications. |
| No `EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL` (not required here) | Not a bug, just a reminder for modules that want to expose symbols. |
| Missing `#include`s for the HWMON API | Include `<linux/hwmon.h>` and `<linux/hwmon-sysfs.h>` otherwise you’ll get implicit‑function warnings. |
| Return value of `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups()` is stored in `hwmon_dev` but you never propagate the *error* if it fails (you do, but you could be more explicit). | It’s fine; just make sure you *always* return the error code (`PTR_ERR()`). |
| No `MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE` (probably in another file) | Keep it – otherwise `modprobe` can’t bind the driver. |
| No *module init/exit* shown | Assume you have them elsewhere. |

---

## 2️⃣  The “big‑picture” issues that cause crashes / probe failures

### a) Returning `-1` (or any non‑standard error) from `probe()`

The kernel treats any **negative** return as a failure, but the *value* is used to print the error message:

```c
probe of <device> failed with error -1
```

`-1` is **`-EPERM`**, which tells the I²C core that the driver “doesn’t have permission”.  In almost every driver the failure will be either:

| Situation | Proper errno |
|-----------|--------------|
| Missing/invalid DT property (`of_property_read_u32`) | `-EINVAL` or `-ENODEV` |
| I²C adapter does not support the required SMBus operations | `-EIO` (you already use it) |
| Out‑of‑memory (`devm_kzalloc`) | `-ENOMEM` (you already use it) |
| `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups()` fails | whatever `PTR_ERR()` returns (often `-ENODEV` or `-ENOMEM`) |

**Fix**:

```c
if (of_property_read_u32(dev->of_node, "reg", &reg_device))
    return -EINVAL;   /* or -ENODEV if you think the node is mis‑configured */
```

### b) Missing driver‑data association

Later in the driver you will probably need to read or write registers in response to sysfs reads (`temp_input` for example).  The common pattern is:

```c
static ssize_t temp_show(struct device *dev,
                         struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
{
    struct Huawei_GPU_data *data = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
    /* …use data… */
}
```

If you never called `i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);` (or the equivalent `dev_set_drvdata(dev, data)`), `dev_get_drvdata()` will return **NULL**, leading to NULL‑pointer dereferences.

**Fix**:

```c
i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);   /* after you have allocated `data` */
```

(If you already use `dev_set_drvdata(dev, data)`, that works as well – they are synonyms for I²C drivers.)

### c) HWMON group termination

You allocate the whole `struct Huawei_GPU_data` with `devm_kzalloc()`. Because of the `kzalloc` the `groups[]` array is zeroed, so the last entry *is* `NULL`.  That’s technically fine, but:

* If you ever add a second group you must remember to set the next slot to `NULL`;
* If you later change the struct layout (e.g. move `groups` into a sub‑structure) you could inadvertently lose the terminator.

For readability and future‑proofing you should **explicitly set the terminator**.

```c
data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group;
data->groups[1] = NULL;   /* explicit */
```

### d) No check for `client->name` being NULL

When you call `devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups(dev, client->name, …)`, the kernel will use the given name as the *sensor name*.  On some platforms (e.g. DT‑only devices) `client->name` can be NULL.  The HWMON core will happily accept a NULL name, but you’ll get a very unhelpful “sensor '<none>'” in dmesg.  It’s safer to supply a fallback:

```c
const char *sensor_name = client->name ? client->name : "huawei_gpu";
hwmon_dev = devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups(dev,
                                                    sensor_name,
                                                    data,
                                                    data->groups);
```

---

## 3️⃣  A “clean” implementation

Below is a **drop‑in replacement** for the function you posted.  All the points above are addressed, plus a few extra niceties (inline comments, better error messages, proper `dev_info` formatting).

```c
/*
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 *
 * Huawei GPU temperature sensor driver – I²C probe
 *
 * The driver is deliberately tiny because the only thing we need
 * is a single temperature reading exposed via hwmon.
 *
 * If you need extra attributes (fan speed, voltage, …) add more
 * attribute groups and fill in the appropriate read/write callbacks.
 */

#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/i2c.h>
#include <linux/of.h>
#include <linux/mutex.h>
#include <linux/hwmon.h>
#include <linux/hwmon-sysfs.h>
#include <linux/errno.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>

struct Huawei_GPU_data {
    struct i2c_client *client;
    struct mutex       update_lock;   /* protect multi‑byte reads */
    u32                read_reg;      /* register that holds the temperature */
    const struct attribute_group *groups[2];   /* NULL‑terminated array */
};

/* Forward declaration – you already have this defined somewhere */
extern const struct attribute_group temp_dev_group;

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  Probe function                                                         */
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
static int Huawei_GPU_probe(struct i2c_client *client,
                            const struct i2c_device_id *id)
{
    struct device *dev = &client->dev;
    struct device *hwmon_dev;
    struct Huawei_GPU_data *data;
    u32 reg_device;
    int ret;
    const char *sensor_name;

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  1️⃣  Verify the adapter can do the SMBus ops we need
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    if (!i2c_check_functionality(client->adapter,
                                 I2C_FUNC_SMBUS_BYTE_DATA |
                                 I2C_FUNC_SMBUS_WORD_DATA)) {
        dev_err(dev, "I2C adapter does not support required SMBus ops\n");
        return -EIO;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  2️⃣  Allocate driver‑private data (zeroed, automatically freed)
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    data = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*data), GFP_KERNEL);
    if (!data)
        return -ENOMEM;

    data->client = client;
    mutex_init(&data->update_lock);

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  3️⃣  Pull the register address from the DT (or fallback to a constant)
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    ret = of_property_read_u32(dev->of_node, "reg", &reg_device);
    if (ret) {
        dev_err(dev, "Missing mandatory 'reg' property in DT node\n");
        return -EINVAL;      /* or -ENODEV if you prefer */
    }
    data->read_reg = reg_device;

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  4️⃣  Fill the attribute‑group array (must be NULL‑terminated)
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group;
    data->groups[1] = NULL;   /* explicit terminator – helps readability */

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  5️⃣  Register the hwmon device
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    sensor_name = client->name ? client->name : "huawei_gpu";
    hwmon_dev = devm_hwmon_device_register_with_groups(dev,
                                                        sensor_name,
                                                        data,
                                                        data->groups);
    if (IS_ERR(hwmon_dev))
        return PTR_ERR(hwmon_dev);

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------------
     *  6️⃣  Store driver data for later callbacks (sysfs, remove, …)
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);   /* same as dev_set_drvdata(dev, data) */

    dev_info(dev, "%s: registered hwmon device '%s'\n",
             dev_name(hwmon_dev), sensor_name);

    return 0;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  Optional: remove() – clean‑up (not strictly needed with devm_*)       */
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
static int Huawei_GPU_remove(struct i8c_client *client)
{
    /* devm_* helpers already free everything, but you can do extra work
       here (e.g. disable a hardware monitor) if required. */
    return 0;
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  I²C device ID table & OF match                                        */
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
static const struct i2c_device_id huawei_gpu_id[] = {
    { "huawei_gpu", 0 },
    { }
};
MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(i2c, huawei_gpu_id);

static const struct of_device_id huawei_gpu_of_match[] = {
    { .compatible = "huawei,gpu-temp" },
    { }
};
MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, huawei_gpu_of_match);

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  I²C driver struct                                                     */
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
static struct i2c_driver huawei_gpu_driver = {
    .driver = {
        .name   = "huawei_gpu",
        .of_match_table = huawei_gpu_of_match,
    },
    .probe      = Huawei_GPU_probe,
    .remove     = Huawei_GPU_remove,
    .id_table   = huawei_gpu_id,
};
module_i2c_driver(huawei_gpu_driver);

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  Module metadata                                                       */
/* ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
MODULE_AUTHOR("Your Name <you@example.com>");
MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Huawei GPU temperature sensor driver (hwmon)");
MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
```

### What changed? (Summary)

| Old line / behaviour | New line / behaviour | Why it matters |
|----------------------|----------------------|----------------|
| `return -1;` on DT read failure | `return -EINVAL;` (or `-ENODEV`) | Proper errno, clearer `dmesg`. |
| No driver data stored | `i2c_set_clientdata(client, data);` | Allows callbacks to retrieve private data. |
| `data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group;` (no explicit terminator) | `data->groups[0] = &temp_dev_group; data->groups[1] = NULL;` | Guarantees NULL‑termination regardless of future changes. |
