**C program – one‑letter input only**

The program reads a whole line (so the user can type anything and press **Enter**) and then checks how many characters were really entered.  
If the line contains **exactly one alphabetic character** the program accepts it; otherwise it prints an error message.

```c
/********************************************************************
 *  one_letter.c
 *
 *  Prompt the user for a single letter.
 *  If the user types more than one character (or nothing at all)
 *  an error message is displayed.
 *
 *  Compile (gcc):  gcc -Wall -Wextra -o one_letter one_letter.c
 ********************************************************************/
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* buffer big enough to hold any reasonable line the user may type */
    char line[256];

    printf("Please enter a single letter: ");

    /* read the whole line, including the trailing '\n' */
    if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Input error.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    /* Remove the trailing newline, if present */
    size_t len = strlen(line);
    if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\n')
        line[--len] = '\0';          /* shrink the string */
    else
        /* the line was longer than our buffer – discard the rest */
        while (getchar() != '\n' && !feof(stdin));

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------
       Validation:
       * exactly one character must be present
       * that character must be a letter (A‑Z or a‑z)
       ------------------------------------------------------------ */
    if (len == 1 && isalpha((unsigned char)line[0])) {
        printf("You entered the letter '%c'.\n", line[0]);
        return 0;
    }

    /* If we get here the input was not a single letter */
    fprintf(stderr, "Error: please enter **exactly one** letter.\n");
    return 1;
}
```

### How it works

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **1.** `fgets` | Reads the whole line the user types, up to the buffer size. |
| **2.** Remove `'\n'` | The newline that `Enter` produces is stripped so that we can count only the characters the user actually typed. |
| **3.** Length test | `len == 1` guarantees that the user typed **exactly** one character (no extra spaces, digits, etc.). |
| **4.** `isalpha` | Makes sure that the single character is a letter (`A‑Z` or `a‑z`). |
| **5.** Output | If both tests succeed the program prints the entered letter; otherwise an error message is shown. |

### Sample runs

```
