**Explanation**

The routine `function()` already builds a DP table `freq[]` where  

`freq[i] = length of the longest “consecutive‑by‑1” subsequence that ends in arr[i]`.

To obtain the *indices* of that subsequence we only have to remember **which element
pre‑cedes* each `arr[i]` in the optimal chain.  
We do that with another array `prev[]` :

* `prev[i] = j` – the index of the element that precedes `arr[i]` in the longest
  subsequence that ends in `arr[i]`.  
* If `arr[i]` starts a new chain, `prev[i] = -1`.

While we are filling `freq[]` we also fill `prev[]`.  
After the double loop we know the overall maximum length (`maxLen`) and the
position `lastIdx` where that maximum occurs.  
Starting from `lastIdx` we walk backwards through `prev[]`, store the visited
indices in a temporary array, then reverse that array so the indices are printed
in the original left‑to‑right order.

The rest of the program (reading the input, printing the length) stays the same;
only the helper routine and a small part of `main()` are changed.

---

```c
#include <stdio.h>

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Returns the length of the longest subsequence whose elements are
   consecutive integers (each element is exactly 1 larger than the
   previous one) and also stores the indices of that subsequence in
   the array *outIdx (0‑based). The caller must provide an array of
   size at least n for outIdx.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
int longest_consecutive_subseq(int arr[], int n, int outIdx[])
{
    int freq[n];      /* DP table – length of the best chain ending at i   */
    int prev[n];      /* predecessor index for the chain ending at i    */
    int maxLen = 1;   /* at least one element forms a chain            */
    int lastIdx = 0;  /* position where the overall maximum is found    */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        freq[i] = 1;      /* a single element alone                     */
        prev[i] = -1;     /* no predecessor yet                         */

        for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
            /* We can extend the chain that ends at j only
               if arr[i] = arr[j] + 1                              */
            if (arr[j] + 1 == arr[i] && freq[j] + 1 > freq[i]) {
                freq[i] = freq[j] + 1;
                prev[i] = j;           /* remember the predecessor   */
            }
        }

        /* Update the global maximum                           */
        if (freq[i] > maxLen) {
            maxLen = freq[i];
            lastIdx = i;
        }
    }

    /* -----------------------------------------------------------------
       Reconstruct the indices of the longest subsequence.
       We walk backwards from lastIdx using prev[] and store the
       visited positions in a temporary buffer, then reverse it.
       ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    int temp[n];
    int cnt = 0;
    for (int cur = lastIdx; cur != -1; cur = prev[cur]) {
        temp[cnt++] = cur;                 /* store in reverse order */
    }

    /* reverse into outIdx so they appear left‑to‑right */
    for (int i = 0; i < cnt; ++i) {
        outIdx[i] = temp[cnt - 1 - i];
    }

    return maxLen;        /* length of the longest subsequence */
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(void)
{
    int n;
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1) return 0;

    int arr[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
    }

    /* Array that will receive the indices of the longest subsequence */
    int idx[n];

    int length = longest_consecutive_subseq(arr, n, idx);

    printf("%d\n", length);

    /* Print the indices (0‑based). If you prefer 1‑based, add +1 while printing */
    for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
        printf("%d ", idx[i]);   /* change to idx[i]+1 for 1‑based output */
    }
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```

**What changed**

| Part | Original | New |
|------|----------|-----|
| DP routine | only `freq[]` and `max` | added `prev[]`, returned length, filled `outIdx[]` |
| `main` | called `function()` and printed only the length | calls the new routine, prints the length **and** the indices |
| Output format | `length` only | `length` on the first line, then a space‑separated list of indices on the second line (0‑based; change `+1` if 1‑based is required) |

The algorithm still runs in **O(n²)** time and **O(n)** extra memory, exactly as the original code, but now it also tells you *where* the elements of the longest consecutive subsequence are located in the original array.

