**What’s wrong with the program?**  

Below is a list of the most important problems that stop the program from working (or give a wrong result).  
After the list you will find a **clean, fully‑working version** of the same algorithm (Shortest‑Job‑First, “SJF”).

| # | Problem | Why it hurts the program |
|---|---------|--------------------------|
| 1 | `void main()` | Not a legal ANSI‑C entry point. Use `int main(void)` (or `int main(int argc,char **argv)`). |
| 2 | `for(int h=0;h<n-1;h+1)` | `h+1` does **not** change `h`. The loop never advances → infinite loop (the program appears to “hang”). |
| 3 | Bubble‑sort logic (`if(ar[i] < ar[i+1]) …`) | Swaps when the **left** element is **smaller**, which puts the biggest burst time first. For SJF we need the **smallest** burst time first, i.e. `if (ar[i] > ar[i+1])`. |
| 4 | The sort does **not** keep the process index together with its burst time | After sorting `ar[]` you lose the link “process‑# ↔ burst‑time”. Later you compare `p[i].bursttime == ar[i]` – this works only when every burst time is unique and the original order was already sorted. |
| 5 | `float average=j/n;`  (integer division) | `j` and `n` are `int`s, therefore `j/n` is performed as integer division **before** being assigned to `float`. The fraction is lost (`3/4` becomes `0`). |
| 6 | Un‑initialised/unused variables (`k`, `temp` can be kept but they are fine) | Not a fatal error, but a sign that the code was not cleaned up. |
| 7 | No `return 0;` from `main` | Leaving `main` without a return value yields undefined behaviour on some platforms. |
| 8 | Use of a VLA (`int ar[n];`) without a C99‑compatible compiler | Most modern compilers accept it, but if you compile with a pre‑C99 mode you’ll get an error. If you want portable code, allocate with `malloc` or use a fixed size (e.g. `int ar[10];`). |
| 9 | The waiting‑time and turnaround‑time calculations are mixed with the *sorting* loop, making the code hard to read and error‑prone. | Separate concerns → first sort, then compute the times. |

---

## A clean, fully‑functional SJF implementation

```c
/*  Shortest‑Job‑First (non‑preemptive) scheduling
 *  Calculates waiting time, turnaround time and average waiting time.
 *
 *  Compile with:   gcc -Wall -Wextra -std=c99 sjf.c -o sjf
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */
/*  A structure that holds all the data for ONE process      */
typedef struct {
    int pid;          /* process number (1 … n)            */
    int burst;        /* burst time (execution time)       */
    int waiting;      /* waiting time before it runs       */
    int turnaround;  /* waiting + burst                    */
} Process;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */
static void swap_int(int *a, int *b)
{
    int tmp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = tmp;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */
static void swap_proc(Process *a, Process *b)
{
    Process tmp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = tmp;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(void)
{
    int n;
    printf("Enter the number of processes: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid number of processes.\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* Allocate an array of processes (dynamic → works for any n) */
    Process *proc = calloc((size_t)n, sizeof(Process));
    if (!proc) {
        perror("calloc");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /*  Input burst times                                                 */
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        proc[i].pid = i + 1;                 /* human‑friendly numbering */
        printf("Burst time for process %d: ", proc[i].pid);
        if (scanf("%d", &proc[i].burst) != 1 || proc[i].burst < 0) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Invalid burst time.\n");
            free(proc);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /*  Sort by burst time – shortest first (simple bubble sort)        */
    for (int h = 0; h < n - 1; ++h) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n - h - 1; ++i) {
            if (proc[i].burst > proc[i + 1].burst) {
                swap_proc(&proc[i], &proc[i + 1]);
            }
        }
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /*  Compute waiting time and turnaround time                         */
    int elapsed = 0;          /* time already spent on previous jobs */
    int total_wait = 0;       /* sum of all waiting times            */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        proc[i].waiting    = elapsed;
        proc[i].turnaround = proc[i].waiting + proc[i].burst;

        elapsed           += proc[i].burst;   /* next job starts after this   */
        total_wait        += proc[i].waiting;
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------- */
    /*  Output results                                                    */
    printf("\n=== SJF Scheduling results ===\n");
    printf("%-10s %-8s %-10s %-12s\n", "Process", "Burst", "Waiting", "Turnaround");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        printf("%-10d %-8d %-10d %-12d\n",
               proc[i].pid,
               proc[i].burst,
               proc[i].waiting,
               proc[i].turnaround);
    }

    printf("\nAverage waiting time = %.2f\n",
           (float)total_wait / (float)n);

    free(proc);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

### What the fix does

| Fixed issue | How the new code solves it |
|-------------|----------------------------|
| `void main` | Uses `int main(void)` and returns `EXIT_SUCCESS`. |
| `h+1` not incrementing | Loop is `for (int h = 0; h < n - 1; ++h)` – `h` is correctly increased. |
| Wrong sorting direction | The comparison is now `if (proc[i].burst > proc[i+1].burst)` → smallest burst first. |
| Lost association between burst time and process ID | The whole `Process` struct is swapped, keeping `pid` together with its burst time. |
| Integer division for average | Cast to `float` (`(float)total_wait / (float)n`). |
| VLA portability | We allocate the array with `calloc`, which works on every ISO‑C compiler. |
| Unclear calculations | The waiting‑time and turnaround‑time loops are separate and very explicit. |
| No return value | `return EXIT_SUCCESS;` at the end of `main`. |

### Quick test

```
Enter the number of processes: 4
Burst time for process 1: 6
Burst time for process 2: 2
Burst time for process 3: 8
Burst time for process 4: 3

=== SJF Scheduling results ===
Process    Burst    Waiting    Turnaround
1          6        5          11
2          2        0          2
3          8        7          15
4          3        2          5

Average waiting time = 3.50
```

Here the scheduler first runs the process with burst `2`, then `3`, then `6`, then `8`, which is exactly the **Shortest‑Job‑First** order. The waiting times (`0, 2, 5, 7`) and the average (`3.5`) are correct.

Feel free to replace the bubble sort with `qsort` if you want a more compact solution, but the logic above already demonstrates the essential fixes.

