*Lemma 3* – After the two loops that call `process_votes` on every line of the
vote part, `totalVotes` equals the total number of votes in the file,
`validVotes` equals the number of valid votes, `invalidVotes` equals the number
of invalid votes and `score[i]` equals the number of votes received by candidate
`i+1`.

*Proof.*  
The first call processes the first vote line (the line that terminated the
candidate‑reading loop). The second loop processes all remaining lines of the
file, which are exactly the remaining vote lines.  
By Lemma&nbsp;2 each call updates the four counters correctly for the processed
line and does not affect votes that belong to other lines.  
Summing over *all* lines yields the exact totals described. ∎



*Lemma 4* – `maxScore` computed after the vote counting is the greatest number
of votes received by any candidate.

*Proof.*  
`maxScore` is defined as the maximum element of the `score` array.
By Lemma&nbsp;3 each `score[i]` equals the votes received by candidate `i+1`.  
Therefore the maximum of these values is precisely the greatest vote count. ∎



*Lemma 5* – The list printed after the line "`Les gagnants:`" contains **exactly**
the names of all candidates whose vote count equals `maxScore`.

*Proof.*  
The program iterates over every index `i`.  
If `score[i] == maxScore`, the corresponding candidate name `candidates[i]`
is printed, otherwise nothing is printed.  
By Lemma&nbsp;4 `maxScore` is the maximal vote count, consequently the printed
names are precisely those of the candidates that achieve this maximal count. ∎



*Theorem* – The program produces a file `resultats.txt` whose content satisfies
all requirements of the exercise.

*Proof.*  

* Invalid votes: By Lemma&nbsp;2 each invalid vote is printed exactly once as
  "`Vote invalide : v`" when it is read, and no other line is printed in this
  section.

* Summary numbers: After all votes are processed, Lemma&nbsp;3 guarantees that
  `totalVotes`, `validVotes` and `invalidVotes` hold the required numbers.
  The program writes them in the prescribed order.

* Candidate scores: Again by Lemma&nbsp;3 `score[i]` equals the votes received
  by candidate `i+1`. The program prints the header "`Candidat score`"
  followed by each name together with its corresponding `score[i]`, exactly as
  required.

* Winners: By Lemma&nbsp;5 the program outputs precisely the candidate(s) with
  the highest score.

Therefore every line of the required output appears once and is correct.
∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Complexity Analysis  

*Let*  

* `c` – number of candidates  
* `v` – total number of votes (including invalid ones)

Reading the candidate names takes **O(c)** time and **O(c)** memory (the names).  
Processing the votes scans each vote once, therefore **O(v)** time.  
All other work (searching the maximum, printing) is **O(c)**.  

Overall:

* **Time:** `O(c + v)`
* **Memory:** `O(c)` (array of scores + candidate strings)

Both are optimal – the program has to look at every vote at least once.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Reference Implementation (C 99)

```c
/*  vote_results.c
 *
 *  Reads the file "votes.txt" (candidates + votes) and writes the election
 *  results to "resultats.txt" exactly as required by the statement.
 *
 *  Compile:  gcc -Wall -Wextra -std=c99 vote_results.c -o vote_results
 *  Run:      ./vote_results
 *
 *  The program assumes that "votes.txt" exists in the current directory.
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

#define MAX_LINE 256          /* maximum length of a line in the input   */
#define MAX_CANDIDATES 100    /* arbitrary upper bound for candidates    */

/*------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/* Helper: remove trailing '\n' (if present)                       */
static void chomp(char *s)
{
    size_t len = strlen(s);
    if (len && s[len - 1] == '\n')
        s[len - 1] = '\0';
}

/*------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/* Checks whether a line (after leading blanks) begins with a digit */
static int line_starts_with_digit(const char *s)
{
    while (isspace((unsigned char)*s))
        ++s;
    return isdigit((unsigned char)*s);
}

/*------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/* Process one line that contains votes.  The results are stored in
 * the arrays passed as parameters and the invalid votes are printed
 * directly to the output file.                                    */
static void process_votes(const char *line,
                           int nCand,
                           int *score,
                           long *totalVotes,
                           long *validVotes,
                           long *invalidVotes,
                           FILE *out)
{
    char buf[MAX_LINE];
    strncpy(buf, line, MAX_LINE - 1);
    buf[MAX_LINE - 1] = '\0';

    char *token = strtok(buf, " \t\r\n");
    while (token) {
        int v = atoi(token);
        ++(*totalVotes);
        if (v >= 1 && v <= nCand) {
            ++(*validVotes);
            ++score[v - 1];
        } else {
            ++(*invalidVotes);
            fprintf(out, "Vote invalide : %d\n", v);
        }
        token = strtok(NULL, " \t\r\n");
    }
}

/*------------------------------------------------------------------*/
int main(void)
{
    FILE *fin  = fopen("votes.txt", "r");
    if (!fin) {
        perror("cannot open votes.txt");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    FILE *fout = fopen("resultats.txt", "w");
    if (!fout) {
        perror("cannot create resultats.txt");
        fclose(fin);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---------- 1. read candidates --------------------------------- */
    char *candidates[MAX_CANDIDATES];
    int nCand = 0;
    char line[MAX_LINE];

    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fin)) {
        chomp(line);
        if (line[0] == '\0')          /* ignore empty lines */
            continue;

        if (line_starts_with_digit(line)) {
            /* this line belongs to the vote part -> stop reading names */
            break;
        }

        if (nCand >= MAX_CANDIDATES) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Too many candidates (limit %d)\n", MAX_CANDIDATES);
            fclose(fin);
            fclose(fout);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }

        /* store the whole line as the candidate's name */
        candidates[nCand] = malloc(strlen(line) + 1);
        if (!candidates[nCand]) {
            perror("malloc");
            fclose(fin);
            fclose(fout);
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }
        strcpy(candidates[nCand], line);
        ++nCand;
    }

    if (nCand == 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "No candidate found in votes.txt\n");
        fclose(fin);
        fclose(fout);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* ---------- 2. initialise counters ------------------------------ */
    int score[MAX_CANDIDATES] = {0};
    long totalVotes   = 0;
    long validVotes   = 0;
    long invalidVotes = 0;

    /* ---------- 3. process the first line that already contains votes */
    if (line[0] != '\0')                /* the line that stopped the loop */
        process_votes(line, nCand, score,
                      &totalVotes, &validVotes, &invalidVotes, fout);

    /* ---------- 4. process remaining vote lines -------------------- */
    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fin)) {
        chomp(line);
        if (line[0] == '\0')
            continue;                  /* ignore empty lines */
        process_votes(line, nCand, score,
                      &totalVotes, &validVotes, &invalidVotes, fout);
    }

    /* ---------- 5. write the summary -------------------------------- */
    fprintf(fout, "Nombre d'électeurs : %ld\n", totalVotes);
    fprintf(fout, "Nombre de votes valides : %ld\n", validVotes);
    fprintf(fout, "Nombre de votes annules : %ld\n", invalidVotes);

    fprintf(fout, "Candidat score\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < nCand; ++i) {
        fprintf(fout, "%s %d\n", candidates[i], score[i]);
    }

    /* ---------- 6. find and write the winner(s) -------------------- */
    int maxScore = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nCand; ++i)
        if (score[i] > maxScore)
            maxScore = score[i];

    fprintf(fout, "Les gagnants:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < nCand; ++i)
        if (score[i] == maxScore)
            fprintf(fout, "%s\n", candidates[i]);

    /* ---------- 7. clean up ------------------------------------------ */
    for (int i = 0; i < nCand; ++i)
        free(candidates[i]);

    fclose(fin);
    fclose(fout);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

**Explanation of the code**

* The program first reads all lines that **do not** start with a digit and stores
  them as candidate names (`candidates` array).  
* The first line that *does* start with a digit is the beginning of the vote
  section; it is processed together with the rest of the file by the routine
  `process_votes`.  
* `process_votes` tokenises a line, converts every token to an integer `v`,
  updates the global counters and prints an “invalid vote” line when needed.  
* After all votes have been examined the program writes the three totals,
  the table *Candidat score*, and finally the winner(s) – all exactly in the
  format required by the statement.  

The program follows the algorithm proven correct above, runs in linear time
with respect to the size of the input, and uses only a modest amount of memory.

