**Solution Overview**

The task is to find a *most reliable* path between two vertices of an undirected (or directed) graph.  
Every edge `e = (u , v)` has a reliability `p(e)  ( 0 < p ≤ 1 )`.  
The reliability of a path is the product of reliabilities of all its edges  

```
R(path) = Π p(e)      (e belonging to the path)
```

We have to maximise this product.

The product can be handled directly – it is a monotone transformation, therefore a
Dijkstra‑like greedy algorithm works, if we always relax edges with the **largest**
current probability.  
(Equivalently, one could minimise `‑log(p)`, but it is not necessary.)

Because the statement forbids the use of `<vector>` and `<queue>`, we build the
graph with classic C‑style arrays and we implement the “priority queue’’ of Dijkstra
by a simple `O(N²)` linear scan – it is fast enough for typical educational limits
(`N ≤ 10⁴` easily fits into the time limit).

The program:

* reads the graph,
* runs the maximal‑product Dijkstra,
* restores the best path,
* prints the reliability (as a floating point number with 6 digits after the dot)
  and the sequence of vertices of the optimal route.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Data structures (no `<vector>`, no `<queue>`)

```text
struct Edge               // one directed edge
{
    int   to;             // neighbouring vertex
    double p;             // reliability of this edge
    int   next;           // index of the next edge in the adjacency list
};

int *head;                // head[v] = index of the first edge leaving v (‑1 = none)
Edge *edges;              // pool of edges, size = 2·M (because the graph is undirected)
int edgeCnt;              // next free position in edges[]
```

The adjacency list is the classical “array‑of‑linked‑lists” technique often used in
contest programming.

Additional arrays used by Dijkstra:

```
double *best;            // best[v] = maximal reliability found so far (0 … 1)
int    *parent;          // predecessor of v on the optimal path
bool   *used;            // vertex already extracted from the set
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Algorithm (max‑product Dijkstra)

```
best[s] = 1, all others = 0
parent[.] = -1
used[.] = false

repeat N times
    u = vertex with maximal best[u] among unused vertices
    if best[u] == 0   break   // remaining vertices are unreachable
    used[u] = true

    for every edge (u → v) with probability p
        if best[u] * p > best[v]
               best[v]   = best[u] * p
               parent[v] = u
```

Complexities  

*Time* – `O(N² + M)` (the `O(N²)` comes from the linear scan for the maximal
`best[u]`).  
*Memory* – `O(N + M)`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Restoring the answer

If `best[t] == 0` there is no path – we output “0.000000” and an empty line.  
Otherwise we walk backwards from `t` using `parent[]`, store the vertices in a
temporary array, reverse it and print.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### Full source code (C++17)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>

using namespace std;

// ---------- Edge structure ----------
struct Edge {
    int   to;      // neighbouring vertex
    double p;      // reliability of this edge
    int   next;    // next edge in adjacency list
};

// ---------- Global containers (no vector, no queue) ----------
int    *head;       // size: n+1, initialised with -1
Edge   *edges;      // size: 2*m
int     edgeCnt = 0;

// ---------- Function to add an undirected edge ----------
void addEdge(int u, int v, double prob) {
    // forward direction
    edges[edgeCnt].to   = v;
    edges[edgeCnt].p    = prob;
    edges[edgeCnt].next = head[u];
    head[u] = edgeCnt++;
    // backward direction
    edges[edgeCnt].to   = u;
    edges[edgeCnt].p    = prob;
    edges[edgeCnt].next = head[v];
    head[v] = edgeCnt++;
}

// ---------- Main ----------
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin >> n >> m)) return 0;          // no input

    // allocate static arrays (new[] because we cannot use vector)
    head   = new int   [n + 1];
    edges  = new Edge  [2 * m + 5];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) head[i] = -1;

    // read edges
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        double p;
        cin >> u >> v >> p;
        addEdge(u, v, p);
    }

    int s, t;
    cin >> s >> t;

    // ---------- Dijkstra (max product) ----------
    double *best   = new double[n + 1];
    int    *parent = new int    [n + 1];
    bool   *used   = new bool   [n + 1];

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        best[i]   = 0.0;
        parent[i] = -1;
        used[i]   = false;
    }
    best[s] = 1.0;                     // probability of staying in the start vertex

    for (int iter = 0; iter < n; ++iter) {
        // --- choose unused vertex with maximal probability ---
        int u = -1;
        double curBest = 0.0;
        for (int v = 1; v <= n; ++v)
            if (!used[v] && best[v] > curBest) {
                curBest = best[v];
                u = v;
            }

        if (u == -1 || curBest == 0.0) break;   // nothing reachable any more
        used[u] = true;

        // --- relax all outgoing edges of u ---
        for (int e = head[u]; e != -1; e = edges[e].next) {
            int   v   = edges[e].to;
            double p  = edges[e].p;
            double cand = best[u] * p;
            if (cand > best[v]) {
                best[v]   = cand;
                parent[v] = u;
            }
        }
    }

    // ---------- Output ----------
    cout << fixed << setprecision(6);
    if (best[t] == 0.0) {
        cout << 0.0 << "\n\n";
    } else {
        cout << best[t] << "\n";

        // reconstruct path
        int *path = new int[n];          // at most n vertices
        int len = 0;
        for (int v = t; v != -1; v = parent[v]) {
            path[len++] = v;
            if (v == s) break;
        }
        // print in reverse order
        for (int i = len - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            cout << path[i];
            if (i) cout << ' ';
        }
        cout << "\n";
        delete [] path;
    }

    // ---------- clean ----------
    delete [] head;
    delete [] edges;
    delete [] best;
    delete [] parent;
    delete [] used;

    return 0;
}
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### How to compile and run

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o most_reliable most_reliable.cpp
./most_reliable < input.txt
```

**Input format (the program follows this layout):**

```
N M                     // number of vertices, number of edges
u1 v1 p1
u2 v2 p2
...
uM vM pM                // edges, p – reliability (real number 0..1)
S T                     // start and target vertices
```

**Output:**

```
<max reliability with 6 digits after dot>
v0 v1 v2 ... vk        // vertices of the optimal path (S = v0, T = vk)
```

If no path exists the program prints `0.000000` and an empty second line.

The solution respects the restriction – it never includes `<vector>` nor
`<queue>`; all containers are built using raw pointers and plain arrays.

