The program has to read an **unknown length** sequence of integers that ends with a
dot (`.`).  
From the numbers a singly linked list is built – new elements are appended at
the end.  
If the list is empty the program prints `NULL`.  
Otherwise the first element has to be exchanged with the last one,
the second with the one before the last, …

Exchanging the *nodes* of a singly linked list in this way is exactly the same
as **reversing the whole list** – after a complete reversal the element that was
first becomes last, the second becomes the one before last, etc.
Therefore the required operation can be performed by an ordinary
list‑reversal algorithm that only changes pointers, never the stored values.

Because the statement asks for protection against incorrect input we read the
input character by character, recognise integers and stop as soon as a dot is
met.  Tokens that are not a valid signed integer are simply ignored – the
program never crashes because of malformed data.



