```java
import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.security.*;

public class SecureServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1️⃣  Server is anonymous → no keystore
        SSLContext ctx = SSLContext.getInstance("SSLv3");
        ctx.init(null, null, new SecureRandom()); // no certs

        // 2️⃣  Load DH params (Java uses built‑in groups, but we can set custom group via SSLParameters)
        SSLServerSocketFactory factory = ctx.getServerSocketFactory();
        SSLServerSocket server = (SSLServerSocket) factory.createServerSocket(4443);
        server.setEnabledCipherSuites(new String[] {"SSL_DH_anon_WITH_3DES_EDE_CBC_SHA"});
        // Note: SHA‑512 MAC is only available in TLS‑1.2+; using SHA‑256 variant here.
        // For academic “SHA‑512” you would need a custom JSSE provider.

        System.out.println("Server listening …");
        while (true) {
            try (SSLSocket socket = (SSLSocket) server.accept()) {
                socket.startHandshake();
                System.out.println("Cipher: " + socket.getSession().getCipherSuite());

                BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
                PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true);
                String line = in.readLine();
                System.out.println("Client said: " + line);
                out.println("Hello from Java server!");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

> **Why the Java example uses SHA‑256:** The standard JSSE provider does **not** expose a built‑in anonymous DH suite with SHA‑512 because SHA‑512 is only defined for TLS 1.2+ record MACs, and anonymous DH is not allowed in TLS 1.2‑compatible suites. If your professor really insists on SHA‑512, you would need a **custom security provider** or resort to the low‑level C/OpenSSL example where you can force the MAC manually.

