## 3️⃣  Parser – `JiraExportParser.java`

```java
package com.example.jira;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.core.type.TypeReference;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.DeserializationFeature;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat.xml.XmlMapper;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVFormat;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser;
import org.apache.commons.csv.CSVRecord;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Utility class that knows how to read a Jira export file (JSON, XML or CSV)
 * and turn it into a list of {@link JiraIssue} objects.
 *
 * The public method is simple:
 *
 * <pre>
 * List&lt;JiraIssue&gt; issues = JiraExportParser.parse(new File("my‑issues.json"));
 * </pre>
 *
 * It will throw {@link IllegalArgumentException} if the file type cannot be
 * detected or if the parsing fails.
 */
public final class JiraExportParser {

    private static final Logger LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(JiraExportParser.class);
    private static final ObjectMapper JSON_MAPPER = new ObjectMapper()
            .configure(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES, false);
    private static final XmlMapper XML_MAPPER = new XmlMapper()
            .configure(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES, false);

    private JiraExportParser() {
        // utility – prevent instantiation
    }

    /**
     * Detects the file type from its extension and reads it.
     *
     * @param file The exported Jira file (must exist)
     * @return a mutable list of {@link JiraIssue}
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if the format is unknown or parsing fails
     */
    public static List<JiraIssue> parse(File file) {
        if (!file.isFile() || !file.canRead()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("File not found or unreadable: " + file);
        }

        String lowerName = file.getName().toLowerCase();
        try {
            if (lowerName.endsWith(".json")) {
                LOG.info("Detected JSON export: {}", file);
                return parseJson(file);
            } else if (lowerName.endsWith(".xml")) {
                LOG.info("Detected XML export: {}", file);
                return parseXml(file);
            } else if (lowerName.endsWith(".csv")) {
                LOG.info("Detected CSV export: {}", file);
                return parseCsv(file);
            } else {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported file extension (expected .json, .xml, .csv)");
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Failed to parse Jira export file: " + e.getMessage(), e);
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // JSON
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static List<JiraIssue> parseJson(File file) throws IOException {
        // Jira's JSON export usually contains a top‑level object with a field named "issues"
        // that is an array. The schema may also be a raw array. We support both.

        // First try to read as a wrapper object:
        try (InputStream is = Files.newInputStream(file.toPath())) {
            // Read whole JSON as a tree so we can inspect it.
            var rootNode = JSON_MAPPER.readTree(is);
            if (rootNode.has("issues")) {
                // {"issues":[{...},{...}]}
                return JSON_MAPPER.convertValue(
                        rootNode.get("issues"),
                        new TypeReference<List<JiraIssue>>() {}
                );
            } else if (rootNode.isArray()) {
                // [{"key":"..."} , ...]
                return JSON_MAPPER.convertValue(
                        rootNode,
                        new TypeReference<List<JiraIssue>>() {}
                );
            } else {
                // maybe the issues are nested under "export" or "results"
                // fallback – try to find the first array node.
                var arrayNode = rootNode.findValues("issues").stream()
                        .filter(v -> v.isArray())
                        .findFirst()
                        .orElse(null);
                if (arrayNode != null) {
                    return JSON_MAPPER.convertValue(
                            arrayNode,
                            new TypeReference<List<JiraIssue>>() {}
                    );
                } else {
                    throw new IOException("Could not locate an array of issues in the JSON");
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // XML
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static List<JiraIssue> parseXml(File file) throws IOException {
        // XML exports from Jira come in two flavours:
        //   1) <rss> ... <item>...</item>...</rss>
        //   2) <issues> <issue>...</issue>...</issues>
        // The XmlMapper can be told to map the whole document into a wrapper class.
        // To keep things lightweight we read the document as a tree and then
        // extract the <issue> elements.

        var rootNode = XML_MAPPER.readTree(file);
        List<JiraIssue> result = new ArrayList<>();

        // Look for any repeating node named "issue" (case‑insensitive)
        var issueNodes = rootNode.findValues("issue");
        if (issueNodes.isEmpty()) {
            // Some exports use <item> instead of <issue>
            issueNodes = rootNode.findValues("item");
        }

        for (var node : issueNodes) {
            JiraIssue issue = XML_MAPPER.convertValue(node, JiraIssue.class);
            result.add(issue);
        }

        if (result.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IOException("No <issue> (or <item>) elements found in the XML file");
        }
        return result;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // CSV
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    private static List<JiraIssue> parseCsv(File file) throws IOException {
        // We will try to be tolerant: the first line is assumed to be a header.
        // The column names used by Jira are usually:
        //   Issue key, Summary, Description, Status, Assignee, Reporter, Created, Updated, Priority
        // but they can be customised, so we map columns by header name (case‑insensitive).

        List<JiraIssue> issues = new ArrayList<>();

        try (Reader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(file.toPath(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
             CSVParser parser = new CSVParser(reader,
                     CSVFormat.DEFAULT
                             .withFirstRecordAsHeader()
                             .withIgnoreEmptyLines()
                             .withTrim())) {

            // Normalise header names to lower case for easier matching
            var headerMap = parser.getHeaderMap(); // name -> index
            var normalizedHeaders = new java.util.HashMap<String, String>();
            headerMap.keySet().forEach(h -> normalizedHeaders.put(h.toLowerCase(), h));

            for (CSVRecord record : parser) {
                JiraIssue issue = new JiraIssue();

                // Helper to fetch by a set of possible column names (case‑insensitive)
                java.util.function.BiConsumer<java.util.function.Consumer<String>, String[]> setter = (consumer, candidates) -> {
                    for (String cand : candidates) {
                        String actual = normalizedHeaders.get(cand.toLowerCase());
                        if (actual != null) {
                            consumer.accept(record.get(actual));
                            return;
                        }
                    }
                };

                setter.accept(issue::setKey, new String[]{"Issue key", "Key", "Issue ID"});
                setter.accept(issue::setSummary, new String[]{"Summary", "Title"});
                setter.accept(issue::setDescription, new String[]{"Description"});
                setter.accept(issue::setStatus, new String[]{"Status"});
                setter.accept(issue::setAssignee, new String[]{"Assignee", "Assignee Name"});
                setter.accept(issue::setReporter, new String[]{"Reporter"});
                setter.accept(issue::setCreated, new String[]{"Created", "Created Date"});
                setter.accept(issue::setUpdated, new String[]{"Updated", "Updated Date"});
                setter.accept(issue::setPriority, new String[]{"Priority"});

                issues.add(issue);
            }
        }

        if (issues.isEmpty()) {
            LOG.warn("CSV file parsed, but it contained no data rows. Check the header line.");
        }
        return issues;
    }
}
```

### How the parser works

1. **Format detection** – based on the file extension (`.json`, `.xml`, `.csv`).  
   If you need more sophisticated detection (e.g., sniffing the first few bytes), you can extend the `parse(File)` method.

2. **JSON** – uses Jackson’s data‑binding. It looks for an array called `issues`. If the export is already a raw array it will map that directly.

3. **XML** – parses the whole document into a Jackson `JsonNode` tree, then extracts any node named `issue` **or** `item`. This works for both the traditional “XML Export” and the RSS‑style export produced by “Backup Server”.

4. **CSV** – Apache Commons CSV reads the header row and then maps columns by *name* (case‑insensitive). Missing columns are simply left `null`.

---

## 4️⃣  Demo / Entrypoint – `Main.java`

```java
package com.example.jira;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.io.File;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Simple demo that shows how to use {@link JiraExportParser}.
 *
 * Run it from the command line:
 *
 * <pre>
