## 1.2️⃣ TCP Socket Programming (Blocking I/O)

```java
// EchoServer – one client per thread (classic demo)
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;

public class EchoServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        int port = 7777;
        try (ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(port)) {
            System.out.println("Echo server listening on " + port);
            while (true) {
                Socket client = server.accept();                 // block until a client connects
                new Thread(() -> handle(client)).start();        // each client gets its own thread
            }
        }
    }

    private static void handle(Socket socket) {
        try (BufferedReader in  = new BufferedReader(
                 new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
             PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(socket.getOutputStream(), true)) {

            String line;
            while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println("Received: " + line);
                out.println("Echo: " + line);   // echo back
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

**Client:**

```java
try (Socket sock = new Socket("localhost", 7777);
     BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
             new InputStreamReader(sock.getInputStream()));
     PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(sock.getOutputStream(), true);
     BufferedReader console = new BufferedReader(
             new InputStreamReader(System.in))) {

    String txt;
    while ((txt = console.readLine()) != null) {
        out.println(txt);              // send to server
        System.out.println(in.readLine()); // read echo
    }
}
```

> **Tip:** For production servers, replace the “one thread per client” model with NIO or a thread‑pool executor to avoid **O(N)** thread explosion.

