## 1.3️⃣ UDP Datagram Programming

```java
// Simple UDP Ping‑Pong server
class UdpServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        DatagramSocket socket = new DatagramSocket(9876);
        byte[] buf = new byte[256];
        System.out.println("UDP server listening on 9876");

        while (true) {
            DatagramPacket request = new DatagramPacket(buf, buf.length);
            socket.receive(request);                     // receive packet (blocking)

            String msg = new String(request.getData(), 0,
                                    request.getLength());
            System.out.println("Got: " + msg);

            // Echo back
            DatagramPacket response = new DatagramPacket(
                    request.getData(), request.getLength(),
                    request.getAddress(), request.getPort());
            socket.send(response);
        }
    }
}
```

**Client:**

```java
DatagramSocket socket = new DatagramSocket();
byte[] buf = "ping".getBytes();
DatagramPacket p = new DatagramPacket(buf, buf.length,
        InetAddress.getByName("localhost"), 9876);
socket.send(p);

// receive reply
DatagramPacket reply = new DatagramPacket(new byte[256], 256);
socket.receive(reply);
System.out.println("Reply: " + new String(reply.getData(), 0, reply.getLength()));
socket.close();
```

*UDP is stateless, so you must handle packet loss, ordering, duplication yourself – typically via application‑level ACKs or sequence numbers.*

