## 2️⃣ Web Services in Java – The Big Picture

Web services let **different systems** (often written in different languages) talk to each other over HTTP. In the Java world you have two main families:

| Family | Protocol | Specification | Primary API / Framework | Typical Use‑Case |
|--------|----------|----------------|--------------------------|------------------|
| **SOAP** | XML over HTTP (or JMS) | WS‑* (WSDL, WS‑Security, WS‑Addressing, etc.) | JAX‑WS (`javax.xml.ws`), Apache CXF, Metro (GlassFish) | Enterprise B2B, legacy contracts, formal contracts & WS‑Security |
| **REST** | Anything over HTTP (JSON, XML, binary) | No formal spec (just HTTP semantics) | JAX‑RS (`javax.ws.rs`), Jersey, RestEasy, Spring MVC/Boot, Micronaut, Quarkus, etc. | Public APIs, mobile/back‑end, micro‑services, lightweight integration |

> **Key Idea:** SOAP gives you a *strict contract* (WSDL) and built‑in WS‑* features (security, reliable messaging). REST favors *resource‑oriented design* and lightweight JSON (or even protobuf) payloads.

Below we’ll walk through **both** routes, from *building a service* to *consuming it*.

---

## 3️⃣ SOAP Web Services – JAX‑WS (Standard, easy to get started)

### 3.1️⃣ Service Interface & Data Model (Top‑Down vs Bottom‑Up)

#### **Bottom‑Up (code‑first) – start with POJOs**

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/soap/Calculator.java
import jakarta.jws.WebMethod;
import jakarta.jws.WebService;

@WebService(targetNamespace = "http://example.com/soap")
public interface Calculator {
    @WebMethod
    int add(int a, int b);

    @WebMethod
    int subtract(int a, int b);
}
```

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/soap/CalculatorImpl.java
import jakarta.jws.WebService;

@WebService(endpointInterface = "com.example.soap.Calculator",
            serviceName = "CalculatorService")
public class CalculatorImpl implements Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b)   { return a + b; }
    public int subtract(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
}
```

#### **Top‑Down (contract‑first) – start from WSDL**

```bash
wsimport -keep -p com.example.soap http://localhost:8080/ws/calculator?wsdl
```

*`wsimport` reads a WSDL and generates the service interface and JAXB classes for you.*

