Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run Java utility** that drives GraalVM’s `native‑image` tool to turn a normal JAR (or a set of class files) into a **Windows .exe** (or a native binary on any OS GraalVM runs on).  
The code is deliberately kept simple – it only uses the JDK (`ProcessBuilder`) – so you can embed it in any build step, CI job, or a larger Java application that needs to produce native executables on‑the‑fly.

---

## 1️⃣  What you need before you run the code  

| Requirement | How to satisfy it |
|-------------|-------------------|
| **GraalVM installation** (including the `native-image` tool) | Download a GraalVM JDK from <https://github.com/graalvm/graalvm-ce-builds/releases>. Unzip it and set `GRAALVM_HOME` to the root folder. |
| **`native-image` component installed** | Run `"$GRAALVM_HOME/bin/gu" install native-image` (or `gu.cmd` on Windows). |
| **Your app packaged as a JAR** (or a directory with compiled classes) | Example: `my‑app.jar` that contains a `public static void main(String[] args)`. |
| **Optional: reflection / resource configuration** | Run the app once with the `native-image-agent` to generate `reflect-config.json`, `resource-config.json`, etc., and feed those files back to the builder (see the “advanced options” section further down). |

Make sure the GraalVM `bin` folder (`%GRAALVM_HOME%\bin`) is **on the `PATH`** (or give the absolute path to `native-image.exe` in the code).

---

## 2️⃣  The Java utility – `GraalVmNativeImageBuilder.java`

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;

public class GraalVmNativeImageBuilder {

    /** Main entry point – edit the constants to match your project. */
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        // -------------------- USER‑CONFIGURABLE SECTION --------------------
        // 1️⃣  Path to the JAR (or the directory that contains compiled classes)
        final Path jarOrClasses = Paths.get("target", "my-app.jar"); // <-- change if needed

        // 2️⃣  Desired name of the generated executable (without extension on *nix)
        final String exeBaseName = "my-app";

        // 3️⃣  Extra native-image options you normally would pass on command line:
        //     * add reflection / resource config files
        //     * enable URL protocols, disable fallback, etc.
        final List<String> extraOptions = List.of(
                // Example: enable HTTP/HTTPS (required for many libraries)
                "-H:EnableURLProtocols=http,https",
                // Example: no fallback image (fail fast if native image cannot be built)
                "--no-fallback",
                // Example: pass a config file generated by native-image-agent
                // "-H:ReflectionConfigurationFiles=reflect-config.json",
                // "-H:ResourceConfigurationFiles=resource-config.json"
                // Add or remove options as you like
        );

        // 4️⃣  Working directory where the exe will be written (default = cwd)
        final Path workingDirectory = Paths.get("").toAbsolutePath(); // current dir

        // --------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Build and run the native-image command
        int exitCode = runNativeImage(jarOrClasses, exeBaseName, extraOptions, workingDirectory);

        if (exitCode == 0) {
            System.out.println("\n✅  Native executable built successfully → "
                    + workingDirectory.resolve(exeBaseName + exeExtension()));
        } else {
            System.err.println("\n❌  native-image failed with exit code " + exitCode);
            System.exit(exitCode);
        }
    }

    /** Calls GraalVM's native-image tool with the supplied arguments. */
    private static int runNativeImage(Path jarOrClasses,
                                      String exeBaseName,
                                      List<String> extraOptions,
                                      Path workingDirectory) throws IOException, InterruptedException {

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Locate GraalVM – we read GRAALVM_HOME (preferred) or fallback to java.home
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        String graalHome = System.getenv("GRAALVM_HOME");
        if (graalHome == null || graalHome.isBlank()) {
            // java.home points to the JDK that's currently running; assume it's GraalVM
            graalHome = System.getProperty("java.home");
            System.out.println("⚠ GRAALVM_HOME not set – falling back to java.home = " + graalHome);
        }

        Path nativeImageExecutable = Paths.get(graalHome, "bin",
                isWindows() ? "native-image.cmd" : "native-image");

        if (!Files.isExecutable(nativeImageExecutable)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("native-image executable not found at " + nativeImageExecutable);
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣  Assemble the command line for native-image
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        List<String> cmd = new ArrayList<>();

        // On Windows we need to launch through cmd.exe /c so that *.cmd files work.
        if (isWindows()) {
            cmd.add("cmd.exe");
            cmd.add("/c");
        }
        cmd.add(nativeImageExecutable.toString());

        // Choose the mode: -jar or -cp classDir
        if (jarOrClasses.toString().endsWith(".jar")) {
            cmd.add("-jar");
            cmd.add(jarOrClasses.toString());
        } else {
            // Classpath mode – you can also use -cp <dirOrJarList>
            cmd.add("-cp");
            cmd.add(jarOrClasses.toString());
        }

        // Output name – GraalVM appends .exe on Windows automatically
        cmd.add("-H:Name=" + exeBaseName);

        // Append all extra options the user specified
        cmd.addAll(extraOptions);

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣  Launch the process
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        System.out.println("\n🔧  Running native-image with command:");
        System.out.println(String.join(" ", cmd));

        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(cmd);
        pb.directory(workingDirectory.toFile());
        // Merge stdout+stderr so we can stream a single console view
        pb.redirectErrorStream(true);
        // IMPORTANT: inherit the current environment (so GRAALVM_HOME stays available)
        Map<String, String> env = pb.environment();
        env.putIfAbsent("GRAALVM_HOME", graalHome); // make sure it is visible

        Process proc = pb.start();

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4️⃣  Stream the process output line‑by‑line to the console
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
                new InputStreamReader(proc.getInputStream()))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5️⃣  Wait for completion and return the exit code
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        return proc.waitFor();
    }

    /** Tiny helper to decide if we are on Windows */
    private static boolean isWindows() {
        return System.getProperty("os.name").toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT).contains("win");
    }

    /** Returns the OS‑specific executable extension (".exe" on Windows, "" otherwise). */
    private static String exeExtension() {
        return isWindows() ? ".exe" : "";
    }
}
```

### How to use the utility

1. **Create a simple Maven/Gradle project** (or just a folder with the above class).  
2. **Add the Java source** (e.g. `src/main/java/GraalVmNativeImageBuilder.java`).  
3. **Build your application JAR** (e.g. `mvn package` → `target/my-app.jar`).  
4. **Edit the constants** in the `main` method to point to the JAR, the desired exe name, and any extra native‑image flags you need.  
5. **Run the builder**:  

```bash
# Ensure GRAALVM_HOME points at a GraalVM distribution that has native-image installed
export GRAALVM_HOME=/path/to/graalvm-ce-java17-23.1.2
# (or set it in Windows environment variables)

# Compile the helper class (you can use any build tool, here plain javac):
javac -d out src/main/java/GraalVmNativeImageBuilder.java

# Run it
java -cp out GraalVmNativeImageBuilder
```

After a few seconds (or minutes for larger apps) you will see a `my-app.exe` (or `my-app` on Linux/macOS) in the working directory.

---

## 3️⃣  Advanced usage – feeding GraalVM configuration files  

When your Java code uses reflection, dynamic proxies, or loads resources from the classpath, GraalVM needs **JSON configuration files** so it knows which classes/fields/resources to keep in the native image.

### 3.1 Generate the config with the *native‑image‑agent*

```bash
# Run your jar with the agent to record reflective calls, resources, etc.
$GRAALVM_HOME/bin/java -agentlib:native-image-agent=config-output-dir=graal-config -jar target/my-app.jar <args>
```

After a typical execution, a directory `graal-config` will contain files like:

```
reflect-config.json
resource-config.json
serialization-config.json
```

### 3.2 Feed those files into the builder

Add them to the `extraOptions` list in the Java utility:

```java
final List<String> extraOptions = List.of(
    "-H:EnableURLProtocols=http,https",
    "--no-fallback",
    "-H:ReflectionConfigurationFiles=graal-config/reflect-config.json",
    "-H:ResourceConfigurationFiles=graal-config/resource-config.json",
    "-H:SerializationConfigurationFiles=graal-config/serialization-config.json"
);
```

(You can also use the wildcard `-H:ConfigurationFileDirectories=graal-config` – GraalVM will load all `*.json` files there.)

### 3.3 Common useful flags

| Flag | What it does | Typical use |
|------|--------------|-------------|
| `--no-fallback` | Fail if a native image cannot be built (no “slow‑java” fallback). | CI pipelines / production builds. |
| `--initialize-at-build-time=...` | Force specific classes to be initialized during native‑image generation. | Improves startup time, but beware of side‑effects. |
| `--initialize-at-run-time=...` | Opposite – keep class initialization for runtime. | For classes that depend on environment variables, random seeds, etc. |
| `-H:Class=your.main.Class` (instead of `-jar`) | Builds from a classpath rather than a JAR; handy for multi‑module builds. | When you have many dependency JARs you don't want to repack. |
| `-H:IncludeResources=.*\\.properties|.*\\.txt` | Explicitly keep resource files that match the regex. | When the agent missed something. |
| `-J-Xmx4g` | Allocate more RAM to the native-image process (common on large projects). | Add as a prefixed argument (e.g. `"-J-Xmx4g"`). |

---

## 4️⃣  Why you **cannot** compile an exe *inside* the same JVM  

`native-image` is a **stand‑alone native compiler** that performs ahead‑of‑time (AOT) compilation of Java bytecode to a platform‑specific binary.  
The process it runs is completely separate from the JVM you are currently executing in, because:

1. The compiler needs to **load class metadata** in a *different* class‑loading environment (the GraalVM *host* VM).  
2. It performs **link‑time static analysis** and **LLVM code generation**, which is not exposed through a regular Java API.  
3. The output binary **does not contain a Java bytecode interpreter** – it is *the* runtime.

Therefore the only practical way to automate it from Java is to **invoke the `native-image` command line** (as shown above) or to use a build‑tool plugin (Maven/Gradle) that does the same under the hood.

---

## 5️⃣  Integration with Maven / Gradle (optional, for completeness)

If you prefer the build‑tool approach, you can still drive it programmatically from Java by executing the same command, but it’s often simpler to let the plugin do the heavy lifting.

### Maven
```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.graalvm.nativeimage</groupId>
    <artifactId>native-image-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>${graalvm.version}</version>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <id>build-native</id>
            <goals><goal>native-image</goal></goals>
            <phase>package</phase>
            <configuration>
                <executableName>my-app</executableName>
                <debug>false</debug>
                <enableUrlProtocols>http,https</enableUrlProtocols>
                <noFallback>true</noFallback>
                <buildArgs>
                    <buildArg>-H:ReflectionConfigurationFiles=graal-config/reflect-config.json</buildArg>
                    <buildArg>-H:ResourceConfigurationFiles=graal-config/resource-config.json</buildArg>
                </buildArgs>
            </configuration>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

### Gradle (Groovy DSL)

```groovy
plugins {
    id 'org.graalvm.nativeimage' version "${graalvmVersion}"
}

nativeImage {
    // The jar built by the `jar` task will be used
    executableName = 'my-app'
    // Common arguments
    args '--no-fallback',
         '-H:EnableURLProtocols=http,https',
         '-H:ReflectionConfigurationFiles=graal-config/reflect-config.json',
         '-H:ResourceConfigurationFiles=graal-config/resource-config.json'
}
```

Both plugins ultimately **run the same `native-image` binary**, so the Java utility presented above works exactly the same way, just without the Maven/Gradle scaffolding.

---

## 6️⃣  TL;DR – Quick copy‑paste checklist

```text
1️⃣  Install GraalVM and native-image (gu install native-image)
2️⃣  Set GRAALVM_HOME (or ensure java.home points to GraalVM)
3️⃣  Build your app JAR (e.g. target/app.jar)
4️⃣  (Optional) Run native-image-agent once to collect config files
5️⃣  Adapt GraalVmNativeImageBuilder.java (paths, exe name, extra flags)
6️⃣  Run the builder → you get my-app.exe (or native binary)
```

That’s it! 🎉  
With the helper above you can **automatically generate native Windows executables (or Linux/macOS binaries) from any Java project** whenever you need, fully controllable from Java code itself. Happy native‑image building!

