## 11️⃣ Production Checklist  

| ✅ Item | Why |
|--------|-----|
| **Certificate renewal monitoring** | Certificates expire after 2 years – set up automated alerts. |
| **Secure storage of the PKCS#12 file** | Keep it out of the repo, restrict file permissions, use HSM if possible. |
| **TLS 1.2+ enforcement** | The RN‑FTP service disables older protocols; configure `SSLContext` accordingly. |
| **Fail‑over / retry** | Network glitches happen – implement a queue (Kafka, RabbitMQ, DB table) with exponential back‑off. |
| **Logging** | Log request/response hashes (not raw sensitive data) for audit. |
| **Monitoring** | Health‑check endpoint that reports “last successful JIR timestamp”. |
| **Disaster recovery** | Have a backup of the JIR database in a separate location. |
| **Compliance test** | Run the official **Fina Test‑Tool** (downloadable from the portal) before the go‑live date. |
| **Time‑zone handling** | All timestamps must include the local offset (`+01:00` or `+02:00` for DST). |

---

## 12️⃣ TL;DR – “Cheat Sheet”  

```text
1️⃣ Obtain PKCS#12 cert + OIB
2️⃣ Load PrivateKey & Cert (KeystoreUtil)
3️⃣ Build receipt POJO (XML via JAXB or JSON via Jackson)
4️⃣ Marshal → String (XML or JSON)
5️⃣ Sign:
   • XML → Apache Santuario (enveloped RSA‑SHA256)
   • JSON → SHA256withRSA over UTF‑8 (no signature field)
6️⃣ POST to https://www.apis-it.hr:8443/rn-ftp/v1/
   • Content‑Type: application/xml  OR  application/json
7️⃣ Parse response, extract `JIR`
8️⃣ Persist receipt + JIR, log, retry on failures
9️⃣ Verify signatures (optional, but good practice)
10️⃣ Keep cert renewed, monitor, audit.
```

---

## 13️⃣ Further Resources  

| Resource | Link |
|----------|------|
| **Official RN‑FTP Spec (PDF)** | <https://www.fina.hr/documents/10180/124634/RN-FTP+Spec+v2.0.pdf> |
| **JSON Schema** | <https://www.fina.hr/documents/10180/124634/RN-FTP+JSON+schema.zip> |
| **BouncyCastle Docs** | <https://www.bouncycastle.org/java.html> |
| **Apache Santuario (XML‑DSig)** | <https://santuario.apache.org/> |
| **Open‑source Java SDK (croatian‑fiskalisation)** | <https://github.com/bseni/croatian-fiskalisation> (good reference implementation) |
| **Fina Test‑Tool** | Available on the portal after you register your cert – used for pre‑production validation. |
| **Sample Docker image** (for CI testing) | `docker run --rm -e PKCS12_PASSWORD=... -v $PWD/cert.p12:/cert.p12 mycompany/fiskal-test:latest` |

---

### Bottom line  

Implementing Croatian fiscalisation in **Java 11** is straightforward once you have the certificate and understand the three pillars:

1. **Correct payload** (XML/JSON adhering to the official schema)  
2. **Cryptographic signature** (RSA‑SHA256 on the whole document)  
3. **Reliable HTTPS transport** + proper **response handling** (JIR persistence & retries)

The code snippets above give you a **complete, compile‑ready baseline**. Plug them into your POS backend, add the persistence layer you already use (JPA, Spring‑Data, etc.), and you’ll be compliant with the law. Good luck, and don’t forget to run the official Fina test‑tool before the go‑live date!

