## 3. NetworkConnection.java – the encapsulated TCP client

```java
package com.example.tcpclient;

import android.util.Log;

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.OutputStream;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.net.Socket;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

/**
 * A tiny wrapper around a TCP socket that runs all I/O on a single
 * background thread (via an ExecutorService).  It exposes a tiny
 * callback interface so the UI layer can react to connection events
 * without ever touching the network thread.
 *
 * <p>Typical usage:
 *
 * <pre>
 *   network = new NetworkConnection(host, port, listener);
 *   network.start();                // opens the socket
 *   network.sendMessage("Hello");   // send data
 *   // …
 *   network.stop();                 // closes the socket
 * </pre>
 */
public final class NetworkConnection {

    /** Tag for logcat */
    private static final String TAG = "NetworkConnection";

    /** Interface the UI can implement to receive callbacks */
    public interface Listener {
        /** Called once a TCP connection has been successfully established */
        void onConnected();

        /** Called when the remote side closes the socket or we encounter a fatal error */
        void onDisconnected(String reason);

        /** Called for every line of UTF‑8 text received from the server */
        void onMessageReceived(String line);
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Configuration (immutable after construction)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    private final String host;
    private final int port;
    private final Listener listener;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Runtime objects
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
    private Socket socket;                // guarded by synchronized(this)
    private PrintWriter outWriter;        // wrapped OutputStream
    private BufferedReader inReader;      // wrapped InputStream

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Construction & public API
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    public NetworkConnection(String host, int port, Listener listener) {
        if (host == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("host == null");
        if (listener == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("listener == null");
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
        this.listener = listener;
    }

    /** Starts the connection on a background thread. */
    public void start() {
        executor.execute(this::runConnect);
    }

    /** Sends a UTF‑8 line to the remote endpoint.  Returns false if not connected. */
    public boolean sendMessage(String msg) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (outWriter == null) {
                Log.w(TAG, "sendMessage() called while not connected");
                return false;
            }
            outWriter.println(msg);
            outWriter.flush();
            return true;
        }
    }

    /** Gracefully shuts down the socket and the worker thread. */
    public void stop() {
        executor.execute(this::runDisconnect);
        executor.shutdownNow(); // no more tasks will be accepted
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // Private helpers – everything that touches the socket lives here
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    private void runConnect() {
        try {
            Log.i(TAG, "Connecting to " + host + ":" + port);
            Socket s = new Socket();
            // 10‑second connect timeout, 30‑second read timeout
            s.connect(new InetSocketAddress(host, port), 10_000);
            s.setSoTimeout(30_000);
            InputStream is = s.getInputStream();
            OutputStream os = s.getOutputStream();

            synchronized (this) {
                socket = s;
                outWriter = new PrintWriter(os, true); // auto‑flush on newline
                inReader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(is, "UTF-8"));
            }

            // Notify UI that we are connected
            listener.onConnected();

            // ----------------- read loop -----------------
            String line;
            while ((line = inReader.readLine()) != null) {
                // Each line is delivered on the UI thread via the listener
                listener.onMessageReceived(line);
            }

            // If we break out of the loop, the remote side closed the socket
            runDisconnect("Remote closed connection");

        } catch (IOException e) {
            runDisconnect("IO error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    /** Close socket and notify UI.  This method is always called from the worker thread. */
    private void runDisconnect(String reason) {
        Log.i(TAG, "Disconnecting: " + reason);
        synchronized (this) {
            // Close in the reverse order of creation – ignore any secondary exceptions
            try { if (inReader != null) inReader.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            try { if (outWriter != null) outWriter.close(); } catch (Exception ignored) {}
            try { if (socket != null && !socket.isClosed()) socket.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
            socket = null;
            outWriter = null;
            inReader = null;
        }

        // Notify the UI (listener) – UI must make sure it runs on the main thread (see MainActivity)
        listener.onDisconnected(reason);
    }
}
```

### What the class does

| Step | Action | Thread |
|------|--------|--------|
| **Construction** | Stores host/port + listener (immutable) | UI thread |
| **`start()`** | Submits `runConnect()` to a single‑threaded `ExecutorService` | Background thread |
| **`runConnect()`** | Opens `Socket`, creates `PrintWriter` & `BufferedReader`, then enters a *blocking* `readLine()` loop. Every line received is forwarded to `listener.onMessageReceived()`. | Background thread |
| **`sendMessage()`** | Synchronized write to the socket (still from whatever thread you call it on – usually UI). | UI thread (or any other) |
| **`stop()`** | Posts `runDisconnect()` to the same executor (ensuring the socket is closed from the same thread that created it). | Background thread |
| **Callbacks** | `listener` methods are invoked **on the same background thread**. The UI (`MainActivity`) marshals them onto the main thread with a `Handler`. | Background → UI thread (via `Handler`) |

Feel free to replace the **text‑line protocol** with a binary protocol (use `DataInputStream/DataOutputStream`) – the pattern stays the same.

