## 4️⃣  Interpreting the Numbers  

| #Threads | Matrix‑Mul (sec) | Speed‑up | Matrix‑Mul Efficiency |
|----------|-----------------|----------|------------------------|
| 1 | 23.45 | 1.00 | 100 % |
| 2 | 12.01 | 1.95 | 97 % |
| 4 | 6.30  | 3.73 | 93 % |
| 6 | 4.28  | 5.48 | 91 % |
| 8 | 3.35  | 6.99 | 87 % |
|10 | 3.10  | 7.57 | 76 % |
|12 | 2.96  | 7.92 | 66 % |
|14 | 2.92  | 8.03 | 57 % |
|16 | 2.90  | 8.09 | 50 % |

(These numbers are **illustrative** – your actual measurements will differ depending on the exact CPU model, cache size, and memory bandwidth of the lab machines.)

**Why does speed‑up taper off?**  

* The computation is **memory‑bandwidth bound** once you start saturating the DRAM channel.  
* Linux scheduler and the hyper‑threading cores (if present) divide the physical core resources, so adding more logical threads than physical cores yields diminishing returns.  

In contrast, the Monte‑Carlo π benchmark typically shows:

| #Threads | π‑Est (sec) | Speed‑up |
|----------|------------|----------|
| 1 | 2.87 | 1.00 |
| 2 | 1.45 | 1.98 |
| 4 | 0.73 | 3.94 |
| 8 | 0.38 | 7.53 |
|16 | 0.20 | 14.4 |

Here the algorithm is **compute‑bound** and the scaling is almost linear up to 16 threads because each thread works on its private RNG state and the only shared data is the final reduction (a single `long` add).

