## 1️⃣ Why `java.time` (the *new* API) instead of `java.util.Date`?

| Feature | `java.util.Date` / `Calendar` | `java.time` (LocalDate, LocalDateTime, ZonedDateTime …) |
|---------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| **Immutability** | Mutable → bugs when you share instances | Immutable → thread‑safe, no accidental changes |
| **Clarity** | `Date` represents a *timestamp* (date+time+zone) but often used just for a date | Separate types for each concept (`LocalDate` = date‑only, `LocalTime` = time‑only, `ZonedDateTime` = date+time+zone) |
| **Formatting / Parsing** | `SimpleDateFormat` (not thread‑safe) | `DateTimeFormatter` (thread‑safe, reusable) |
| **Time‑zone handling** | `TimeZone`/`Calendar` – cumbersome | Built‑in `ZoneId`, `ZonedDateTime` |
| **API design** | Lots of “setter” methods, 0‑based months, confusing offsets | Fluent, expressive, method names make intent obvious |
| **Java version** | Exists forever, but **deprecated** in favor of `java.time` | Standard since Java 8 (still the recommended way) |

> **Bottom line:** only use the old `Date`/`Calendar` if you are forced to (e.g., third‑party API that still expects them). For everything else, go with `java.time`.

---

## 2️⃣ Common patterns you’ll run into

### a) Storing a *date* without a time‑of‑day → `LocalDate`

```java
LocalDate birthday = LocalDate.of(1990, Month.AUGUST, 15); // 1990‑08‑15
```

### b) Storing a *date‑and‑time* without a time‑zone → `LocalDateTime`

```java
LocalDateTime meeting = LocalDateTime.of(2024, 7, 1, 14, 30); // 2024‑07‑01T14:30
```

### c) Storing a *date‑and‑time* **with** a zone → `ZonedDateTime`

```java
ZoneId tokyo = ZoneId.of("Asia/Tokyo");
ZonedDateTime tokyoNow = ZonedDateTime.now(tokyo);
```

### d) Converting between the old and new APIs (only if you have to)

```java
// From java.util.Date → java.time.Instant → LocalDate (in a specific zone)
Date oldDate = new Date();                       // now()
Instant instant = oldDate.toInstant();           // point on the timeline
ZoneId zone = ZoneId.systemDefault();            // or any zone you like
LocalDate localDate = instant.atZone(zone).toLocalDate();
```

```java
// From java.time → java.util.Date
LocalDate ld = LocalDate.of(2024, 6, 24);
Instant i = ld.atStartOfDay(zone).toInstant();
Date backToOld = Date.from(i);
```

---

## 3️⃣ Tips & Gotchas

| Situation | What to watch out for |
|-----------|-----------------------|
| **Parsing user input** | Use a `DateTimeFormatter` that matches the exact pattern. `LocalDate.parse(text)` only works for ISO‑8601 (`yyyy‑MM‑dd`). |
| **Time‑zone differences** | `LocalDate` has **no** zone. When you convert `LocalDate` → `Instant` you must decide which zone the “start of day” belongs to. |
| **Database mapping** (JPA/Hibernate) | `java.time` classes are fully supported in modern JPA providers. Use `@Column` + `java.time.LocalDate` → SQL `DATE`. |
| **Serialisation (JSON, XML, etc.)** | Most libraries (Jackson, Gson, JAXB) understand `java.time` out‑of‑the‑box or with a small module (`jackson-datatype-jsr310`). |
| **Equality & hashing** | Because they’re immutable, you can safely use them as keys in `HashMap` or elements of `Set`. |

---

## 4️⃣ Full‑blown Example – A “Task” class with a due‑date

```java
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.util.Objects;

/**
 * Simple domain object representing a task that has an optional due date.
 */
public class Task {

    private final String id;          // immutable identifier
    private String description;
    private LocalDate dueDate;        // can be null → no due date

    // ---------- Constructors ----------
    public Task(String id, String description) {
        this.id = Objects.requireNonNull(id);
        this.description = Objects.requireNonNull(description);
    }

    public Task(String id, String description, LocalDate dueDate) {
        this(id, description);
        this.dueDate = dueDate; // may be null – means "no deadline"
    }

    // ---------- Getters ----------
    public String getId() { return id; }

    public String getDescription() { return description; }

    public LocalDate getDueDate() { return dueDate; }

    // ---------- Setters ----------
    public void setDescription(String description) {
        this.description = Objects.requireNonNull(description);
    }

    /** Set a due‑date from a LocalDate instance */
    public void setDueDate(LocalDate dueDate) {
        this.dueDate = dueDate; // null is allowed → remove the deadline
    }

    /** Set a due‑date from a string and a formatter */
    public void setDueDate(String text, DateTimeFormatter formatter) {
        this.dueDate = (text == null || text.isBlank())
                ? null
                : LocalDate.parse(text, formatter);
    }

    // ---------- Helper ----------
    /** Returns a nicely formatted due‑date, or “no deadline” */
    public String getDueDateFormatted(DateTimeFormatter fmt) {
        return (dueDate == null) ? "no deadline" : dueDate.format(fmt);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Task{id='" + id + '\'' +
               ", description='" + description + '\'' +
               ", dueDate=" + (dueDate != null ? dueDate : "none") +
               '}';
    }
}
```

