## 4. Indexing – walk all files once  

The index is a **concurrent hash map** (`Map<String, FileMeta>`), keyed by the absolute path.
We keep it thread‑safe because the `WatchService` will modify it while the UI/CLI searches it.

```java
package dev.example.fileindex;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.nio.file.attribute.BasicFileAttributes;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

/**
 * Walks a file tree (or several roots) and builds a map.
 * The operation is *blocking* – it returns only when the whole tree has been visited.
 *
 * If you want to speed things up you can run each root on its own thread;
 * the implementation below already uses a thread‑safe map,
 * so you can call {@link #indexDrive(File)} concurrently.
 */
public final class IndexBuilder {

    /** The live in‑memory index. */
    private final Map<String, FileMeta> index = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    /** Returns a reference to the map – callers must not replace it, but they may read/write. */
    public Map<String, FileMeta> getIndex() {
        return index;
    }

    /**
     * Indexes *all* drives detected on the system.
     * This method blocks until every drive has been scanned.
     */
    public void indexAllDrives() throws IOException {
        for (File drive : DriveDetector.listRootDrives()) {
            indexDrive(drive);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Indexes one drive (or any arbitrary directory) using {@link Files#walkFileTree}.
     */
    public void indexDrive(File root) throws IOException {
        Path start = root.toPath();
        Files.walkFileTree(start, new SimpleFileVisitor<>() {
            @Override
            public FileVisitResult visitFile(Path file, BasicFileAttributes attrs) {
                if (!attrs.isDirectory()) {
                    String absolutePath = file.toAbsolutePath().toString();
                    index.put(absolutePath,
                            new FileMeta(absolutePath, attrs.size(), attrs.lastModifiedTime().toMillis()));
                }
                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }

            // If we can't read a directory we just skip it – otherwise the whole scan would abort.
            @Override
            public FileVisitResult visitFileFailed(Path file, IOException exc) {
                System.err.println("Unable to access: " + file + " (" + exc.getMessage() + ")");
                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }
        });
    }
}
```

