## 8. Putting everything together – a tiny CLI demo  

```java
package dev.example.fileindex;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

/**
 * Simple command‑line program that shows how the building blocks interact.
 *
 * 1️⃣ Loads (or creates) the persisted index.  
 * 2️⃣ Starts the file‑system watcher in the background.  
 * 3️⃣ Provides a tiny REPL:
 *      - `search <query>` – prints up to 20 matches.
 *      - `listdrives` – prints the detected drives.
 *      - `quit` – shuts down cleanly (watcher + persistor).
 */
public final class Main {

    // Change this path if you want the index somewhere else:
    private static final Path INDEX_FILE = Path.of("index.json");

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣ Load or build the index
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        ConcurrentMap<String, FileMeta> index = (ConcurrentMap<String, FileMeta>)
                IndexPersistence.load(INDEX_FILE);

        if (index.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Index file empty – performing full scan…");
            IndexBuilder builder = new IndexBuilder();
            builder.indexAllDrives();
            index.putAll(builder.getIndex());
            IndexPersistence.save(index, INDEX_FILE);
            System.out.println("Initial scan finished – indexed " + index.size() + " files.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Loaded index with " + index.size() + " entries.");
        }

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣ Start the background watcher (keeps the map up‑to‑date)
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        DriveWatcher watcher = new DriveWatcher(index);
        watcher.start();

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3️⃣ Simple REPL for demo / ad‑hoc searches
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        FileMetaSearcher searcher = new FileMetaSearcher(index);
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("\n=== File‑Index REPL ===");
        System.out.println("Commands:  search <text> | listdrives | size | quit");
        while (true) {
            System.out.print("> ");
            String line = scanner.nextLine().trim();
            if (line.isEmpty()) continue;

            String[] parts = line.split("\\s+", 2);
            String cmd = parts[0].toLowerCase();

            switch (cmd) {
                case "search" -> {
                    if (parts.length < 2) {
                        System.out.println("Usage: search <text>");
                        continue;
                    }
                    String query = parts[1];
                    List<FileMeta> hits = searcher.searchByName(query, 20);
                    System.out.println("Found " + hits.size() + " matches (first 20):");
                    for (FileMeta fm : hits) {
                        System.out.println("  " + fm.getPath() + "  [" + fm.getSize() + " B]");
                    }
                }
                case "listdrives" -> {
                    System.out.println("Detected drives:");
                    DriveDetector.listRootDriveNames()
                            .forEach(d -> System.out.println("  " + d));
                }
                case "size" -> System.out.println("Current index size: " + index.size() + " entries.");
                case "quit", "exit" -> {
                    System.out.println("Shutting down …");
                    watcher.stop();
                    // Persist the *current* index (some updates may have happened)
                    IndexPersistence.save(index, INDEX_FILE);
                    System.out.println("Index saved to " + INDEX_FILE);
                    return;
                }
                default -> System.out.println("Unknown command. Available: search, listdrives, size, quit");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

> **How to run**  
> ```bash
> mvn clean package          # or ./gradlew build
> java -jar target/FileIndexing-1.0.0.jar
> ```
>  
> The first run will **scan the whole machine** – on a typical Windows laptop this can take 30 seconds to a few minutes, depending on size. Subsequent starts will be instantaneous because the saved JSON index is loaded.

---

## 9. Things you may want to improve / scale

| Feature | Why it matters | How to add it |
|---------|----------------|---------------|
| **Persist only essential data** (e.g. keep only file name & size, drop `lastModified` if you don’t need it) | Reduces JSON file size & load time. | Edit `FileMeta` to drop fields, adjust `IndexPersistence`. |
| **Use an embedded DB (SQLite / H2)** | Faster random look‑ups, optional indexes on size, date, etc. | Replace `ConcurrentMap` with a DAO that runs simple `SELECT` statements; the watcher just runs `INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE`. |
| **Full‑text search** (wildcards, regex, fuzzy) | More powerful queries. | Plug in Apache Lucene – on each `handleCreate/Modify` call `Document.addField(...)`. |
| **Batch updates** (debounce rapid file‑system events) | Avoid thrashing the index on huge copy‑operations. | The watcher can accumulate events for a short window (e.g., 1 s) then apply them in bulk. |
| **Skip unwanted directories** (e.g., system folders, recycle bin, `node_modules`) | Prevent index bloat and speed up scans. | Before `registerRecursively` add a `Predicate<Path> exclude` check; also ignore in `IndexBuilder`. |
| **Parallel indexing** | Use multi‑core CPUs for large drives. | In `IndexBuilder.indexAllDrives` launch one thread per drive (or split each drive into sub‑trees). |
| **Cross‑platform long‑path handling** | Windows >260‑char paths need `\\?\` prefix. | Use `Path.of("\\\\?\\", absolute)` when you suspect long paths (or use JDK‑17’s built‑in support with `-Djdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize=...`). |
| **Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM** | Ensure index is saved if the program is killed. | Add a shutdown hook: `Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {/*save index*/}));`. |

---

## 10. TL;DR – What the code does

| Step | What happens | Which class |
|------|--------------|------------|
| **Detect drives** | `DriveDetector.listRootDrives()` | `DriveDetector` |
| **Load persisted index** | `IndexPersistence.load(...)` | `IndexPersistence` |
| **If empty → full scan** | `IndexBuilder.indexAllDrives()` → writes map | `IndexBuilder` |
| **Save index to file** | `IndexPersistence.save(...)` | `IndexPersistence` |
| **Start monitoring** | `DriveWatcher.start()` registers every directory with a `WatchService` and runs a background loop | `DriveWatcher` |
| **Search** | `FileMetaSearcher.searchByName(...)` runs a case‑insensitive substring match over the map | `FileMetaSearcher` |
| **Graceful quit** | `DriveWatcher.stop()` + final `IndexPersistence.save(...)` | `Main` (REPL) |

The whole solution is **self‑contained**, requires **no external services**, works on **Windows, macOS, Linux**, and can be compiled with the standard JDK. Feel free to copy‑paste the classes into your own project, replace the JSON persistence with a DB, or drop the REPL and embed the API in a GUI / web service – the core parts (`IndexBuilder`, `DriveWatcher`, `FileMetaSearcher`) stay unchanged. Happy indexing!

