```java
import com.sun.net.httpserver.*;

import javax.net.ssl.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.security.*;
import java.security.cert.CertificateException;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class SimpleHttpsServer {

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    //  Configuration – change these if you like
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    private static final int    PORT          = 8443;                     // HTTPS port
    private static final String KEYSTORE_PATH = "server.keystore";       // relative to working dir
    private static final String KEYSTORE_PWD  = "changeit";              // keystore password
    private static final String KEY_PWD       = "changeit";              // key password (same as above)
    // --------------------------------------------------------------

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1️⃣  Create an SSLContext that uses our keystore
        SSLContext sslContext = createSSLContext();

        // 2️⃣  Create the HttpsServer instance
        HttpsServer httpsServer = HttpsServer.create(new InetSocketAddress(PORT), 0);
        httpsServer.setHttpsConfigurator(new HttpsConfigurator(sslContext) {
            @Override
            public void configure(HttpsParameters params) {
                // Initialise the SSL parameters
                SSLParameters sslParams = getSSLContext().getDefaultSSLParameters();
                params.setSSLParameters(sslParams);
            }
        });

        // 3️⃣  Add a simple handler (GET /hello -> "Hello, HTTPS!")
        httpsServer.createContext("/hello", new HelloHandler());

        // 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a handler for everything else
        httpsServer.createContext("/", exchange -> {
            String response = "Welcome to the simple HTTPS server. Try /hello";
            sendResponse(exchange, 200, response);
        });

        // 5️⃣  Use a thread‑pool executor (default is a single thread)
        httpsServer.setExecutor(Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4));

        // 6️⃣  Start it!
        httpsServer.start();
        System.out.println("HTTPS server started on https://localhost:" + PORT);
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    //  Helper methods
    // --------------------------------------------------------------

    /** Build an SSLContext from a PKCS12 keystore. */
    private static SSLContext createSSLContext()
            throws KeyStoreException, IOException, NoSuchAlgorithmException,
                   CertificateException, UnrecoverableKeyException,
                   KeyManagementException {

        // Load the keystore
        char[] ksPassword = KEYSTORE_PWD.toCharArray();
        KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance("PKCS12");
        try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(KEYSTORE_PATH)) {
            ks.load(fis, ksPassword);
        }

        // Set up key manager factory to use our key
        KeyManagerFactory kmf = KeyManagerFactory.getInstance(KeyManagerFactory.getDefaultAlgorithm());
        kmf.init(ks, KEY_PWD.toCharArray());

        // (Optional) Trust manager that trusts everything – useful for demo clients.
        // In production you would use the default TrustManager or a custom one.
        TrustManager[] trustAll = new TrustManager[]{
                new X509TrustManager() {
                    public java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return null; }
                    public void checkClientTrusted(java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] certs, String authType) { }
                    public void checkServerTrusted(java.security.cert.X509Certificate[] certs, String authType) { }
                }
        };

        // Initialise the SSLContext
        SSLContext sslContext = SSLContext.getInstance("TLS");
        sslContext.init(kmf.getKeyManagers(), trustAll, new SecureRandom());
        return sslContext;
    }

    /** Very small helper that writes a string body and closes the exchange. */
    private static void sendResponse(HttpExchange exchange, int statusCode, String body) throws IOException {
        byte[] bytes = body.getBytes("UTF-8");
        exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Content-Type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8");
        exchange.sendResponseHeaders(statusCode, bytes.length);
        try (OutputStream os = exchange.getResponseBody()) {
            os.write(bytes);
        }
    }

    /** A tiny HttpHandler that says “Hello, HTTPS!”. */
    static class HelloHandler implements HttpHandler {
        @Override
        public void handle(HttpExchange exchange) throws IOException {
            if (!"GET".equalsIgnoreCase(exchange.getRequestMethod())) {
                sendResponse(exchange, 405, "Method Not Allowed");
                return;
            }
            String response = "Hello, HTTPS! This is a secure response.";
            sendResponse(exchange, 200, response);
        }
    }
}
```

### How the code works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **SSLContext creation** | Loads the PKCS12 keystore, extracts the private key & certificate, and builds an `SSLContext` that knows how to do TLS hand‑shakes. |
| **HttpsServer** | `HttpsServer.create` binds a TCP socket on the configured port (`8443`). |
| **HttpsConfigurator** | Supplies the `SSLContext` to the server and optionally tweaks SSL parameters (cipher suites, client authentication, …). |
| **Context/Handler** | Registers a path (`/hello`) and a tiny `HttpHandler` that writes a plain‑text response. A catch‑all “/” context is also added for convenience. |
| **Executor** | A small thread‑pool so the server can handle concurrent connections. |
| **Start** | `httpsServer.start()` begins listening. The main thread then exits (the server keeps the JVM alive because the executor threads are non‑daemon). |

---

## 3️⃣  Running the example  

1. **Compile**  

   ```bash
   javac SimpleHttpsServer.java
   ```

2. **Run**  

   ```bash
   java SimpleHttpsServer
   ```

   You should see  

   ```
   HTTPS server started on https://localhost:8443
   ```

3. **Test**  

   Open a browser (or `curl`) and go to  

   ```
   https://localhost:8443/hello
   ```

   Because the certificate is self‑signed, the browser will warn you. Add a security exception (or import the keystore into the browser) and you’ll see the plain‑text response.

   ```bash
   # Using curl, ignoring the self‑signed cert
   curl -k https://localhost:8443/hello
   # → Hello, HTTPS! This is a secure response.
   ```

---

## 4️⃣  What to change for a **real production** deployment  

| Area | What to adjust |
|------|----------------|
| **Keystore** | Use a certificate issued by a trusted CA (or an internal PKI). Do **not** use the `changeit` password. |
| **Trust manager** | Replace the “trust‑all” `X509TrustManager` with the default (`null`) or a proper validation chain. |
| **Cipher suites / Protocols** | Call `sslParams.setCipherSuites(...)` and `sslParams.setProtocols(...)` to disable outdated algorithms (e.g., TLS 1.0, RC4). |
| **Client authentication** | If you need mutual TLS, configure `sslParams.setNeedClientAuth(true)` and load a truststore with the client CA certificates. |
| **Logging & monitoring** | Plug in a proper logger (SLF4J/Logback) instead of `System.out`. |
| **Thread pool** | Size the `Executor` according to expected load, perhaps using a `ThreadPoolExecutor` with custom queue policies. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Add a shutdown hook that calls `httpsServer.stop(0)`. |
| **HTTP handling** | For anything beyond trivial demos, consider a richer framework (Jetty, Netty, Spring Boot, etc.) – they give you routing, filters, async I/O, etc. |

---

## 5️⃣  Quick reference cheat‑sheet

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `keytool -list -keystore server.keystore -storetype PKCS12 -storepass changeit` | Verify the keystore contents. |
| `openssl s_client -connect localhost:8443 -servername localhost` | Inspect the TLS handshake from the command line. |
| `java -Djavax.net.debug=ssl,handshake SimpleHttpsServer` | Enable verbose SSL debugging (very helpful when things don’t work). |
| `curl -v -k https://localhost:8443/hello` | See the raw HTTP response and TLS details. |

