### 2.2. Core Java classes (the only part that can’t change without recompiling both sides)

```java
package com.example.clustering;

import java.io.Serializable;
import java.util.Map;

/** Payload that represents one unit of work. */
public final class Task implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    /** Unique ID so we can match results later */
    private final long taskId;

    /** Arbitrary data that the worker will use – keep it small! */
    private final Map<String, Object> parameters;

    public Task(long taskId, Map<String, Object> parameters) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.parameters = parameters;
    }

    public long getTaskId() { return taskId; }
    public Map<String, Object> getParameters() { return parameters; }
}

/** Result produced by a Task. */
public final class Result implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final long taskId;
    private final Object payload;          // could be anything serializable
    private final long durationMillis;     // optional profiling data

    public Result(long taskId, Object payload, long durationMillis) {
        this.taskId = taskId;
        this.payload = payload;
        this.durationMillis = durationMillis;
    }

    public long getTaskId() { return taskId; }
    public Object getPayload() { return payload; }
    public long getDurationMillis() { return durationMillis; }
}

/** Very small envelope – the first byte on the wire tells the receiver what follows */
public enum MessageType {
    TASK, RESULT, HEARTBEAT, SHUTDOWN
}

/** Wrapper that carries type + payload */
public final class Message implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final MessageType type;
    private final Object payload;       // must be either Task or Result

    public Message(MessageType type, Object payload) {
        this.type = type;
        this.payload = payload;
    }

    public MessageType getType() { return type; }
    public Object getPayload() { return payload; }
}
```

*Why `Serializable`?*  
It lets us use `ObjectOutputStream` for a fast proof‑of‑concept. When you need higher throughput you can **swap the serializer** (Kryo, protobuf, FlatBuffers) without touching any other code – just change the `writeMessage(..)`/`readMessage(..)` helpers.

---

## 3️⃣ Networking Layer – “TaskManager” / “TaskClient”

The networking code lives **outside** the core library so the core stays pure‑Java. The pattern is:

1. **Reader thread** – blocks on the socket, deserializes a `Message`, puts it on a `BlockingQueue`.
2. **Writer thread** – takes `Message` objects from a queue and streams them out.
3. **Worker pool** – pulls `Task`s from the inbound queue, processes them, and pushes `Result`s onto the outbound queue.

We’ll write **two thin wrappers**:

* `DesktopTaskManager` – runs on the PC, accepts many Android workers.
* `AndroidTaskClient` – runs on Android, connects to the desktop.

Both share the same **TaskProcessor** interface that you implement once.

### 3.1. Common utilities (socket + serialization)

```java
package com.example.clustering.net;

import com.example.clustering.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public final class SocketHelper {

    /** Write a Message atomically – synchronised on the underlying stream */
    public static void writeMessage(ObjectOutputStream out, Message msg) throws IOException {
        synchronized (out) {
            out.writeObject(msg);
            out.flush();      // important for TCP push
        }
    }

    /** Blocking read – returns null only on EOF/exception */
    public static Message readMessage(ObjectInputStream in) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        return (Message) in.readObject();
    }
}
```

### 3.2. The **TaskProcessor** contract

```java
package com.example.clustering;

@FunctionalInterface
public interface TaskProcessor {
    /** Process a task and return the result. Must not block the caller long-term. */
    Result process(Task task) throws Exception;
}
```

Implement it **once** – you can reuse the same logic on both sides, just with different data volumes.

### 3.3. Desktop – Multi‑client TaskManager

```java
package com.example.desktop;

import com.example.clustering.*;
import com.example.clustering.net.SocketHelper;

import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DesktopTaskManager {

    private final int listenPort;
    private final TaskProcessor processor;
    private final ExecutorService workerPool;
    private final ServerSocket serverSocket;
    private final Map<Socket, ClientHandler> clients = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public DesktopTaskManager(int listenPort, TaskProcessor processor, int workerThreads) throws IOException {
        this.listenPort = listenPort;
        this.processor = processor;
        this.workerPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(workerThreads);
        this.serverSocket = new ServerSocket(listenPort);
    }

    /** Start accepting connections in a background thread */
    public void start() {
        Thread acceptor = new Thread(this::acceptLoop, "Desktop-Acceptor");
        acceptor.setDaemon(true);
        acceptor.start();
    }

    private void acceptLoop() {
        while (!serverSocket.isClosed()) {
            try {
                Socket client = serverSocket.accept();
                System.out.println("New worker connected: " + client);
                ClientHandler handler = new ClientHandler(client);
                clients.put(client, handler);
                handler.start();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                if (!serverSocket.isClosed()) e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }

    /** Push a new Task to *any* connected client (simple round‑robin) */
    public void submitTask(Task task) {
        // Very naïve load‑balancing – just pick the first client with a free queue.
        for (ClientHandler h : clients.values()) {
            if (h.offerTask(task)) {
                return;
            }
        }
        // No client able to accept – fallback to local processing
        System.out.println("No remote worker available → processing locally");
        workerPool.submit(() -> {
            try {
                Result r = processor.process(task);
                // you could store the result locally, or route it somewhere else
                System.out.println("Locally processed task " + task.getTaskId() + " => " + r.getPayload());
            } catch (Exception ex) {
                ex.printStackTrace();
            }
        });
    }

    /** Graceful shutdown */
    public void stop() throws IOException {
        for (ClientHandler h : clients.values()) h.shutdown();
        serverSocket.close();
        workerPool.shutdownNow();
    }

    /** One client – its own inbound/outbound queues + its own socket */
    private class ClientHandler {
        private final Socket socket;
        private final ObjectOutputStream out;
        private final ObjectInputStream in;
        private final BlockingQueue<Task> outboundTaskQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(10);
        private final ExecutorService writer = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
        private final ExecutorService reader = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

        ClientHandler(Socket socket) throws IOException {
            this.socket = socket;
            this.out = new ObjectOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());
            this.in = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
        }

        void start() {
            // Writer: pull Task from queue -> send to client
            writer.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    while (!socket.isClosed()) {
                        Task task = outboundTaskQueue.take(); // blocks
                        SocketHelper.writeMessage(out, new Message(MessageType.TASK, task));
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException | IOException e) {
                    // normal when client disconnects
                }
            });

            // Reader: read Result objects and feed back to local pool
            reader.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    while (!socket.isClosed()) {
                        Message msg = SocketHelper.readMessage(in);
                        if (msg.getType() == MessageType.RESULT) {
                            Result r = (Result) msg.getPayload();
                            // Example: simply log it, or forward to your UI
                            System.out.println("Received result for task " + r.getTaskId()
                                    + " => " + r.getPayload() + " (took " + r.getDurationMillis() + "ms)");
                        } else if (msg.getType() == MessageType.HEARTBEAT) {
                            // ignore or update liveness map
                        }
                    }
                } catch (EOFException e) {
                    // client closed
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            });
        }

        /** Offer a task to this client – returns false if its queue is full */
        boolean offerTask(Task task) {
            return outboundTaskQueue.offer(task);
        }

        void shutdown() throws IOException {
            socket.close();
            writer.shutdownNow();
            reader.shutdownNow();
        }
    }
}
```

