### What’s really happening under the hood?

| DSL method                | Matcher class (spring‑security version)                               | Pattern syntax |
|---------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|
| `pathMatchers(String…)`  | **Servlet stack** – `AntPathRequestMatcher` (uses `org.springframework.util.AntPathMatcher`)<br>**WebFlux stack** – `PathPatternParserServerWebExchangeMatcher` (uses `PathPatternParser`) | Ant‑style patterns (`*`, `**`, `?`) |
| `anyExchange()`           | matches *every* request that didn’t match a previous `pathMatchers`   | N/A            |

* `*`  – matches **zero or one** path segment (`/foo/*/bar` matches `/foo/x/bar` but not `/foo/x/y/bar`).  
* `?`  – matches **one character** inside a segment.  
* `**` – matches **zero or more whole path segments**. It can appear **anywhere** in the pattern, even multiple times, and it can also be the **first** token.

> **Tip:**  
> The `**` wildcard is **greedy** – it will consume as many segments as possible while still allowing the rest of the pattern to match. In most cases this is exactly what you want, but if you need a *non‑greedy* match you have to split the rule into two separate `pathMatchers` calls.

### Common real‑world use‑cases

| Goal | Pattern | Explanation |
|------|---------|-------------|
| **Public API that can live under any version prefix** (`/v1/api/**`, `/v2/api/**`, …) | `"/**/api/**"` | `**` at the start swallows `/v1`, `/v2`, or any custom prefix; the second `**` captures the rest of the endpoint tree. |
| **Admin console that may be hosted under a custom context root** (`/admin/**` or `/my‑app/admin/**`) | `"/**/admin/**"` | Works no matter what “root” you deploy the war/jar under. |
| **Health‑check endpoint that can be placed anywhere** (`/health`, `/actuator/health`, `/foo/health`) | `"/**/health"` | Matches a single‑segment `health` at any depth. |
| **Static assets served from a versioned folder** (`/static/v1/images/**`, `/static/v2/images/**`) | `"/static/**/images/**"` | The first `**` eats the version part, the second `**` eats any sub‑folder under `images`. |
| **Swagger UI that may appear under a “prefix” path** (`/swagger-ui.html`, `/myapp/swagger-ui.html`) | `"/**/swagger-ui.html"` | Guarantees the UI is reachable no matter where you mount the app. |

### Frequently asked nuances

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| **Can `**` appear together with other characters inside the same segment?** | **No.** `**` must be a *stand‑alone* segment (i.e. surrounded by `/` or at the very start/end). `/**foo**/` is illegal and will throw an `IllegalArgumentException`. |
| **Do I need to escape `*` or `?`?** | No. Ant‑style patterns treat these characters as wildcards automatically. If you really want a literal `*` in the URL you must either (a) encode it (`%2A`) or (b) avoid using `pathMatchers` for that particular rule. |
| **What if I need “any‑exact‑segment‑count” (e.g. exactly two segments)?** | Use a combination of `*` placeholders: `"/*/admin/**"` matches *exactly* one segment before `admin`. For “exactly two” use `"/*/*/admin/**"`. |
| **Is `/**` the same as `/**/**`?** | Yes, both match *any* path depth, but `/**` is the canonical, shortest representation. |
| **Can I combine several patterns in a single `pathMatchers` call?** | Absolutely: `.pathMatchers("/public/**", "/error/**", "/**/health")`. The order of those arguments does **not** matter; the matcher builds a **union** of them. |
| **Does the order of `pathMatchers` calls matter?** | **Yes.** Spring Security evaluates the *first* matching rule it finds. Place the most specific patterns **before** the more generic ones (e.g. `/admin/**` before `/**`). |
| **What about trailing slashes?** | The matcher normalises the request path, so `/admin/` and `/admin` are considered equivalent. Both will match `"/admin/**"` and `"/admin"` alike. |

