## 3️⃣  **Option A – Functional routing (the natural fit for `HandlerFilterFunction`)**

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.router;

import com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis.RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.*;

import static org.springframework.web.reactive.function.server.RequestPredicates.*;

@Configuration
public class ApiRouter {

    /** Your regular handler (could be a service, component, etc.) */
    private final MyHandler myHandler;
    private final RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction rateLimiter;

    public ApiRouter(MyHandler myHandler,
                     RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction rateLimiter) {
        this.myHandler = myHandler;
        this.rateLimiter = rateLimiter;
    }

    @Bean
    public RouterFunction<ServerResponse> routes() {
        return RouterFunctions
                // 👉 all routes go through the rate‑limiter filter
                .route(GET("/api/v1/hello")
                                .and(accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)),
                        myHandler::hello)          // <-- your business logic
                .filter(rateLimiter)                // <--- apply the filter
                .andOther(RouterFunctions
                        .route(GET("/api/v1/goodbye")
                                        .and(accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)),
                                myHandler::goodbye)
                        .filter(rateLimiter));
    }
}
```

*`MyHandler`* can be a simple component that returns a `Mono<ServerResponse>`:

```java
@Component
public class MyHandler {

    public Mono<ServerResponse> hello(ServerRequest request) {
        return ServerResponse.ok()
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .bodyValue(Collections.singletonMap("msg", "Hello world!"));
    }

    public Mono<ServerResponse> goodbye(ServerRequest request) {
        return ServerResponse.ok()
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .bodyValue(Collections.singletonMap("msg", "Good‑bye!"));
    }
}
```

Result: every request that matches `/api/v1/**` goes through `RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction`. If the client (identified by IP) exceeds the configured limit **20 requests per minute**, a `429 Too Many Requests` is returned instantly – no controller logic is executed.

