## 4️⃣  **Option B – Using an annotation‑driven `@RestController`**

If you prefer the classic `@RestController` style, the **same rate‑limiting logic** can be expressed as a `WebFilter`. This filter sits in the **WebFlux filter chain** and works for any `@RequestMapping` endpoint.

### 4.1  Create a `WebFilter` that delegates to the same Redis code

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.filter;

import com.mediaworld.apimanager.configuration.redis.RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import org.springframework.web.server.WebFilter;
import org.springframework.web.server.WebFilterChain;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

/**
 * A **global** filter that re‑uses the functional implementation.
 * It extracts the same pieces of information from the reactive request
 * and either short‑circuits with 429 or lets the request continue.
 */
@Component
public class RateLimiterWebFilter implements WebFilter {

    private final RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction delegate;

    public RateLimiterWebFilter(RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction delegate) {
        this.delegate = delegate;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, WebFilterChain chain) {

        // Build a "fake" ServerRequest that the functional filter expects
        ServerRequest fakeRequest = ServerRequest.create(exchange, List.of());

        // Wrap the chain into a HandlerFunction:
        HandlerFunction<ServerResponse> next = req -> {
            // Chain the original downstream WebFilterChain and adapt the result back to ServerResponse
            return chain.filter(exchange)
                    .then(Mono.empty())
                    .then(Mono.defer(() -> ServerResponse.ok()
                            .build()));
        };

        // Run the functional filter
        return delegate.filter(fakeRequest, next)
                .flatMap(resp -> {
                    // If the filter produced a response (e.g. 429) write it back immediately
                    if (resp.statusCode().equals(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)) {
                        exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);
                        return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
                    }
                    // Otherwise the request was already forwarded downstream → nothing to do here
                    return Mono.empty();
                })
                // If the functional filter returned `Mono.empty()` (i.e. count < limit)
                // we simply continue with the original chain
                .then(chain.filter(exchange));
    }
}
```

> **Important:** The `WebFilter` above is a **thin adapter** – it does **not** duplicate the Redis logic, it simply forwards to the existing `RateLimiterHandlerFilterFunction`. This means you keep a **single source of truth** for the rate‑limiting algorithm.

### 4.2  Sample `@RestController`

```java
package com.mediaworld.apimanager.controller;

import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Map;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1")
public class GreetingController {

    @GetMapping(value = "/hello", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    public Mono<Map<String, String>> hello() {
        return Mono.just(Collections.singletonMap("msg", "Hello from @RestController"));
    }

    @GetMapping(value = "/goodbye", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    public Mono<Map<String, String>> goodbye() {
        return Mono.just(Collections.singletonMap("msg", "Good‑bye from @RestController"));
    }
}
```

Because `RateLimiterWebFilter` is a **`@Component`**, Spring registers it automatically **for every request**. If you want to apply it only to a specific path pattern, you can:

```java
@Component
@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)               // optional – decide ordering
public class RateLimiterWebFilter implements WebFilter {
    // ...

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, WebFilterChain chain) {
        // Apply only to "/api/v1/**"
        if (!exchange.getRequest().getPath().value().startsWith("/api/v1/")) {
            return chain.filter(exchange); // skip rate‑limiting
        }
        // … normal logic as above …
    }
}
```

