Below is a **complete, self‑contained example** of a tiny “database” that

* stores objects in a `java.util.Hashtable`,
* gives you the usual CRUD (Create‑Read‑Update‑Delete) operations,
* persists the whole table to disk by **Java serialization**, and
* can be re‑loaded later.

The code is deliberately simple so you can copy‑paste it into a single file (or split it into a few files) and start playing with it right away.

---

## 1.  The record that we will store – `Person`

```java
import java.io.Serializable;

/**
 * Example domain object – any class that implements Serializable can be stored.
 */
public class Person implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private final String id;          // primary key – must be unique
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Person(String id, String name, int age) {
        this.id   = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.age  = age;
    }

    // ----- getters / setters -------------------------------------------------
    public String getId()   { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void   setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public int    getAge()  { return age; }
    public void   setAge(int age) { this.age = age; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Person{id='" + id + '\'' +
               ", name='" + name + '\'' +
               ", age=" + age + '}';
    }
}
```

> **Why `Serializable`?**  
> Java’s built‑in object serialization can write *any* object graph to a stream as long as every class in the graph implements `java.io.Serializable`. Nothing else is required for our tiny DB.

---

## 2.  The mini‑DB itself – `MiniDB`

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.Hashtable;

/**
 * A very small in‑memory database that uses a Hashtable for storage
 * and Java serialization for persistence.
 *
 * @param <K> type of the key (must be Serializable)
 * @param <V> type of the value (must be Serializable)
 */
public class MiniDB<K extends Serializable, V extends Serializable> implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    /** The underlying storage – synchronized because Hashtable is thread‑safe. */
    private final Hashtable<K, V> table = new Hashtable<>();

    // ----- CRUD operations ---------------------------------------------------

    /** Create/Insert – overwrites any existing entry with the same key. */
    public void put(K key, V value) {
        table.put(key, value);
    }

    /** Read – returns the value for the given key or null if not present. */
    public V get(K key) {
        return table.get(key);
    }

    /** Update – same as put(); separate method only for API clarity. */
    public void update(K key, V value) {
        if (!table.containsKey(key)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("No entry with key '" + key + "' to update.");
        }
        table.put(key, value);
    }

    /** Delete – removes the key/value pair, returns the removed value or null. */
    public V delete(K key) {
        return table.remove(key);
    }

    /** Returns the number of stored records. */
    public int size() {
        return table.size();
    }

    // ----- Persistence (serialization) ---------------------------------------

    /**
     * Saves the whole database to the supplied file.
     *
     * @param file the destination file (will be overwritten)
     * @throws IOException if an I/O error occurs
     */
    public void saveToFile(File file) throws IOException {
        // Use try‑with‑resources so the stream is always closed
        try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(
                new BufferedOutputStream(new FileOutputStream(file)))) {
            out.writeObject(this);          // writes entire MiniDB instance
        }
    }

    /**
     * Loads a MiniDB from the supplied file.
     *
     * @param file the file previously written by {@link #saveToFile(File)}
     * @return a MiniDB instance populated with the stored records
     * @throws IOException            if an I/O error occurs
     * @throws ClassNotFoundException if the serialized classes cannot be found
     */
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static <K extends Serializable, V extends Serializable>
    MiniDB<K, V> loadFromFile(File file) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        try (ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(
                new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file)))) {
            // The cast is safe because the file was written by the same generic type
            return (MiniDB<K, V>) in.readObject();
        }
    }

    // ----- Utility -----------------------------------------------------------

    /** Returns a shallow copy of the internal Hashtable – useful for inspection. */
    public Hashtable<K, V> snapshot() {
        return (Hashtable<K, V>) table.clone();
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "MiniDB{" + table + '}';
    }
}
```

### What the class does

| Method | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `put` | Insert a new record (or replace an existing one). |
| `get` | Retrieve a record by its key. |
| `update` | Same as `put` but throws an exception if the key does not exist (helps catch bugs). |
| `delete` | Remove a record. |
| `saveToFile` | Serialize the whole `MiniDB` instance to a file. |
| `loadFromFile` | Re‑create a `MiniDB` instance from a previously saved file. |
| `snapshot` | Get a copy of the Hashtable for printing / debugging (the internal table itself stays private). |

Because `Hashtable` is synchronized, all operations are **thread‑safe** out of the box. If you don’t need synchronization you could replace it with `HashMap` and guard the methods yourself.

---

## 3.  Demo / Test – `MiniDBDemo`

```java
import java.io.File;

/**
 * Simple driver that shows how to use MiniDB.
 */
public class MiniDBDemo {
    private static final File DB_FILE = new File("person-db.ser");

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // -------------------------------------------------
            // 1️⃣  Create a fresh DB (or load an existing one)
            // -------------------------------------------------
            MiniDB<String, Person> db;
            if (DB_FILE.exists()) {
                System.out.println("Loading existing DB from " + DB_FILE);
                db = MiniDB.loadFromFile(DB_FILE);
            } else {
                System.out.println("No existing DB – creating a new one.");
                db = new MiniDB<>();
            }

            // -------------------------------------------------
            // 2️⃣  CRUD demo
            // -------------------------------------------------
            // CREATE
            Person alice = new Person("p001", "Alice", 30);
            Person bob   = new Person("p002", "Bob",   25);
            db.put(alice.getId(), alice);
            db.put(bob.getId(),   bob);

            // READ
            System.out.println("\n--- After insert ---");
            System.out.println("Alice = " + db.get("p001"));
            System.out.println("Bob   = " + db.get("p002"));
            System.out.println("Size  = " + db.size());

            // UPDATE
            Person bobUpdated = new Person("p002", "Robert", 26);
            db.update(bobUpdated.getId(), bobUpdated);

            System.out.println("\n--- After update ---");
            System.out.println("Bob = " + db.get("p002"));

            // DELETE
            db.delete("p001");
            System.out.println("\n--- After delete ---");
            System.out.println("Contains Alice? " + (db.get("p001") != null));
            System.out.println("Size = " + db.size());

            // -------------------------------------------------
            // 3️⃣  Persist the DB
            // -------------------------------------------------
            System.out.println("\nSaving DB to disk...");
            db.saveToFile(DB_FILE);
            System.out.println("Done.");

            // -------------------------------------------------
            // 4️⃣  Load again (just to prove it works)
            // -------------------------------------------------
            System.out.println("\nReloading DB from disk...");
            MiniDB<String, Person> reloaded = MiniDB.loadFromFile(DB_FILE);
            System.out.println("Reloaded DB contents: " + reloaded);

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

