## 5️⃣  Common pitfalls & how the class solves them

| Problem | What happens if you ignore it | How `SecureTcpClient` helps |
|--------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|
| **Network on UI thread** | `NetworkOnMainThreadException` → app crashes. | All heavy work runs in a dedicated `ExecutorService`. |
| **Untrusted/self‑signed certificates** | Handshake fails with `SSLHandshakeException`. | You can enable **trust‑all** (dev) or provide a **custom TrustManager** (pinning). |
| **Hostname mismatch** | Handshake succeeds, but you’re vulnerable to MITM. | By default `setVerifyHostname(true)` uses the built‑in hostname verifier (`HTTPS`). |
| **Lost data because the read‑loop stops** | You miss incoming bytes. | The read loop runs continuously until the remote closes or an error occurs, delivering every chunk via `onDataReceived`. |
| **Leaked sockets** | Battery drain, FD exhaustion. | `disconnect()` & internal error handling always close streams & sockets. |
| **Partial writes** | Some bytes might not be sent if you use `OutputStream.write(byte[])` incorrectly. | `SecureTcpClient.send()` flushes after every call; you can also batch multiple writes yourself. |

