## 5️⃣ `WebSecurityConfig.java` – The heart of the CAS integration  

```java
package org.eduverse.lms.config;

import org.jasig.cas.client.validation.Cas20ServiceTicketValidator;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.AuthenticationProvider;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.ProviderManager;
import org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.cas.ServiceProperties;
import org.springframework.security.cas.authentication.CasAuthenticationProvider;
import org.springframework.security.cas.web.CasAuthenticationEntryPoint;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.logout.LogoutSuccessHandler;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.logout.SecurityContextLogoutHandler;

import java.util.List;

/**
 * Configures Spring Security to use the Apereo CAS server as the
 * authentication authority for Eduverse LMS.
 */
@Configuration
public class WebSecurityConfig {

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1️⃣  CAS server URLs (injected from application.yml)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Value("${cas.serverUrlPrefix}")
    private String casServerUrl;

    @Value("${cas.serviceUrl}")
    private String serviceUrl;

    @Value("${cas.loginUrl}")
    private String casLoginUrl;

    @Value("${cas.logoutUrl}")
    private String casLogoutUrl;

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  ServiceProperties – tells Spring Security what the
    //     callback URL (service URL) is.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public ServiceProperties serviceProperties() {
        ServiceProperties sp = new ServiceProperties();
        sp.setService(serviceUrl);
        // `sendRenew` forces a fresh login at the CAS server – we keep false for normal SSO
        sp.setSendRenew(false);
        return sp;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  TicketValidator – uses the CAS 2.0 protocol (the most common)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public Cas20ServiceTicketValidator ticketValidator() {
        // true -> allow any proxy (good for demo); set false in production & list allowed proxies.
        return new Cas20ServiceTicketValidator(casServerUrl);
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  AuthenticationProvider – validates the ticket & builds a Spring Security principal
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public AuthenticationProvider casAuthenticationProvider(ServiceProperties serviceProperties,
                                                             Cas20ServiceTicketValidator ticketValidator) {
        CasAuthenticationProvider provider = new CasAuthenticationProvider();
        provider.setServiceProperties(serviceProperties);
        provider.setTicketValidator(ticketValidator);
        provider.setKey("eduverseCAS"); // arbitrary identifier; must be unique per provider

        // Optionally you can map CAS attributes to local authorities:
        // provider.setAuthoritiesMapper(...);
        // For this simple demo we grant every user a ROLE_USER.
        provider.setUserDetailsService(username -> {
            // In a real Eduverse LMS you’d look up the student record, roles, etc.
            // For now we just give a generic user.
            return org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User
                    .withUsername(username)
                    .password("")          // password is not used – CAS already authenticated
                    .authorities("ROLE_USER")
                    .accountExpired(false)
                    .accountLocked(false)
                    .credentialsExpired(false)
                    .disabled(false)
                    .build();
        });

        return provider;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  Entry point – when an unauthenticated request arrives, redirect to CAS login page.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public CasAuthenticationEntryPoint casAuthenticationEntryPoint(ServiceProperties serviceProperties) {
        CasAuthenticationEntryPoint entryPoint = new CasAuthenticationEntryPoint();
        entryPoint.setLoginUrl(casLoginUrl);   // e.g. https://cas.eduverse.org/cas/login
        entryPoint.setServiceProperties(serviceProperties);
        return entryPoint;
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  Logout handling – send a CAS‑single‑logout request + clear local session.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    public LogoutSuccessHandler casLogoutSuccessHandler() {
        return (request, response, authentication) -> {
            // After local logout, redirect the user to the CAS logout page.
            // You can also append a `service` parameter to bring them back to a public
            // page after logout, e.g. https://lms.eduverse.org/logoutSuccess.
            String redirect = casLogoutUrl + "?service=" + serviceUrl;
            response.sendRedirect(redirect);
        };
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 7️⃣  Security filter chain – glue everything together.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    @Bean
    @Order(1)   // Ensure this chain runs before any other (if you have multiple)
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http,
                                            ServiceProperties serviceProperties,
                                            AuthenticationProvider casAuthProvider,
                                            CasAuthenticationEntryPoint entryPoint,
                                            LogoutSuccessHandler logoutSuccessHandler) throws Exception {

        // Register the CAS authentication provider
        http.authenticationProvider(casAuthProvider);

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7a️⃣  Declare which URLs are public, which need authentication.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/", "/public/**", "/error", "/logout").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()           // everything else requires CAS login
        );

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7b️⃣  Use the CAS entry point for unauthenticated access.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.exceptionHandling(ex -> ex
                .authenticationEntryPoint(entryPoint)
        );

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7c️⃣  Enable CAS “login” filter (automatically created behind the scenes).
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults()); // not used, but keeps Spring happy

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7d️⃣  Session management – optional, but we usually want a single session per user.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.sessionManagement(sess -> sess
                .maximumSessions(1)
                .maxSessionsPreventsLogin(false)
        );

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7e️⃣  Logout – first invalidate local session, then tell CAS to log out globally.
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.logout(logout -> logout
                .logoutUrl("/logout")
                .addLogoutHandler(new SecurityContextLogoutHandler())
                .logoutSuccessHandler(logoutSuccessHandler)
                .permitAll()
        );

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7f️⃣  CSRF – disabled for a quick demo (enable in production!).
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());

        return http.build();
    }
}
```

### What the filter chain does, step‑by‑step

| Request flow | What happens |
|---|---|
| **Unauthenticated → `/dashboard`** | `CasAuthenticationEntryPoint` redirects Alex to `https://cas.eduverse.org/cas/login?service=http://localhost:8080/login/cas`. |
| **Alex enters credentials on CAS** | CAS validates Alex against the university LDAP / AD, creates a **Ticket‑Granting Ticket (TGT)**, then issues a **Service Ticket (ST)** and redirects him back to `http://localhost:8080/login/cas?ticket=ST‑…`. |
| **`/login/cas` endpoint (auto‑configured by Spring Security)** | Spring’s `CasAuthenticationFilter` extracts the `ticket`, validates it via `Cas20ServiceTicketValidator`, builds a `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` with the principal (Alex) and authorities (`ROLE_USER`). |
| **Successful authentication** | Alex’s `SecurityContext` now holds a fully authenticated principal. Any request to protected URLs (`/dashboard`, `/courses/*`) proceeds. |
| **Logout (`/logout`)** | Spring clears the local session, then redirects Alex to the CAS `/logout` endpoint, which destroys his TGT and causes **single‑sign‑out** across all Eduverse services. |

