## 4️⃣ Common pitfalls & how the snippet avoids them

| Problem | What can go wrong | How the code protects you |
|---------|-------------------|---------------------------|
| **XML External Entity (XXE) attacks** | If the string contains `<!DOCTYPE …>` the parser may fetch remote resources. | All parser features that enable external entities are disabled (`disallow-doctype-decl`, etc.). |
| **Namespace mismatches** | XPath `"//book"` returns nothing when the XML actually uses `<bk:book>` with a namespace. | The utility is *namespace‑aware* (`setNamespaceAware(true)`). You can supply a `NamespaceContext`. |
| **Returning a node with an XML declaration** | `Transformer` by default adds `<?xml …?>`, which makes a fragment illegal inside another XML document. | `OMIT_XML_DECLARATION = "yes"` ensures only the fragment is emitted. |
| **Getting a `Text` node instead of an `Element`** | If you evaluate an XPath that ends on a text node (`/book/title/text()`) the transformer cannot handle it directly. | The helper wraps non‑element nodes in a dummy document so the transform still works. |
| **Performance on large documents** | Parsing the whole XML for each extraction is costly. | The API is deliberately simple; for heavy‑use you can parse once, keep the `Document`, and reuse the `XPathExpression` objects. (Just move the `Document` and compiled `XPathExpression` to fields.) |
| **Indentation/pretty‑printing** | By default, the fragment may be a single long line. | The transformer is configured with `INDENT="yes"` and an indent amount of 2 spaces (adjustable). |

