### What’s happening in the code you posted?

```js
function loadcontent() {
    document.getElementById("forum-content").innerHTML = '';

    function handleSuccess(response) {
        // … build a bunch of <div>/<p>/<u> elements …
    }
}
```

* You **clear** the container (`#forum-content`), which is fine.  
* You **declare** a function called `handleSuccess`, but you never **call** it.  
* Inside `handleSuccess` you **create** a `<div>` (`var divs = document.createElement("div");`) and you put `<p>` / `<u>` tags **inside** that `<div>`, but you never **append** that `<div>` to anything in the DOM.  
* There is also no AJAX request in the snippet, so there is nothing that ever gives you a `response` to feed into `handleSuccess`.

Because the async callback never runs and nothing is appended to the page, you see **no output** even though there are no JavaScript errors.

Below is a **complete, working example** that shows exactly how to fetch the text file, split it the way you want, create the elements, and finally insert them into `#forum-content`.

---

## A clean, working version (using the native `fetch` API)

> **Why `fetch`?**  
> It’s built‑in to modern browsers, requires no extra library, and works the same way as any Ajax call you would write with jQuery, Axios, etc. If you *must* use jQuery, replace the `fetch…` block with `$.ajax({...})` – the rest of the code stays the same.

```html
<!-- HTML -->
<div id="forum-content"></div>
<button onclick="loadContent()">Load forum text</button>
```

```js
/* JavaScript */
function loadContent() {
    const container = document.getElementById('forum-content');

    // 1️⃣  Clear any previous content
    container.innerHTML = '';

    // 2️⃣  Request the file (replace with your real URL)
    fetch('path/to/your/file.txt')
        .then(res => {
            if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
            return res.text();                // we expect a plain‑text response
        })
        .then(handleSuccess)                  // <-- call the callback with the response
        .catch(err => console.error('Load failed:', err));

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  This is the callback that builds the DOM nodes
    // -------------------------------------------------
    function handleSuccess(response) {
        console.log('Raw response →', response);

        // The file is assumed to be divided into “sections” by "~"
        // e.g.  "section1 line1\nline2~section2 line1\nline2"
        const sections = response.split('~');

        sections.forEach(section => {
            // Trim removes a stray newline at the very start / end of a section
            const lines = section.trim().split('\n');

            // Create a container for this section
            const block = document.createElement('div');   // <-- we’ll append this later

            // Build each line inside the section
            lines.forEach(rawLine => {
                const line = rawLine.trim();   // get rid of leading/trailing spaces

                // If the line ends with a colon (":") we want an <u> element,
                // otherwise a normal <p>.  The `endsWith` test is simpler than the
                // manual substring you had before.
                const el = line.endsWith(':')
                    ? document.createElement('u')
                    : document.createElement('p');

                // `textContent` is safer than `innerHTML` when you are only showing
                // plain text (prevents accidental HTML injection).
                el.textContent = '>>> ' + line;

                block.appendChild(el);
            });

            // Finally attach the whole section to the page
            container.appendChild(block);
        });
    }
}
```

### What changed?

| # | Issue in the original snippet | Fix |
|---|------------------------------|-----|
| 1 | `handleSuccess` never called. | The `fetch(...).then(handleSuccess)` line invokes it when the request resolves. |
| 2 | Created `<div>` (`divs`) never appended to the DOM. | After the inner loop finishes we do `container.appendChild(block)`. |
| 3 | No actual Ajax request. | Added a `fetch` call (or you could use `$.ajax` if you prefer jQuery). |
| 4 | Mixed use of `innerHTML` for plain text. | Switched to `textContent` – safer and avoids XSS. |
| 5 | Manual `substring` check for `:` was fragile. | Used `String.prototype.endsWith(':')`, which is clearer. |
| 6 | Variable naming (`divs`) and scoping (`var` vs `let`). | Use `const`/`let` consistently; `block` is more descriptive. |
| 7 | Missing closing braces in the posted code (caused syntax errors if copied verbatim). | All braces are balanced in the final version. |

---

## If you really want to keep **jQuery Ajax**

```js
function loadContent() {
    const $container = $('#forum-content').empty();

    $.ajax({
        url: 'path/to/your/file.txt',
        dataType: 'text',
        success: handleSuccess,
        error: (xhr, status, err) => console.error('Load failed:', err)
    });

    function handleSuccess(response) {
        // exactly the same code from above, just replace
        //   const container = document.getElementById('forum-content');
        // with
        //   const $container = $('#forum-content');
        // and use $container.append(block);
        // (the rest of the function can stay unchanged)
    }
}
```

The only difference is the way the request is made; the DOM‑building logic stays the same.

---

## Quick checklist when you’re still not seeing anything

1. **Open DevTools → Network** and confirm that the request for `file.txt` returns **200 OK** and the expected body.
2. **Console → Logs** – you should see the `Raw response → …` message. If not, the request failed.
3. **Inspect the DOM** – after the request finishes you should see one (or more) `<div>` elements inside `#forum-content`.  
   If they’re missing, double‑check the `appendChild`/`append` call.
4. Make sure the **file’s encoding** is plain text (UTF‑8). If the server sends a different MIME type, some browsers may block the request for security reasons.

---

### TL;DR

* **Call** the success handler (via `fetch(...).then(handleSuccess)` or jQuery’s `success:` option).  
* **Append** the built `<div>` to the container (`container.appendChild(div)` / `$container.append(div)`).  
* Use a proper Ajax request to actually get the data.

With those two fixes the text from your file will appear exactly as you intended. 🎉

