## TL;DR  

* A middle‑click (or “open in new tab”) does **not** perform a client‑side navigation – the browser loads the URL as a **brand‑new page**.  
* The React component that contains your `useEffect(() => …, [])` is therefore **mounted only after the page is hydrated**.  
* If the effect that fires the Effector event is **conditioned on something that only changes on a client‑side navigation** (e.g. `location`, a router‐derived prop, a store flag, a debounce, etc.) the effect never runs on the fresh‑load case.  

The fix is to make the “fetch‑status‑on‑mount” logic **independent of navigation state** and to ensure it runs the first time the component is rendered, no matter how the page was opened.

Below is a checklist of the most common pitfalls and concrete ways to guarantee that the event is dispatched both on normal SPA navigation **and** when the page is opened in a new tab.

---

## 1. What actually happens when you middle‑click a `<Link>` (or a plain `<a>`)?

| Navigation method | What the browser does | What React does |
|-------------------|-----------------------|-----------------|
| **Normal click** (left button, no modifiers) | React Router intercepts the click, prevents a full page reload, changes the history entry, re‑renders the matching route component. | The component is **already mounted** (or is mounted by the router) → `useEffect` runs. |
| **Middle‑click / Ctrl‑click / “Open in new tab”** | Browser performs a **full HTTP request** for the URL, loads a fresh copy of `index.html`, runs the JS bundle from scratch, then hydrates the React tree. | The whole React app boots again. The component is mounted **once**, after hydration. If your effect relies on a *change* that never happens on this fresh load, it stays silent. |

So the *only* thing you can rely on in both cases is the **mount** of the component itself.

---

## 2. Typical patterns that break on fresh loads

| Pattern | Why it fails on fresh load | Example |
|---------|---------------------------|---------|
| **Effect depends on `location` / router params** | On a fresh load the location never “changes” after the component mounts, so an effect that watches `location` won’t fire. | ```js useEffect(() => { fetchStatus(); }, [location.pathname]); ``` |
| **Guarded by a store flag that is already set** | If you initialise the store with a default value (e.g. `status: null`) and then set it to “loading” **outside** of the component (maybe in a root layout), the guard (`if (!status) …`) is false on the first render of the new tab. | ```js const status = useStore($status); if (status) return; // skip request ``` |
| **Effector `sample`/`guard` with `filter` that blocks the first call** | A filter that checks `!store.isLoaded` works after a client‑side navigation (because `isLoaded` is still `false`), but on a hard reload the store is recreated with `isLoaded: true` (e.g. from SSR or localStorage). | ```js sample({ clock: initPage, filter: $isLoaded.map(v => !v), target: fetchFx })``` |
| **Debounce / throttle** on the event | The first call can be dropped if the debounce timer is started elsewhere (e.g. in a parent component that isn’t rendered on the fresh page). | ```js createEvent().debounce(300)``` |

If any of the above patterns appear in your code, they are the *most likely* reason the event never fires when the page opens in a new tab.

---

## 3. The safe way to fire a request on component mount

### 3.1. Use plain `useEffect` with **empty dependency array**

```tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useEvent } from 'effector-react';
import { fetchStatus } from '@/effector/status';

export const StatusPage = () => {
  const requestStatus = useEvent(fetchStatus); // `fetchStatus` is an Effector event (or effect)

  useEffect(() => {
    // This runs **once** the component is mounted, regardless of how the page arrived.
    requestStatus();
  }, []); // <-- empty array = "run only on mount"

  // … render UI …
};
```

**Why it works**

* The empty dependency array guarantees the effect is scheduled after the first paint.
* The effect does **not** depend on any external variable that could stay constant on a full reload.

### 3.2. Prefer `useIsomorphicLayoutEffect` for SSR‑aware apps

If you also render the page on the server (Next.js, Remix, etc.), the effect will be ignored during SSR (as it should) and will run once on the client after hydration:

```tsx
import { useIsomorphicLayoutEffect } from 'react-use';
import { useEvent } from 'effector-react';
import { fetchStatusFx } from '@/effector/status';

export const StatusPage = () => {
  const request = useEvent(fetchStatusFx);

  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {
    request();
  }, []);
};
```

### 3.3. Or, use the **Effector‑React** helper `useEffect` (`useUnit`) with `onMount`

Effector‑React ships a convenient `useEffect`‑compatible helper that runs **exactly once when the component mounts** – even if you are in StrictMode (double‑mount) it will fire only after the component is actually inserted into the DOM.

```tsx
import { useEvent, useIsomorphicLayoutEffect } from 'effector-react';
import { fetchStatusFx } from '@/effector/status';

export const StatusPage = () => {
  const load = useEvent(fetchStatusFx);

  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {
    load();
  }, []); // Same as above, but using the effector‑react hook for typing
};
```

---

## 4. If you still need **router data** (e.g. an ID from the URL)

When a page requires a param (`/users/:id`) you can still safely request inside the same mount effect – just read the param **once** from the router and include it in the dependency array **as a constant**:

```tsx
import { useParams } from 'react-router-dom';
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useEvent } from 'effector-react';
import { loadUserFx } from '@/effector/user';

export const UserPage = () => {
  const { id } = useParams<{ id: string }>();
  const loadUser = useEvent(loadUserFx);

  useEffect(() => {
    // `id` is a string that never changes for the lifetime of this component instance
    loadUser(id);
  }, [id]); // OK – `id` is stable because the component will unmount if the route changes
};
```

If the route *does not* change (the same component stays mounted while the URL changes), you’d need a second effect that monitors the param, but for the “open in new tab” case the component is mounted **once**, so the first effect suffices.

---

## 5. Guard against accidental double‑mount in React 18 StrictMode

React 18 runs `useEffect` **twice** in development (mount → unmount → mount) to help spot side‑effects. This can cause two requests to be sent on a fresh load. If you want exactly **one** request even in StrictMode, wrap the call in an `once` guard:

```tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useEvent } from 'effector-react';
import { createEvent, createStore } from 'effector';

const initRequested = createEvent();
const $initDone = createStore(false).on(initRequested, () => true);

export const StatusPage = () => {
  const request = useEvent(fetchStatus);
  const initDone = useStore($initDone);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!initDone) {
      request();
      initRequested(); // mark that we already asked
    }
  }, [initDone]);
};
```

Or simply let Effector’s own `fx` deduplicate – most `createEffect` calls are **idempotent** because they return a promise you can ignore extra calls.

---

## 6. Full‑example (React‑Router v6 + Effector)

```tsx
// src/pages/StatusPage.tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useEvent } from 'effector-react';
import { fetchStatusFx } from '@/effector/status';

export const StatusPage = () => {
  const loadStatus = useEvent(fetchStatusFx);

  // 1️⃣ Run exactly once when the component appears, no matter how the page was opened.
  useEffect(() => {
    loadStatus();
  }, []); // <-- essential part

  // UI rendering …
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Status</h1>
      {/* show status from store, etc. */}
    </div>
  );
};
```

```tsx
// src/App.tsx
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';
import { StatusPage } from '@/pages/StatusPage';
import { HomePage } from '@/pages/HomePage';

export const App = () => (
  <BrowserRouter>
    <nav>
      <Link to="/">Home</Link>
      {/* note: <Link to="/status" target="_blank"> opens in a new tab */}
      <Link to="/status" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Status (new tab)</Link>
    </nav>

    <Routes>
      <Route path="/" element={<HomePage />} />
      <Route path="/status" element={<StatusPage />} />
    </Routes>
  </BrowserRouter>
);
```

**Test it**

1. Click the “Status” link normally → the request fires (see network tab).  
2. Middle‑click or Ctrl‑click the same link → a new tab opens, request fires **again** (you’ll see a fresh network request).  
3. If step 2 shows no request, look for any of the guard patterns above.

---

## 7. Checklist – “Does my init‑effect work in a new tab?”

| ✅ Yes | ☐ No |
|---|---|
| The effect has **no dependencies** on router state that stay unchanged on a fresh load. | It listens to `location`, `history.action`, or any store that is already set. |
| The effect *dispatches* an Effector **event/effect** directly, not through a filtered guard that may be `false` on first render. | It uses `sample({…, filter: $something })` where `$something` is `true` on hard reload. |
| If you use a debounce/throttle, you call the **raw event** (`fetchStatus`) instead of the debounced version. | You call `fetchStatus.debounce(300)` by mistake. |
| You have **no early‑return** in the component that prevents rendering the hook (e.g. `if (!user) return null;`). | You guard the whole component with `if (store.ready) …`. |
| The component **mounts** on the page you open (check with a console.log in the component body). | The component isn’t rendered at all because of a mismatching route or lazy‑load fallback. |
| You’re not in a development‑only StrictMode double‑mount scenario that you mistake for “no request”. | You see two requests and think the first one failed. |

If any line is unchecked, adjust the code accordingly.

---

## 8. Bonus – Using a **global “app‑init”** event instead of per‑page `useEffect`

If many pages need a “load current status on first render”, you can centralise the logic:

```ts
// src/effector/appInit.ts
import { createEvent, createEffect, createStore } from 'effector';

export const appInitialized = createEvent();
export const fetchStatusFx = createEffect(() => api.get('/status'));

export const $isAppReady = createStore(false)
  .on(appInitialized, () => true);

// Run once when the root component mounts
export const initApp = () => {
  appInitialized();   // could also be a Promise‑based async init
  fetchStatusFx();    // fire the status request
};
```

```tsx
// src/index.tsx
import React from 'react';
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { App } from './App';
import { initApp } from '@/effector/appInit';

const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!);
root.render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <App />
  </React.StrictMode>
);

// Kick‑off the global init **once per page load**
initApp();
```

Now every page can just read `$status` from the store; you no longer need a per‑page `useEffect`. The global init runs on a fresh load **and** on the first client‑side navigation (because the root component stays mounted, so you only call `initApp` once).

---

## 9. TL;DR code summary

```tsx
// 1️⃣ The simplest, bullet‑proof way:
useEffect(() => {
  // Fires on mount *and* when the page is opened in a new tab.
  requestStatus();              // <-- Effector event or effect
}, []);                         // <-- empty deps

// 2️⃣ If you need router params:
const { id } = useParams();
useEffect(() => {
  requestStatus(id);
}, [id]); // id is stable for the lifetime of this page instance

// 3️⃣ Optional: guard double‑mount in dev
const alreadyRequested = useRef(false);
useEffect(() => {
  if (!alreadyRequested.current) {
    alreadyRequested.current = true;
    requestStatus();
  }
}, []);
```

---

### Bottom line

The mouse‑wheel / middle‑click opens a **brand‑new document**. The only thing that survives from the previous page is the URL. Anything that only runs on a *router navigation* will never fire. Put the request inside a plain `useEffect(() => …, [])` (or an equivalent “on mount” helper) or centralise the init logic in the app’s root component. Once you do that, the Effector event will be dispatched whether the user navigates normally **or** opens the page in a new tab. Happy coding!

