Below is a **complete, self‑contained solution** that

1. walks through a DOM subtree,
2. finds every `<b>`, `<i>` and `<u>` element (they may be nested or appear together),
3. copies the text that lives inside them,
4. builds a `<span>` that carries the **equivalent CSS** (`font‑weight`, `font‑style`, `text‑decoration`),
5. puts the text back into that `<span>` and finally
6. removes the original tag from the markup.

The result is plain text (no `<b>/<i>/<u>` tags) but the visual appearance stays exactly the same.  
You can call the function on any container (e.g. `document.body`, a specific `<div>`, etc.).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Strip B/I/U tags while keeping styles</title>
  <style>
    /* Just a little demo styling */
    .demo { margin: 1rem; font-size: 1.2rem; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="demo" id="demo">
  Normal text,
  <b>bold text</b>,
  <i>italic text</i>,
  <u>underlined text</u>,
  <b><i>bold‑italic</i> and <u>underline‑inside‑bold</u></b>,
  <i><u>italic‑underline</u></i>.
</div>

<script>
/**
 * Convert all <b>, <i> and <u> elements inside `root` into plain text wrapped
 * in <span> elements that carry the same visual style.
 *
 * @param {Element|Document} root The node whose descendants should be processed.
 */
function replaceFormattingTagsWithStyledSpans(root) {
  // Mapping from tag name → CSS rule(s) that emulate its default semantics.
  const TAG_STYLES = {
    B: { fontWeight: 'bold' },
    I: { fontStyle:  'italic' },
    U: { textDecoration: 'underline' }
  };

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Gather **all** B/I/U elements in the subtree – we do it *first* and
  //     store them in an array because we will be mutating the DOM while we
  //     walk it later.  Traversing in reverse order guarantees that we replace
  //     children before their ancestors (so nested tags are handled correctly).
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  const elems = [];
  const walker = document.createTreeWalker(
    root,
    NodeFilter.SHOW_ELEMENT,
    null,
    false
  );

  let node;
  while ((node = walker.nextNode())) {
    if (TAG_STYLES[node.tagName]) {
      elems.push(node);
    }
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Replace each collected element with a <span> that merges *all* styles
  //     that apply to it (its own + the styles of any ancestor formatting tags).
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  for (let i = elems.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const el = elems[i];

    // ----- a) build a style object that aggregates the current element + its
    //          formatting ancestors (e.g. <b><i>…</i></b> → bold + italic).
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    const accumulatedStyle = {};

    // Walk up the tree merging the styles of any B/I/U ancestors.
    let ancestor = el;
    while (ancestor && ancestor !== root.parentNode) {
      const styles = TAG_STYLES[ancestor.tagName];
      if (styles) {
        Object.assign(accumulatedStyle, styles);
      }
      ancestor = ancestor.parentNode;
    }

    // ----- b) create the replacement <span> and apply the collected CSS.
    const span = document.createElement('span');

    // Convert the JS style object into a CSS string (e.g. "font-weight:bold;").
    span.style.cssText = Object.entries(accumulatedStyle)
      .map(([prop, value]) => `${prop.replace(/[A-Z]/g, m => '-' + m.toLowerCase())}:${value}`)
      .join(';');

    // ----- c) move *all* child nodes (text nodes, other elements, etc.) into the span.
    while (el.firstChild) {
      span.appendChild(el.firstChild);
    }

    // ----- d) replace the old formatting element with the new styled <span>.
    el.parentNode.replaceChild(span, el);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demo – run the conversion on the example container when the page loads.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const demo = document.getElementById('demo');
  replaceFormattingTagsWithStyledSpans(demo);
});
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

## How It Works – Step‑by‑Step Explanation

| Step | What Happens | Why It Matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1. Define a tag‑→‑style map** | `B → {fontWeight: 'bold'}` etc. | Gives us a *data‑driven* way to translate HTML tags into equivalent CSS rules. |
| **2. Collect all `<b>/<i>/<u>` nodes** | A `TreeWalker` walks the tree once, pushing matching elements into an array. | We must collect *first* because we will later **mutate** the DOM (replace nodes). If we walked and replaced simultaneously we could miss nested elements. |
| **3. Process nodes in reverse order** | Loop from the end of the array to the beginning. | Guarantees that inner (deepest) tags are replaced before their parents, preserving nesting semantics. |
| **4. Merge styles from ancestors** | While climbing up the parent chain we add any `B/I/U` styles we encounter. | Handles cases like `<b><i>text</i></b>` where the `<i>` element must become BOTH *bold* **and** *italic*. |
| **5. Create a `<span>` and set its `style`** | Convert the aggregated style object into a CSS string and assign it to `span.style.cssText`. | `<span>` is a neutral, inline container that can hold any CSS you like. |
| **6. Move all children to the `<span>`** | `while (el.firstChild) span.appendChild(el.firstChild);` | This preserves the original text **and** any other nested elements (e.g., an `<a>` link inside `<b>`). |
| **7. Replace the old element** | `el.parentNode.replaceChild(span, el);` | The original `<b>/<i>/<u>` disappears from the markup, leaving only the styled `<span>`. |
| **8. Call the function on any container** | In the demo we call it on `#demo`. | You can run it on `document.body`, or any specific part of the page you need to “clean up”. |

### Why Not Use a Simple Regex?

Parsing or modifying HTML with regular expressions is *dangerous* because:

* HTML can be arbitrarily nested.
* Tags may contain attributes, comments, or whitespace (`<b class="x">`).
* Regexes cannot reliably track hierarchy.

The DOM‑based approach above works on **real nodes**, respects nesting, and is future‑proof.

### Extending the Solution

* **Add more tags** – Just extend `TAG_STYLES` with entries like `STRONG: {fontWeight:'bold'}` or `EM: {fontStyle:'italic'}`.
* **Preserve existing inline styles** – If a formatting element already has a `style` attribute, merge it with the computed one:

  ```js
  const original = el.getAttribute('style');
  if (original) span.style.cssText += ';' + original;
  ```

* **Use a CSS class instead of inline style** – Create a class (`.bold {font-weight:bold;}`) and add it to the span (`span.classList.add('bold')`). This keeps markup cleaner and lets you change the styling centrally.

### Performance Tips

* For **large documents** you might want to **batch** the operation with `requestAnimationFrame` or `setTimeout` to avoid blocking UI.
* If you only need to *display* the cleaned‑up version and never edit it again, you could simply hide the original tags via CSS (`b,i,u {display:none;}`) and expose a parallel styled copy – but that leaves the semantic tags in the DOM, which is often undesirable for accessibility or SEO.

---

#### TL;DR

*Walk the DOM → gather `<b>/<i>/<u>` → compute cumulative CSS (bold, italic, underline) → replace each tag with a `<span>` that has those CSS rules applied.*  
The code above does exactly that, works on nested constructions, and leaves you with clean, style‑preserving markup. Happy coding!

