### Why each option matters

| Option | Why we need it |
|--------|----------------|
| `xhrFields: { responseType: 'blob' }` | Instructs the low‑level `XMLHttpRequest` to **not** try to decode the response as UTF‑8 text or JSON. It returns a `Blob` object that contains the raw MP3 bytes. |
| `cache: false` (or add a timestamp query string) | Browsers aggressively cache POST responses in some edge cases; turning caching off guarantees you get a fresh stream each click. |
| `URL.createObjectURL(blob)` | Turns the in‑memory blob into a *temporary* URL like `blob:https://example.com/abcd‑1234`. The `<audio>` element can load that just as if it were a regular file URL. |
| `URL.revokeObjectURL()` | Frees memory after playback; good practice if you’ll be loading many files in a session. |
| `audio.controls` / `audio.autoplay` | Optional UI — you may want custom controls, a styled button, etc. |

---

## 3️⃣  When you need **streaming** (progressive playback) instead of “download‑then‑play”

If the MP3 is **large** (e.g., > 5 MB) or you want playback to **start while the file is still downloading**, a single “blob‑once‑finished” approach can be a little sluggish.

Two mainstream ways:

### 3.1 Use the `<audio>` tag directly, let the browser stream it

```html
<audio controls src="/api/chat/getStream?token=XYZ"></audio>
```

*Pros* – no JavaScript needed, the browser handles buffering & seeking.  
*Cons* – you lose the ability to add custom POST data (GET only), and you can't do per‑request authentication that isn’t baked into the URL.

### 3.2 Use **Fetch + ReadableStream + MediaSource** (modern browsers only)

```js
async function streamMp3() {
    const response = await fetch('/api/chat/getStream', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({ /* payload */ })
    });

    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network error');

    const mediaSource = new MediaSource();
    const audio = document.createElement('audio');
    audio.controls = true;
    audio.src = URL.createObjectURL(mediaSource);
    document.body.appendChild(audio);

    mediaSource.addEventListener('sourceopen', async () => {
        const sourceBuffer = mediaSource.addSourceBuffer('audio/mpeg');

        const reader = response.body.getReader();
        let done, value;
        while ({ done, value } = await reader.read(), !done) {
            // value is a Uint8Array chunk of MP3 data
            sourceBuffer.appendBuffer(value);
            // Wait for the buffer to be ready for the next chunk
            await new Promise(resolve => {
                sourceBuffer.addEventListener('updateend', resolve, { once: true });
            });
        }

        // All chunks appended → signal end of stream
        mediaSource.endOfStream();
        audio.play();
    });
}
```

*Pros* – true streaming, works with POST, can add auth headers.  
*Cons* – requires **MediaSource Extensions (MSE)** support (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari ≥ 14). Complex error handling.

> **Tip:** In most cases the simple `blob`‑after‑download approach is enough. Only switch to MSE if you *must* start playback within < 1 second of the first byte.

