## TL;DR  

1. **Give the text a separate wrapper** (e.g. a `<span class="caption">…</span>`).  
2. **Leave the `<img>` alone** – it will stay visible.  
3. Hide the caption with CSS (`display:none`).  
4. Show it only when the mouse is over the container (`#ghostinfo:hover .caption {display:block;}`) **or** with a tiny bit of JS if you need more control.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready example that fits right into the code you already have.

---  

## 1. Change the `stationInfo` structure  

Wrap the *text* part of each entry in a `<span class="caption">…</span>`.  
The image stays as‑is, so it will always be shown.

```js
const stationInfo = {
  [strand]:
    `<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/f4cb3da3-e38f-4c57-8538-cd16160b85b3/ef1f442c-24bf-40c8-a82b-e89b71c66ecf_3000.jpg?v=1704458402437"
          onclick="this.requestFullscreen()"
          class="center"
          alt="View of the Grand Canal"
          width="400">
     <span class="caption">
       View of the Grand Canal: Santa Maria della Salute and the Dogana from Campo Santa Maria Zobenigo (c1743) by Bernardo Bellotto.
       <br><br>From the <a href='https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RJP#full-artwork-details'>Getty's Collection Online</a>.
     </span>`,
  [down]:
    `<img src="https://cdn.glitch.global/f4cb3da3-e38f-4c57-8538-cd16160b85b3/ef390b6c-6021-4738-92fe-2338c38cc2af_3000.jpg?v=1704459300215"
          onclick="this.requestFullscreen()"
          class="center"
          alt="A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité"
          width="400">
     <span class="caption">
       A View of Paris with the Ile de la Cité (c1763) by Jean‑Baptiste Raguenet.
       <br><br>From the <a href='https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RBA'>Getty's Collection Online</a>.
     </span>`
};
```

> **Why a `<span>`?**  
> A `<span>` is an inline element, so it won’t break the flow of the image.  
> You could also use a `<div>` if you prefer a block‑level caption; just change the CSS selector accordingly.

---

## 2. Add the CSS that hides/shows the caption  

Put this in your stylesheet (or inside a `<style>` tag in the HTML).

```css
/* 1. hide the caption by default */
#ghostinfo .caption {
  display: none;
}

/* 2. show it when the mouse is over #ghostinfo */
#ghostinfo:hover .caption {
  display: block;              /* or inline‑block if you kept it a span */
  margin-top: 0.5rem;          /* a little breathing room */
  background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); /* optional: make it slightly opaque */
  padding: 0.4rem;
}

/* (Optional) a nice transition */
#ghostinfo .caption {
  transition: opacity .2s ease-in-out;
}
#ghostinfo:hover .caption {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

**Result:**  
*The image appears immediately.*  
*When the user moves the cursor over the `#ghostinfo` div, the hidden `<span class="caption">` becomes visible.*

---

## 3. (Optional) Use a tiny JS hover handler instead of CSS  

If you want to do something extra on hover (e.g., change the cursor, log a message, or animate), you can attach listeners directly:

```js
const ghost = document.getElementById('ghostinfo');

ghost.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
  // find the caption inside the div (there will be exactly one)
  const cap = ghost.querySelector('.caption');
  if (cap) cap.style.display = 'block';
});

ghost.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
  const cap = ghost.querySelector('.caption');
  if (cap) cap.style.display = 'none';
});
```

> **When to prefer JS?**  
> Use JavaScript only if you need something **more** than “show/hide on hover” (e.g., lazy‑load the text, fetch additional data, animate height, etc.). For a plain show/hide effect, CSS is lighter and works even if JavaScript is disabled.

