## 📚 Why these particular checks?

| Feature (ES version) | Reason for inclusion                                                                                 |
|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `globalThis`        | The canonical way to get the global object (ES2020).                                                |
| `Promise.allSettled`, `Promise.any` | New Promise combinators (ES2020/ES2021).                              |
| `BigInt` & `123n`   | Literal and constructor – first class integers bigger than `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`.             |
| Optional chaining (`?.`) & nullish coalescing (`??`) | Very common ergonomics introduced in ES2020.                |
| Logical assignment (`&&=`, `||=`, `??=`) | Reduces boilerplate, ES2021.                                         |
| Numeric separators (`1_000_000`) | Improves readability for large numbers, ES2021.                     |
| Private class fields / methods (`#x`, `#inc`) | Immutable encapsulation, ES2022.                                  |
| Static class fields (`static foo = …`) | Consistent class syntax, ES2022.                                      |
| Top‑level `await` (tested inside an async arrow) | Enables await without an extra async function wrapper, ES2022.   |
| `Array.prototype.at` | Allows negative indexing & friendly iteration, ES2022.                                            |
| `String.prototype.replaceAll` | Better string replacement, ES2021.                                      |
| `Object.hasOwn` | Safer alternative to `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty`, ES2022.                                     |
| `Intl.*` family (Locale, ListFormat, RelativeTimeFormat, PluralRules) | Internationalisation got major upgrades in ES2020‑2022. |
| `WeakRef` & `FinalizationRegistry` | Advanced memory‑management primitives (ES2021). |
| `Atomics`/`SharedArrayBuffer` | Low‑level concurrency primitives (ES2017, still modern). |
| RegExp `/d/` flag (`/a/du`) | Gives match indices, a subtle feature added in ES2022.                                            |
| `matchAll` iterator protocol | Returns an iterator of matches, ES2020.                                                             |

If, for a specific project, you don’t need *all* of those, simply comment‑out (or remove) the entries you don’t care about. The function will still work – it only fails when a required check is missing.

---

## ⚙️ How it works under the hood

1. **Runtime checks** (`typeof … === …`) never throw and are *instantaneous*. They are the cheapest way to verify the existence of globals, prototype methods, or static helpers.

2. **Syntax checks** use `new Function(code)`.  
   - The `Function` constructor parses its argument **once**.  
   - If the parser encounters syntax it doesn't understand, it throws a `SyntaxError`.  
   - Because we never invoke the resulting function, there are **no side effects** (no network requests, no DOM changes, etc.).  

3. **Early‑exit** – The `for … of` loops stop as soon as a single test fails, which makes the utility fast even on very old browsers that miss the first few features.

---

## 🚀 Using it in the real world

* **Feature‑gate a polyfill bundle** – Load a hefty polyfill only when `supportsLatestJS()` returns `false`.

```html
<script>
  if (!supportsLatestJS()) {
    // Load your “old‑browser” bundle
    var s = document.createElement('script');
    s.src = 'polyfills.bundle.js';
    document.head.appendChild(s);
  }
</script>
```

* **Conditional API usage** – Guard newer APIs that you only want to call when they exist.

```js
if (supportsLatestJS()) {
  // Safe to use private class fields, top‑level await, etc.
  // (or just keep using them; this block will never run on a non‑supporting browser)
}
```

* **Analytics** – Record the proportion of users on “cutting‑edge” browsers.

```js
fetch('/analytics', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify({ modernBrowser: supportsLatestJS() })
});
```

---

## 📌 TL;DR

*The function `supportsLatestJS` above gives you a **one‑line boolean** that tells you whether the current environment implements **all modern ECMAScript features (up to ES‑2023)**. It uses pure feature detection – no user‑agent sniffing, no external libraries – and can be dropped into any project (plain script tag, module, Node, Deno, etc.).*

