## 3️⃣ PowerShell Chat Client (`client.ps1`)

PowerShell can use the .NET `System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket` class.  
The script below:

1. Connects to the server.
2. Starts a **background thread** (`Receive-Loop`) that prints any incoming messages.
3. Reads user input line‑by‑line and sends it to the server.
4. Handles clean exit (`Ctrl+C`).

### 3.1 Create `client.ps1`

```powershell
# client.ps1
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# Simple PowerShell WebSocket chat client
# ----------------------------------------------------------

param(
    [string]$Uri = "ws://localhost:8080"
)

# Make sure we are running on PowerShell 7+ (or Windows PowerShell 5+)
if ($PSVersionTable.PSVersion.Major -lt 5) {
    Write-Error "PowerShell version 5 or later is required."
    exit 1
}

Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Net.WebSockets.Client

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Helper: async receive loop
function Receive-Loop {
    param(
        [System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket]$Socket
    )
    $buffer = New-Object 'Byte[]' 4096
    while ($Socket.State -eq [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketState]::Open) {
        try {
            $result = $Socket.ReceiveAsync($buffer, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).GetAwaiter().GetResult()
            if ($result.MessageType -eq [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketMessageType]::Close) {
                break
            }
            $msgBytes = $buffer[0..($result.Count - 1)]
            $msg = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString($msgBytes)
            Write-Host $msg
        } catch {
            Write-Error "Receive error: $_"
            break
        }
    }
}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Main
try {
    $socket = [System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket]::new()
    Write-Host "Connecting to $Uri ..."
    $socket.ConnectAsync($Uri, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).GetAwaiter().GetResult()
    Write-Host "✅ Connected! Type your messages below. Press Ctrl+C to quit."

    # Start receiving in the background
    $receiveJob = Start-Job -ScriptBlock { param($s) Receive-Loop -Socket $s } -ArgumentList $socket

    # Capture Ctrl+C so we can shut down gracefully
    $null = Register-EngineEvent PowerShell.Exiting -Action {
        Write-Host "`n🛑 Exiting…"
        if ($socket.State -eq [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketState]::Open) {
            $socket.CloseAsync([System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketCloseStatus]::NormalClosure, "Client exit", [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).GetAwaiter().GetResult()
        }
    }

    # Read user input line‑by‑line and send
    while ($true) {
        $line = Read-Host
        if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($line)) { continue }

        $bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($line)
        $segment = [System.ArraySegment[byte]]::new($bytes)
        $socket.SendAsync($segment, [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketMessageType]::Text, $true, [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).GetAwaiter().GetResult()
    }
}
catch {
    Write-Error "❌ $($_.Exception.Message)"
}
finally {
    # Clean up
    if ($socket -and $socket.State -eq [System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketState]::Open) {
        $socket.CloseAsync([System.Net.WebSockets.WebSocketCloseStatus]::NormalClosure, "Bye", [System.Threading.CancellationToken]::None).GetAwaiter().GetResult()
    }

    if ($receiveJob) { Stop-Job $receiveJob; Remove-Job $receiveJob }
}
```

### 3.2 Run the client

Open a **new PowerShell window** (you can open many of them for multiple participants) and run:

```powershell
