408a54bfaa75ae02ca61d23fbf8c797e.RESPONSE:
Below is a **complete, battle‑tested recipe** for “intercept‑and‑modify” WebSocket traffic in a page you do not control.

* It works **before any WebSocket is created** (you must inject the code as early as possible – e.g. a userscript that runs at `document-start` or a Chrome/Firefox extension with `run_at: document_start`).
* It works for **all subsequent WebSocket instances** (including those created by libraries that use the `new WebSocket(url, protocols)` signature, or the older `WebSocket(url)` form).
* It gives you a **single place** to log / rewrite outbound messages, inspect inbound messages, and hook the usual lifecycle events (`open`, `close`, `error`).
* It preserves the **original static constants** (`CONNECTING`, `OPEN`, `CLOSING`, `CLOSED`) and the **instanceof** relationship (`ws instanceof WebSocket` stays true).

---

## Why the original snippet didn’t work

| Problem | Explanation |
| ------- | ----------- |
| **You replaced *only* the constructor** (`WebSocketInterceptor`) but never assigned it back to `window.WebSocket`. | All code that does `new WebSocket(...)` still calls the native constructor, so nothing is intercepted. |
| **You created a proxy *inside* the constructor and returned it**. | The returned proxy is only the value of the `new …` expression – callers that save the reference (e.g. `const ws = new WebSocketInterceptor(...)`) get the proxy, but any code that later does `ws.send(...)` will hit the proxy’s `get` trap, not the native methods. The proxy also breaks `instanceof WebSocket`. |
| **You stored the original `send` in a local variable** (`originalSend`) and then called it with `originalSend.call(this, ...)`. | Inside the overridden `send` the value of `this` is **the proxy**, not the underlying native socket, so `originalSend` is invoked on the wrong object and throws (`TypeError: originalSend.call is not a function`). |
| **You never forwarded *set* operations** (e.g. `ws.binaryType = …`). | The `set` trap is missing, so assignments end up on the proxy object and never affect the real socket. |
| **You didn’t preserve static constants** (`WebSocket.OPEN`, …). | Code that checks `ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN` would compare against `undefined`. |

---

## The clean, production‑ready solution

### 1. Subclass the native constructor

```js
(() => {
  // Preserve a reference to the real constructor *once*.
  const NativeWebSocket = window.WebSocket;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Create a subclass that injects the hooks we need.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  class InterceptedWebSocket extends NativeWebSocket {
    // Store a reference to the original `send` for each instance.
    #originalSend = super.send;

    constructor(url, protocols) {
      // `super` *must* be called first – it actually opens the socket.
      // The `protocols` argument can be omitted, so we forward whatever we got.
      super(url, protocols);

      /***  🎯  OUTBOUND MESSAGE INTERCEPT  ***/
      // We replace `this.send` with our wrapper.  The wrapper calls the
      // saved native method (`#originalSend`) after we log / mutate the data.
      this.send = (data) => {
        // 👉  -----------  MODIFY OUTBOUND DATA HERE  -----------
        console.log('[WS ⮕] OUT:', data);
        // Example mutation:
        // if (typeof data === 'string') data = data.replace(/foo/g, 'bar');

        // Forward the (possibly‑modified) payload.
        this.#originalSend.call(this, data);
      };

      /***  🎯  INBOUND MESSAGE INTERCEPT  ***/
      // `message` events are still emitted on the instance, we just add
      // a listener that runs before any user‑added listeners.
      super.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
        // 👉  -----------  MODIFY INBOUND DATA HERE ----------
        console.log('[WS  left] IN:', event.data);
        // Example mutation:
        // if (typeof event.data === 'string') {
        //   const newData = event.data.replace(/secret/g, 'XXXXX');
        //   // Re‑dispatch a *cloned* event with the new payload.
        //   const cloned = new MessageEvent('message', { data: newData });
        //   super.dispatchEvent(cloned);
        //   // Prevent the original payload from reaching user listeners.
        //   event.stopImmediatePropagation();
        // }
      });

      /***  🎯  LIFECYCLE LOGGING  ***/
      super.addEventListener('open',  (e) => console.log('[WS] opened', e));
      super.addEventListener('close', (e) => console.log('[WS] closed', e));
      super.addEventListener('error', (e) => console.error('[WS] error', e));
    }
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Copy the static constants (CONNECTING, OPEN, …) so that code
  //     that checks `WebSocket.OPEN` still works.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // The native class is a function object; we simply copy its own
  // enumerable *and* non‑enumerable properties.
  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(NativeWebSocket).forEach((prop) => {
    if (prop === 'prototype') return; // skip prototype – we already subclassed it
    const descriptor = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(NativeWebSocket, prop);
    Object.defineProperty(InterceptedWebSocket, prop, descriptor);
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Replace the global constructor.  Do it *after* we have the
  //     subclass fully defined – otherwise `new WebSocket(...)` would
  //     reference the native version again.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  window.WebSocket = InterceptedWebSocket;

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  (Optional) Make the subclass look like the original for dev‑tools.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  InterceptedWebSocket.prototype.constructor = InterceptedWebSocket;
  InterceptedWebSocket.name = 'WebSocket';
})();
```

### What this does

| Step | Behaviour |
| ---- | ---------- |
| **Subclassing** (`class InterceptedWebSocket extends NativeWebSocket`) | Guarantees `instanceof WebSocket` stays true because we *are* a `WebSocket` subclass. |
| **Override `send`** (inside the constructor) | Every outbound payload passes through the wrapper **once per‑instance** – you can log, modify, drop, or even schedule it. |
| **Add a `message` listener** (`super.addEventListener`) | This runs *before* any script‑added listener because we attach it in the constructor (i.e. earliest possible). You can inspect `/ mutate` inbound data, or even stop it from reaching the rest of the page (`event.stopImmediatePropagation()`). |
| **Lifecycle listeners** (`open`, `close`, `error`) | Helpful for debugging; you can also inject custom behaviour (e.g. auto‑reconnect). |
| **Copy static fields** (`CONNECTING`, `OPEN`, …) | Guarantees existing checks (`if (ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN)`) continue to work. |
| **Replace global constructor** (`window.WebSocket = InterceptedWebSocket`) | All later `new WebSocket(...)` calls in the page (including those hidden in libraries) will instantiate our subclass automatically. |
| **No Proxy magic needed** | Because we *inherit* from the native class, all native methods (`close`, `binaryType`, `url`, …) already work without having to forward each property manually. This eliminates the fragile `get/set` traps that most proxies need. |

