🟢 client open                        ← user‑code listener
[WS ⮕] OUT: hello world               ← our wrapper logs outbound payload
[WS  left] IN: hello world            ← interceptor logs inbound payload
🟣 client got: hello world            ← user‑code receives the (unchanged) message
```

If you want to **rewrite** the inbound payload, uncomment the mutation block in the `message` listener. Example: replace a secret token before it reaches the page.

---

## 6️⃣  Intercepting *already‑opened* sockets (when you cannot run code early)

If you **miss the window** and the page has already called `new WebSocket(...)` before you inject your script, you can still monkey‑patch the **prototype**:

```js
(() => {
  const nativeSend = WebSocket.prototype.send;
  WebSocket.prototype.send = function (data) {
    console.log('[WS (late) ⮕] OUT:', data);
    // mutate if you like …
    return nativeSend.call(this, data);
  };

  const nativeAddEventListener = WebSocket.prototype.addEventListener;
  WebSocket.prototype.addEventListener = function (type, listener, options) {
    if (type === 'message') {
      // wrap the user listener so we can see the event first
      const wrapped = (event) => {
        console.log('[WS (late)  left] IN:', event.data);
        // modify event.data if needed, then forward
        listener.call(this, event);
      };
      return nativeAddEventListener.call(this, type, wrapped, options);
    }
    // for other event types just forward unchanged
    return nativeAddEventListener.call(this, type, listener, options);
  };
})();
```

*This technique works **after the socket exists**, but it **does not give you a chance to intercept the handshake** or any messages that have already flown before your code runs.*

---

## 7️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Pitfall | Why it happens | Fix |
| ------- | -------------- | ---- |
| **`ws.send` is undefined** after you replace `window.WebSocket`. | The code that creates the socket used the *original* constructor (e.g. `var WS = window.WebSocket; new WS(...)`) **before** your injection ran. | Make sure your snippet runs **as early as possible** (e.g. userscript `@run-at document-start`). |
| **`instanceof WebSocket` returns `false`.** | You returned a plain `Proxy` from a constructor, breaking the prototype chain. | Subclass instead of proxy; the subclass inherits the native prototype. |
| **`ws.binaryType = "arraybuffer"` has no effect** | The `set` trap of a Proxy was missing, so the assignment landed on the proxy object. | Use subclassing (no `set` needed) or implement a `set` trap that forwards to `target.socket`. |
| **Static constants are `undefined`.** | You overwrote `window.WebSocket` with a plain function that has no `OPEN`, `CLOSING`, …| Copy those constants from the native constructor as shown in the snippet. |
| **`send` wrapper throws `TypeError: ... is not a function`.** | You called `originalSend.call(this, …)` where `this` is the proxy, not the underlying native socket. | Store the original method **on the instance** (`this.#originalSend = super.send`) and use it with `this.#originalSend.call(this, …)`. |
| **Incoming messages are double‑logged** (once by your interceptor, once by page code) and you want to **suppress** them. | You only `console.log`ed the event, you didn’t stop propagation. | In the `message` listener, call `event.stopImmediatePropagation()` after you have processed the event. Then optionally dispatch a **new** `MessageEvent` with the (possibly) altered data. |

---

## 8️⃣  TL;DR – “Copy‑paste ready” one‑liner

If you just need the **basic logging** version (no payload mutation) and you can inject **before any `WebSocket` is created**, paste this into a `<script>` tag or a userscript and you’re done:

```js
(() => {
  const NativeWS = window.WebSocket;
  class InterceptedWS extends NativeWS {
    constructor(url, protocols) {
      super(url, protocols);
      this.send = (d) => { console.log('[WS →]', d); super.send(d); };
      super.addEventListener('message', e => console.log('[WS ←]', e.data));
      super.addEventListener('open',   e => console.log('[WS] open', e));
      super.addEventListener('close',  e => console.log('[WS] close', e));
      super.addEventListener('error',  e => console.error('[WS] error', e));
    }
  }
  Object.getOwnPropertyNames(NativeWS).forEach(p => {
    if (p !== 'prototype') Object.defineProperty(InterceptedWS, p,
      Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(NativeWS, p));
  });
  window.WebSocket = InterceptedWS;
})();
```

That single self‑executing function gives you full‑blown interception with **zero further changes** to the page.

---

### Bottom line

* **Inject early** → replace `window.WebSocket` with a subclass.
* **Never use a `Proxy` inside the constructor** unless you really need to trap *every* property; subclassing already gives you all native methods.
* **Store the original `send`** (or any other method you override) on the instance, not in a closure, to avoid `this`‑binding bugs.
* **Copy the static fields** to keep compatibility with existing code.
* If you cannot inject early, **fallback to prototype monkey‑patching**, but you won’t be able to see the handshake or any messages that already flew.

Feel free to drop any of the optional mutation snippets into the `send`/`message` handlers to suit your exact use‑case (scrubbing passwords, injecting auth tokens, etc.). Happy hacking! 🚀

