## Short answer  
Yes – but **only in two ways**:

| Method | What you can change | Browser support | How it works |
|--------|--------------------|-----------------|--------------|
| **`accent‑color` CSS property** | The fill colour of the native radio‑button *and* the check‑mark of a checkbox. | Chrome 93+, Edge 93+, Firefox 92+, Safari 15+ (iOS 15+). | You keep the native control and tell the browser “paint it with this colour”. |
| **Custom UI (hide the native control and draw your own)** | Anything you like – shape, size, colour, gradients, icons, animation, etc. | All browsers (including older ones). | You visually replace the `<input type="radio">` with a styled pseudo‑element (usually on a `<label>`). The real `<input>` stays in the DOM so it still receives focus, keyboard events, and form data. |

Below you’ll find a **complete, self‑contained example** that shows both approaches side‑by‑side, explains the pitfalls, and gives you a solid starting point for production use.

---

## 1️⃣  Using the native `accent-color` property  

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Radio buttons – accent‑color</title>
<style>
  body {font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; padding: 2rem;}

  /* -----------------------------------------------------------
     1️⃣  Simple native styling – just change the accent colour.
     ----------------------------------------------------------- */
  .native-radio input[type="radio"] {
    accent-color: teal;          /* any CSS colour value */
    margin-right: .4rem;
    width: 1.2rem; height: 1.2rem;  /* optional – makes the circle a bit bigger */
  }

  /* Show a different colour for the :focus state (optional) */
  .native-radio input[type="radio"]:focus {
    outline: 2px solid rgba(0,128,128,.4);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>📌 Native radio – `accent-color`</h2>
<div class="native-radio">
  <label><input type="radio" name="color1" value="a"> Red</label><br>
  <label><input type="radio" name="color1" value="b"> Green</label><br>
  <label><input type="radio" name="color1" value="c"> Blue</label>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

### What you get  

* The **filled circle** (the “dot”) is painted `teal`.  
* The **border** of the circle still follows the OS/theme.  
* The element stays fully accessible (keyboard focus, screen‑reader label, form submission).  

### When to use it  

* You need a quick colour tweak and **don’t need a totally custom look**.  
* Your target audience uses relatively recent browsers (the property landed in 2022‑2023).  

### Limitations  

* Only the **accent** colour can be changed – you **cannot** change the size of the inner dot, the border thickness, or replace the circle with a different shape.  
* Older browsers (IE, older Safari, very old Android browsers) ignore the rule and fall back to the default OS style.

---

## 2️⃣  Building a fully custom radio button  

If you want full design freedom (e.g. a larger circle, a gradient, a check‑icon, a bounce animation, etc.) you need to **hide the native control** and draw a replacement with CSS. The pattern is:

1. Keep the `<input type="radio">` (so the form works and accessibility stays intact).  
2. Visually hide it (`opacity:0` + `position:absolute` + `pointer-events:none`).  
3. Use the **adjacent sibling selector** (`input:checked + label::before`) or **`label::before`/`label::after`** to draw the UI.  
4. Keep the `<label>` bound to the `<input>` via the `for` attribute (or wrap the input inside the label).

### Full example – custom radio buttons  

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Radio buttons – custom CSS UI</title>
<style>
  :root {
    /* Define your own colour palette once */
    --primary:   #0066ff;
    --secondary:#ff6600;
    --bg:        #f9f9f9;
    --border:    #bbb;
  }

  body {font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; background: var(--bg); padding:2rem;}

  /* -----------------------------------------------------------
     2️⃣  Custom radio UI – hide native control, style the label.
     ----------------------------------------------------------- */
  .custom-radio {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: .8rem;
  }

  /* hide the native input – but keep it in the flow for screen‑readers */
  .custom-radio input[type="radio"] {
    position: absolute;   /* remove from layout */
    opacity: 0;           /* hide visually */
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
  }

  /* the label becomes the visible button */
  .custom-radio label {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
    /* spacing for the pseudo‑element */
    padding-left: 2.2rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  /* ── the empty circle (unchecked state) ── */
  .custom-radio label::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    width: 1.6rem;
    height: 1.6rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #fff;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    transition: border-color .2s, background .2s;
  }

  /* ── the inner dot – appears only when checked ── */
  .custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + label::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0.4rem; top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%) scale(0);
    width: 0.8rem;
    height: 0.8rem;
    background: var(--primary);
    border-radius: 50%;
    transition: transform .2s ease-out;
  }

  /* animate the dot when the radio becomes checked */
  .custom-radio input[type="radio"]:checked + label::after {
    transform: translateY(-50%) scale(1);
  }

  /* ---- focus style (accessibility) ---- */
  .custom-radio input[type="radio"]:focus-visible + label::before {
    outline: 2px solid var(--primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* ---- hover (optional) ---- */
  .custom-radio label:hover::before {
    border-color: var(--primary);
  }

  /* ---- variant: secondary colour (just add a class) ---- */
  .custom-radio .secondary input[type="radio"]:checked + label::after {
    background: var(--secondary);
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>📌 Completely custom radio buttons</h2>

<div class="custom-radio">
  <!-- First group – primary colour -->
  <input type="radio" id="opt-a" name="group1" value="a">
  <label for="opt-a">Option A</label>

  <input type="radio" id="opt-b" name="group1" value="b">
  <label for="opt-b">Option B</label>

  <input type="radio" id="opt-c" name="group1" value="c">
  <label for="opt-c">Option C</label>

  <!-- Second group – secondary colour (illustrates how you can vary the colour) -->
  <input type="radio" id="opt-d" name="group2" value="d" class="secondary">
  <label for="opt-d">Red theme</label>

  <input type="radio" id="opt-e" name="group2" value="e" class="secondary">
  <label for="opt-e">Green theme</label>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works, step by step  

| Step | Code | What it does |
|------|------|--------------|
| **1. Keep the `<input>`** | `<input type="radio" id="opt-a" name="group1">` | The browser still tracks the checked state and includes the value when the form is submitted. |
| **2. Visually hide the input** | `position:absolute; opacity:0; width:0; height:0;` | The element is still focusable (via `tab` key) and screen‑readers can announce it, but the user never sees the default UI. |
| **3. Use a `<label>`** | `<label for="opt-a">Option A</label>` | Clicking the label toggles the hidden radio because the `for` attribute points to the input’s `id`. |
| **4. Draw the “outer circle”** | `label::before` | A 1.6 rem circle with a border – this is the static part of the UI. |
| **5. Draw the “dot” only when checked** | `input:checked + label::after` | The `+` sibling selector means “the label that immediately follows a checked radio”. The dot scales from 0 → 1 to create a smooth pop‑in animation. |
| **6. Add focus styles** | `input:focus-visible + label::before` | Gives a visible outline when the user navigates with keyboard, satisfying WCAG 2.1 focus‑visible requirement. |
| **7. Optional colour variants** | Adding a class (`.secondary`) on the input (or label) and targeting it in CSS lets you swap the inner‑dot colour per group. | Demonstrates how you can build a theme system without duplicating the entire rule set. |

#### Why the **adjacent sibling** (`+`) selector and not a descendant?  

* Because the `<label>` **must be after** the `<input>` in the DOM for the `+` to work.  
* This order also guarantees that the label’s `for` attribute is *not* strictly required (the label can wrap the input), but keeping them separate makes the selector easier to read.  

If you prefer to **wrap the input inside the label**, you can still style it:

```html
<label class="custom">
  <input type="radio" name="g" value="1">
  Option 1
</label>
```

and the CSS changes to:

```css
.custom input[type="radio"]:checked + .custom::after { … }
```

but the adjacent‑sibling approach (`input + label`) is the most common because it separates the clickable area (label) from the hidden input in a clean way.

---

## 3️⃣  When to pick which technique  

| Use case | Recommended technique |
|----------|------------------------|
| **Simple colour change, modern browsers only** | `accent-color` (few lines, native). |
| **Need to support Internet Explorer / very old Android** | Custom UI (the hidden‑input + pseudo‑elements approach). |
| **You want a non‑circular shape (square, star, custom SVG icon)** | Custom UI. |
| **You need a larger hit‑area or want a “ripple” animation** | Custom UI (you control the `::before`/`::after` layer). |
| **You need a dark‑mode override without rewriting whole component** | `accent-color` (just change a CSS variable). |
| **You already have a design‑system that uses CSS custom properties for colours** | Both work – you can set a variable (`--radio-color`) and use it with either `accent-color` **or** your custom UI. |

---

## 4️⃣  Accessibility checklist  

Even though you’re “faking” the radio button, you **must** preserve these ARIA and interaction fundamentals:

| Checklist item | How you satisfy it |
|----------------|--------------------|
| **Keyboard operable (`Tab` → focus, `Space` → toggle)** | Keep the native `<input>` in the DOM. The hidden input receives focus automatically because it is still tabbable (`opacity:0` does **not** remove it from tab order). |
| **Screen‑reader label** | Either wrap the input in a `<label>` **or** associate it via `for="id"` and `id="..."`. The label text is read aloud. |
| **Focus indicator** | Add a visible `outline` or `box‑shadow` on the `input:focus-visible + label::before`. The default focus ring disappears once you hide the input, so you must provide a custom one. |
| **Hover/focus contrast** | Ensure the colour of the custom UI (border, dot) meets WCAG 2.1 AA contrast ratios against the background. |
| **State announcement** | The browser already announces `selected`/`checked` because the real `<input>` is present. No extra `aria-checked` needed. |
| **Form submission** | The value of the checked `<input>` is submitted with the form – the hidden input still participates. |
| **Responsive tap‑target** | A minimum of **44 × 44 px** (or 48 × 48 dp on Android) is recommended. In the example the `label` is `inline‑flex` with a left padding that creates a generous tap area even though the visible circle is smaller. |

---

## 5️⃣  Bonus: One‑line colour swap using CSS variables  

If you want a **single CSS custom property** to drive the colour for both native (`accent-color`) *and* custom UI, you can do:

```css
:root {
  --radio-accent: #d9534f;   /* bootstrap‑like red */
}

/* Native */
input[type="radio"].native { accent-color: var(--radio-accent); }

/* Custom */
.custom-radio label::after { background: var(--radio-accent); }

/* Optional — change the border in unchecked state */
.custom-radio label::before { border-color: var(--radio-accent); }
```

Now you only have to change `--radio-accent` in one place (perhaps from a theme switcher) and every radio on the page updates instantly.

---

## 6️⃣  TL;DR – The practical copy‑paste answer  

```css
