---

## 2️⃣ Signalling Server (tiny WebSocket – 30 lines of Node.js)

WebRTC itself **does not transport signalling**; you need any bidirectional channel (WebSocket, HTTP‑long‑poll, MQTT, …). Here’s a minimal **Node.js** server you can start with `npm i ws` and run on the same machine.

```js
// signalling_server.js -------------------------------------------------------
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

let peers = [];

wss.on('connection', ws => {
  peers.push(ws);
  console.log('Peer connected, total:', peers.length);

  ws.on('message', data => {
    // Broadcast every message to the *other* peer(s)
    peers.forEach(p => {
      if (p !== ws && p.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
        p.send(data);
      }
    });
  });

  ws.on('close', () => {
    peers = peers.filter(p => p !== ws);
    console.log('Peer left, total:', peers.length);
  });
});

console.log('🚀 Signalling server listening on ws://0.0.0.0:8080');
```

**How to use it from the C++ client**

Add a tiny wrapper (uses `libwebsockets` or `Boost.Beast`). The simplest is to **use the already‑bundled `rtc::WebSocketChannel`** that lives in `webrtc/api/` (it mirrors the same API as the JavaScript WebSocket). The code sketch to replace the console `stdin/stdout` in the skeleton:

```cpp
// ... inside SimpleSignaling constructor:
websocket_ = std::make_unique<rtc::WebSocketChannel>(signaling_thread, "ws://your.host:8080");

// When you have a local description ready:
std::string local_sdp; desc->ToString(&local_sdp);
websocket_->Send(rtc::CopyOnWriteBuffer(local_sdp));

// When remote message arrives:
void OnMessage(const rtc::CopyOnWriteBuffer& buffer) override {
    std::string remote_sdp(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(buffer.data()), buffer.size());
    // parse and set remote description as before …
}
```

That’s all – the **signalling server simply shuffles SDP and ICE candidates** between the two peers, and the WebRTC stack does the heavy lifting (NAT traversal, encryption, video encoding/decoding).

---

## 3️⃣ Quick‑look at the *alternatives* (if you decide not to use Google’s WebRTC)

| # | Library | Why you might pick it | Build notes (Ubuntu) |
|---|---------|----------------------|----------------------|
| **OpenWebRTC** | More “C++‑ish” API, supports **OpenGL‑ES** rendering, better on low‑power devices (Raspberry Pi). | If you need a **lightweight footprint** (<10 MB) and a sample Qt UI. | `cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release .. && make -j$(nproc)` after installing `libgstreamer-plugins-bad1.0-dev` |
| **Mediastreamer2** | Part of the **Linphone** ecosystem; ships a **`fake_video`** demo that can be turned into a P2P client. | If you already use the **SIP** stack of Linphone and want to add video‑chat without pulling a massive WebRTC engine. | `./configure && make && sudo make install` (autotools). |
| **GStreamer + webrtc plugin** | You code a **pipeline** like `v4l2src ! videoconvert ! vp8enc ! webrtcbin` – all in **C**. | If you already use GStreamer for other media processing, you get zero‑copy pipelines and can embed **RTSP**, **MPEG‑TS** etc. | `meson build && ninja -C build` (needs `gst-plugins-bad` & `gst-plugins-good`). |
| **pion‑webrtc‑c** | Very thin C wrapper around a **Go** native library – good if your product already embeds Go for other services. | Small code footprint, easy to compile with `gcc`. | `go build -buildmode=c-archive ./...` then link `-lwebrtc` in your C project. |

All of those can be compiled on Ubuntu with the same system packages listed above; you only need to change the *package list* for GStreamer (`libgstreamer1.0-dev`, `libgstreamer-plugins-base1.0-dev`, `gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad`) or for Linphone (`liblinphone-dev`).

---

## 4️⃣ Checklist – “Is it ready for production?”

| ✅ Feature | WebRTC native | OpenWebRTC | Mediastreamer2 | GStreamer‑webrtc |
|-----------|---------------|------------|----------------|-------------------|
| **End‑to‑end encryption (DTLS‑SRTP)** | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ (via libsrtp) | ✔ (via webrtcbin) |
| **Adaptive bitrate / congestion control** | ✔ (Google Congestion Control) | ✔ | ✖ (no built‑in) | ✔ (via webrtcbin) |
| **H.264, VP8, VP9 hardware‑accel** | ✔ (via `openh264` & `libvpx`; optional VAAPI) | ✔ | ✖ (only software) | ✔ (via `vp8enc`/`vaapi`) |
| **Multi‑party (SFU) support** | Requires external SFU (e.g. Janus, Jitsi‑Videobridge) | Same | Same | Same |
| **Cross‑platform (Android/iOS/Windows/Mac)** | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ (via Linphone) | ✔ (via GStreamer) |
| **License** | BSD‑3 (WebRTC) | BSD‑3 | GPLv2 (Linphone) | LGPL‑2.1 (GStreamer) |
| **Community size / docs** | Very large, many tutorials | Small but well‑documented | Moderate (VoIP focus) | Large (GStreamer docs) |

If you only need **two‑way video** and you’re comfortable running a tiny Node/WebSocket signalling service, **WebRTC native C++** is the winner: it gives you the *full* feature set, a **single binary** (no external runtime), and you can ship it on any Ubuntu machine—embedded board or desktop.

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – One‑click “starter” for Ubuntu

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install deps
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y build-essential git python3 python3-pip ninja-build \
    libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libvpx-dev libopus-dev libavcodec-dev \
    libavformat-dev libavutil-dev libyuv-dev libgstreamer1.0-dev \
    gstreamer1.0-plugins-base gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad

# 2️⃣ Pull WebRTC + build tools
git clone https://github.com/webrtc/webrtc.git
cd webrtc
git clone https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/tools/depot_tools.git
export PATH=$PWD/depot_tools:$PATH
./tools_webrtc/install-build-deps.sh
gclient sync

# 3️⃣ Build the console demo (debug)
gn gen out/debug "--args=target_os=\"linux\" target_cpu=\"x64\" is_debug=true"
ninja -C out/debug peerconnection_client

# 4️⃣ Run two terminals – copy‑paste SDP
./out/debug/peerconnection_client --peer=0   # caller
./out/debug/peerconnection_client --peer=1   # callee
```

That’s it – you now have a **functional P2P video‑conference** client compiled from pure C++ source on Ubuntu. Replace the `stdin/stdout` signalling with the 30‑line Node.js WebSocket server (or any other transport) and you’re ready to embed the code into a GUI (Qt/GTK) or a headless daemon for IoT cameras.

---

### 🎉 Bonus: Where to go next

| Goal | Recommended next step |
|------|-----------------------|
| **Add a UI** | Use **Qt5/Qt6** (`QVideoWidget`) and feed frames from the `VideoTrack` via `webrtc::VideoSinkInterface`. |
| **Multi‑party (SFU)** | Deploy **Janus** or **Jitsi‑Videobridge** and have each native client connect as a WebRTC peer to the SFU. |
| **Screen‑share** | Replace the webcam source with a `DesktopCapturer` (`webrtc::DesktopCaptureModule`) – already part of the repo. |
| **Low‑latency (sub‑100 ms)** | Enable **hardware‑accelerated VP8/VP9** (`libvpx` with `--enable-vp9-highbitdepth`) and tweak `RTCOfferAnswerOptions` (`iceRestart`, `maxBitrate`). |
| **Run on a Raspberry Pi** | Build with **`target_cpu="arm"`** and add **VA‑API** (`sudo apt install libva-dev`) – WebRTC works fine on Pi 4 with a cheap USB webcam. |

Feel free to ask follow‑up questions about any of the alternative stacks, how to integrate the code into a Qt application, or how to set up a TURN server (e.g. **coturn**) for NAT‑unfriendly environments. Happy coding!

