## TL;DR  

Yes – you can show a live preview of **any** element that lives on another page, but you have to pull that content into the current page first.  
Typical ways to do it are:

| Method | How it works | When it’s a good fit | Quick code sketch |
|--------|--------------|----------------------|-------------------|
| **Hidden `<iframe>`** | Load the other page in an off‑screen iframe, clone the element you need, and inject it into a tooltip/pop‑over. | Same‑origin pages, you need the *live* DOM (scripts, styles, interactions). | See below. |
| **AJAX  (`fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest`) + DOMParser** | Request the HTML of the other page, parse it, pull the element out, and inject it. | Same‑origin or CORS‑enabled pages, you only need the markup (no heavy scripts). | See below. |
| **jQuery `.load(selector)`** | Shortcut for the AJAX‑parse‑inject pattern. | Small projects or when you already use jQuery. | See below. |
| **Server‑side snapshot (image or JSON)** | Render the target element on the server (e.g., a screenshot or a JSON of its data) and serve that as a static asset. | Cross‑origin pages, or when you want a lightweight “static preview”. | See below. |
| **CSS `content: url(...)`** | Use a pre‑generated image (e.g., a screenshot) as the tooltip background. | When the preview is simply an image, not interactive. | See below. |

Below you’ll find a deeper dive into each technique, the pitfalls to avoid, and ready‑to‑copy code snippets.

---

## 1. Understanding the constraints

| Constraint | Why it matters | How to handle it |
|------------|----------------|------------------|
| **Same‑origin policy** | Browsers block JS from reading content from a different domain. | *Same domain* → everything works. <br> *Different domain* → you need CORS headers from the target or a server‑side proxy. |
| **Performance** | Hover previews should feel instant; loading a whole page each time hurts UX. | Pre‑fetch on page load or on first hover, cache the result in memory. |
| **Styling/Script dependencies** | The preview may rely on CSS or JS that lives on the other page. | Pull the relevant CSS into the preview container, or clone the whole `<head>` from the iframe. |
| **Security** | Injected HTML can contain scripts that run in your page’s context. | Sanitize if you’re pulling *untrusted* content; or isolate the preview in an `<iframe>` sandbox. |
| **Responsiveness** | The preview may need to resize to fit a tooltip. | Use CSS `max-width`, `max-height`, and `object-fit: contain` for images; for HTML clone, set a fixed size and `overflow:auto`. |

---

## 2. Solution #1 – Hidden `<iframe>` (live DOM)

### When to use it
* You need the **exact same interactive element** (including its JS behavior) as on the source page.
* Both pages are on the **same origin**, or the source page allows being embedded (`X‑Frame‑Options: SAMEORIGIN` or `ALLOW‑FROM`).

### How it works
1. Put an off‑screen (or `display:none`) iframe that loads the target page.
2. When the user hovers the trigger element, clone the desired node from the iframe’s document.
3. Insert that clone into a tooltip/pop‑over that you render on the current page.

### Minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Hover preview with iframe</title>
  <style>
    /* The tooltip container */
    .preview {
      position: absolute;
      z-index: 1000;
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #ddd;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.15);
      padding: 5px;
      max-width: 300px;
      max-height: 200px;
      overflow: auto;
      display: none; /* hidden by default */
    }
    /* visually hide the iframe but keep it in DOM */
    #preview-iframe {
      position: absolute;
      width: 0;
      height: 0;
      border: 0;
      visibility: hidden; /* hide from screen readers */
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- The element you hover on -->
<a href="#" class="trigger" data-preview-id="#product-42">
  Look at product 42
</a>

<!-- Tooltip placeholder -->
<div id="preview-tooltip" class="preview"></div>

<!-- Hidden iframe that loads the other page -->
<iframe id="preview-iframe" src="/products.html"></iframe>

<script>
  const iframe = document.getElementById('preview-iframe');
  const tooltip = document.getElementById('preview-tooltip');

  // Wait until the iframe has fully loaded its document
  iframe.addEventListener('load', () => {
    // Attach hover listeners AFTER iframe is ready
    document.querySelectorAll('.trigger').forEach(el => {
      const selector = el.dataset.previewId; // e.g. "#product-42"

      el.addEventListener('mouseenter', (e) => {
        // Get the element from the iframe's document
        const sourceEl = iframe.contentDocument.querySelector(selector);
        if (!sourceEl) return;

        // Clone it (deep clone copies children)
        const clone = sourceEl.cloneNode(true);

        // Optionally, strip IDs to avoid duplicate IDs in the page
        clone.removeAttribute('id');

        // Insert into tooltip
        tooltip.innerHTML = ''; // clear any old content
        tooltip.appendChild(clone);

        // Position tooltip near cursor
        const rect = e.target.getBoundingClientRect();
        tooltip.style.top = `${rect.bottom + window.scrollY + 4}px`;
        tooltip.style.left = `${rect.left + window.scrollX}px`;
        tooltip.style.display = 'block';
      });

      el.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
        tooltip.style.display = 'none';
        tooltip.innerHTML = '';
      });
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

#### What’s happening?
* The iframe loads `/products.html` **in the background**.
* `data-preview-id` holds a **CSS selector** that points to the element you want to preview.
* On `mouseenter`, we pull that element from the iframe’s DOM, deep‑clone it, and drop the clone into a positioned `<div>` that works as a tooltip.
* On `mouseleave` we hide the tooltip.

#### Things to consider
* **CSS** – The cloned element inherits styles from the **current page**, not from the iframe’s `<head>`. If the source page uses its own stylesheet you’ll need to import it into the parent page (or copy needed rules into the tooltip container).  
  ```js
  // Example: copy all <link rel="stylesheet"> from iframe to the parent:
  const links = iframe.contentDocument.querySelectorAll('link[rel="stylesheet"]');
  links.forEach(l => {
    const copy = document.createElement('link');
    copy.rel = 'stylesheet';
    copy.href = l.href;
    document.head.appendChild(copy);
  });
  ```
* **Scripts** – Because we only clone markup, any inline scripts inside the source element won’t automatically run. If the element needs its own JS, you could either:
  * Load the script file(s) from the source page into the parent page, or
  * Keep the whole source page inside the iframe and simply **show the iframe itself** (position it like a tooltip); this bypasses cloning entirely but you must size the iframe manually.
* **Security** – The clone lives in the same DOM as your page, so *any* JavaScript inside the source markup will execute with the same privileges as your site. If the target page isn’t fully under your control, **sanitize** the clone first (e.g., strip `<script>` tags, remove event‑handler attributes).

---

## 3. Solution #2 – AJAX (`fetch`) + `DOMParser`

### When to use it
* You only need the **static markup** (HTML and CSS) of the target element, not the full runtime environment.
* Both pages share the **same origin** **or** the target provides the proper **CORS** header (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain).

### How it works
1. Use `fetch()` to request the full HTML of the other page.
2. Parse that HTML string into a detached `Document` using `DOMParser`.
3. Pick the element you want (by ID, class, data‑attribute, etc.).
4. Insert that element into your tooltip.

### Minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Hover preview via fetch</title>
<style>
  .preview {
    position: absolute;
    background:#fff;
    border:1px solid #ccc;
    padding:8px;
    max-width:300px;
    max-height:200px;
    overflow:auto;
    display:none;
    box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.2);
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<a href="#" class="trigger" data-page="/products.html" data-id="product-42">
  Hover for product 42
</a>

<div id="tooltip" class="preview"></div>

<script>
  // Cache for fetched pages → reduces network chatter on subsequent hovers
  const pageCache = {};

  document.querySelectorAll('.trigger').forEach(el => {
    const pageUrl = el.dataset.page;
    const targetId = el.dataset.id; // we’ll look for #product-42

    let hoverTimer; // optional debounce to avoid rapid firing

    el.addEventListener('mouseenter', async (e) => {
      // simple debounce: only act if the pointer stays >200 ms
      hoverTimer = setTimeout(async () => {
        // 1️⃣ Get HTML (cached if already fetched)
        let html = pageCache[pageUrl];
        if (!html) {
          const resp = await fetch(pageUrl, { credentials: 'same-origin' });
          html = await resp.text();
          pageCache[pageUrl] = html;
        }

        // 2️⃣ Parse into a detached document
        const parser = new DOMParser();
        const doc = parser.parseFromString(html, 'text/html');

        // 3️⃣ Find the element we want
        const source = doc.getElementById(targetId);
        if (!source) return;

        // 4️⃣ Clone and inject
        const clone = source.cloneNode(true);
        // Optional: strip IDs to avoid duplication
        clone.removeAttribute('id');

        // 5️⃣ Insert into tooltip
        const tooltip = document.getElementById('tooltip');
        tooltip.innerHTML = '';      // clear previous content
        tooltip.appendChild(clone);

        // 6️⃣ Position near mouse
        tooltip.style.top = `${e.pageY + 8}px`;
        tooltip.style.left = `${e.pageX + 8}px`;
        tooltip.style.display = 'block';
      }, 200);
    });

    el.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
      clearTimeout(hoverTimer);
      const tooltip = document.getElementById('tooltip');
      tooltip.style.display = 'none';
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

#### Advantages
* **No iframe** – the preview sits directly in the page’s DOM, making it easier to style.
* **Fine‑grained caching** – you can keep the fetched HTML in a JavaScript object, localStorage, or IndexedDB to avoid re‑fetching.

#### Caveats
* **CSS isolation** – same as before, you need the source page’s CSS to be available in the parent page, or you must copy the relevant rules.  
  *Quick trick*: copy the `<style>` blocks from the fetched document:
  ```js
  doc.querySelectorAll('style, link[rel="stylesheet"]').forEach(styleEl => {
    document.head.appendChild(styleEl.cloneNode(true));
  });
  ```
* **Scripts are ignored** – any `<script>` tags in the fetched HTML are not executed. If the preview element depends on JS, you’ll have to load those scripts manually (or revert to the iframe solution).

