Below is a **complete, end‑to‑end, declarative‑first** solution that works on a **list view** (any table) in **ServiceNow UI‑Builder / Classic UI**.  

It gives you:

* a **list‑action button** that appears in the list header (or the three‑dot “more actions” menu)  
* a **popup (GlideModal) that contains a simple file‑upload form** (the same UI you see when you add an attachment from a form)  
* a **Script Include** that receives the uploaded file and **adds the same attachment to every record you selected** on the list.

You can copy‑paste the snippets, change a few names, and you’ll have a working feature in minutes – **no UI Policy, no Flow Designer, no extra plugins** required.

---

## 1. Create the **List Action** (UI Action)

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | `Attach document to many` |
| **Table** | *your table* (e.g. `incident`) |
| **Action type** | `List` |
| **Show on form** | *unchecked* |
| **Client** | `true` |
| **Script** | *(see script below)* |

```javascript
/* UI Action – List type (client side) */
function attachMany() {
    // 1️⃣ Grab the list of records the user has checked
    var selected = g_list.getChecked();        // array of sys_id strings

    if (!selected || selected.length === 0) {
        alert('Please select at least one record first.');
        return;
    }

    // 2️⃣ Pass the sys_id list to the modal via a URL parameter
    var modal = new GlideModal('attach_many_modal');
    modal.setTitle('Attach a document to the selected records');
    // Encode the list as a comma‑separated string
    modal.addParm('sysparm_selected', selected.join(','));

    // 3️⃣ Show the modal (it will load the UI page we create in step 2)
    modal.render();
}
```

> **What the script does**  
> * `g_list.getChecked()` returns the sys_ids of the rows the user ticked.  
> * It opens a **GlideModal** that points to a UI page we will create next (`attach_many_modal`).  
> * The modal receives the selected sys_ids via a URL parameter (`sysparm_selected`).

> **Remember:** The UI Action script **must be wrapped in a function** (any name) and the *Action name* on the list button must reference that function (`attachMany()` in the example).

---

## 2. Create the **UI Page** that renders inside the modal

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | `attach_many_modal` |
| **HTML** | *(see HTML snippet below)* |
| **Client Script** | *(see client‑side snippet below)* |
| **Server Script** | *(see server‑side snippet below)* |

### 2.1 UI Page – HTML (the form)

```html
<!-- UI Page: attach_many_modal -->
<div class="modal-body">
    <form id="attach_many_form" enctype="multipart/form-data">
        <input type="hidden" name="selected_ids" id="selected_ids" value="">
        <div class="form-group">
            <label for="attachment_file">Choose a file to attach</label><br>
            <input type="file" id="attachment_file" name="attachment_file" required>
        </div>
        <br>
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Attach to all selected records</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-default" id="cancelBtn">Cancel</button>
    </form>
    <div id="statusMsg" style="margin-top:10px;"></div>
</div>
```

### 2.2 UI Page – Client Script (handle the submit)

```javascript
/* UI Page client script – runs inside the modal */
(function () {
    // 1️⃣ Pull the selected sys_ids that were passed from the UI Action
    var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
    var selected = params.get('sysparm_selected') || '';
    document.getElementById('selected_ids').value = selected;   // hidden field

    // 2️⃣ Wire up the Cancel button
    document.getElementById('cancelBtn').onclick = function () {
        // Close the modal – GlideModal exposes a global method for this
        if (typeof GlideModal != 'undefined' && GlideModal.getCurrent) {
            GlideModal.getCurrent().destroy();
        }
    };

    // 3️⃣ Intercept the form submit, send the file via GlideAjax
    document.getElementById('attach_many_form').onsubmit = function (e) {
        e.preventDefault();

        var fileInput = document.getElementById('attachment_file');
        if (!fileInput.files.length) {
            alert('Please pick a file.');
            return;
        }

        var file = fileInput.files[0];
        var reader = new FileReader();

        // Convert the file to Base64 (required for GlideAjax payload)
        reader.onload = function (event) {
            var content = event.target.result.split(',')[1]; // strip the data:...;base64, part

            // Call the Script Include (see step 3)
            var ga = new GlideAjax('AttachManyHelper');
            ga.addParam('sysparm_name', 'attachToRecords');
            ga.addParam('sysparm_sys_ids', selected);   // comma‑separated list
            ga.addParam('sysparm_file_name', file.name);
            ga.addParam('sysparm_mime_type', file.type);
            ga.addParam('sysparm_file_content', content);
            ga.getXMLAnswer(function (response) {
                // response is a JSON string: {"status":"ok","msg":"X records updated"}
                var result = JSON.parse(response);
                var el = document.getElementById('statusMsg');
                if (result.status === 'ok') {
                    el.innerHTML = '<div class="alert alert-success">' + result.msg + '</div>';
                    // optional: close after a short delay
                    setTimeout(function () {
                        if (typeof GlideModal != 'undefined' && GlideModal.getCurrent) {
                            GlideModal.getCurrent().destroy();
                        }
                    }, 1500);
                } else {
                    el.innerHTML = '<div class="alert alert-danger">Error: ' + result.msg + '</div>';
                }
            });
        };
        reader.readAsDataURL(file);
    };
})();
```

### 2.3 UI Page – Server Script (just to satisfy the UI Page; all work happens in the Script Include)

```javascript
/* UI Page server script – not used for the attachment logic.
   It can stay empty or just return a dummy response. */
```

> **Why Base64?**  
> `GlideAjax` can only send text, not binary blobs. Converting the file to a Base64 string lets us ship the content safely, then decode it server‑side and write the attachment.

---

## 3. **Script Include** – the real workhorse

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | `AttachManyHelper` |
| **Client Callable** | `true` |
| **Application** | *your scoped app* (or global) |
| **Script** | *(see script below)* |

```javascript
var AttachManyHelper = Class.create();
AttachManyHelper.prototype = Object.extendsObject(AbstractAjaxProcessor, {

    /**
     * Entry point called from the UI page's client script.
     *
     * Expected parameters (all strings):
     *   sysparm_sys_ids        – comma‑separated list of record sys_ids
     *   sysparm_file_name      – original file name (incl. extension)
     *   sysparm_mime_type      – e.g. "application/pdf"
     *   sysparm_file_content   – Base64‑encoded file content (no data: prefix)
     */
    attachToRecords: function () {
        var sysIds      = this.getParameter('sysparm_sys_ids');
        var fileName    = this.getParameter('sysparm_file_name');
        var mimeType    = this.getParameter('sysparm_mime_type');
        var fileB64     = this.getParameter('sysparm_file_content');

        if (!sysIds || !fileName || !mimeType || !fileB64) {
            return JSON.stringify({status: 'error', msg: 'Missing parameters'});
        }

        // Decode Base64 to a binary stream (GlideSysAttachment expects a js ByteArray)
        var fileBytes = Packages.org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Base64.decodeBase64(fileB64);

        var ca = new GlideSysAttachment();
        var tableName = this.getTableName(); // The table where this Script Include lives (e.g. incident if placed there)
        // If you want this to be reusable for any table, you could also pass the table name as a param.

        var count = 0;
        var ids = sysIds.split(',');

        ids.forEach(function (sysId) {
            // 1️⃣ Verify the record exists (optional but nice)
            var gr = new GlideRecord(tableName);
            if (gr.get(sysId)) {
                // 2️⃣ Create the attachment for **this** record
                ca.write(gr, fileName, mimeType, fileBytes);
                count++;
            }
        });

        var msg = count + ' attachment(s) added to ' + count + ' record(s).';
        return JSON.stringify({status: 'ok', msg: msg});
    },

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper – returns the table name the UI Action is attached to.
    // If you put the Script Include in Global, just hard‑code it (e.g. 'incident')
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    getTableName: function () {
        // The UI Action will pass the table name automatically if you store the Script Include
        // in the same application scope, otherwise override with a fixed value.
        // Example for a generic solution:
        // return this.getParameter('sysparm_table') || 'incident';
        return 'incident'; // <-- change to your table (or make it a param)
    },

    type: 'AttachManyHelper'
});
```

### What this script does

1. **Collects the parameters** that the client script sent (list of sys_ids, file name, MIME type, and Base64 content).  
2. **Decodes the Base64 string** into a byte array (`fileBytes`).  
3. Instantiates `GlideSysAttachment` and loops over every sys_id, writing the same file to each record.  
4. Returns a **tiny JSON payload** (`status`, `msg`) that the client script displays in the modal.

> **Scoping tip** – If you want the same UI action to work on *any* table, move the Script Include to **Global** and add an extra parameter `sysparm_table` (the UI Action can send `g_list.getTableName()`).

---

## 4. Wire everything together (quick sanity‑check)

