## 1.  Why a CircleMarker can’t hold text

`L.circleMarker` (the black dot you already use) is an **SVG‑based** layer – it only knows about geometric properties (radius, colour, opacity, …).  
If you want a number *inside* that dot you have two practical options:

| Option | How it works | Pros | Cons |
|--------|--------------|------|------|
| **Tooltip** (`bindTooltip({permanent:true})`) | Keeps the number attached to the marker, but the tooltip is an HTML element that sits **on top of** the SVG and can be slightly displaced. | Very little code. | The number isn’t truly *inside* the circle; it can look offset on high‑DPI screens. |
| **DivIcon** (`L.marker` + `L.divIcon`) | The marker is rendered as a **plain HTML `<div>`** that you can style any way you like – a black circle with a number centred inside. | Gives you pixel‑perfect control of size, colour, font, etc. | You have to replace the `L.circleMarker` you currently use. |

The DivIcon solution is the cleanest and works on every browser, so we’ll go with that.

---

## 2.  CSS for the train “dot”

