Below is a **complete, production‑ready example** of how you can model a real‑time chat system with **MongoDB + Mongoose** in a Node.js project.

It includes:

| Entity | What it stores | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|----------------|
| **User** | profile data, status, contacts | Basic identity & presence |
| **Chat** | a conversation (group or 1‑to‑1) | Holds participants, metadata, unread counters |
| **Message** | text, media, reactions, timestamps | The actual chat payload |
| **MessageRead** *(optional)* | per‑user read receipts | Allows “seen‑by” functionality without bloating the Message doc |

> **Design decisions** – we keep **messages in a separate collection** (instead of embedding them in a chat) so a chat can grow to millions of messages without hitting the 16 MB BSON limit, and we can paginate efficiently.  Relationships are expressed with **ObjectId references** and we use **Mongoose indexes** for fast look‑ups.

---

## 1️⃣ Install dependencies

```bash
npm i mongoose
# (optional, for typing)
npm i -D @types/mongoose
```

---

## 2️⃣ Connection helper (`db.js`)

