## 6️⃣ Production Tips & Gotchas

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---------|----------------|
| **Scalability** | Keep messages **sharded** by `chat` (MongoDB sharding key). 0‑index on `{ chat: 1, createdAt: -1 }` for fast pagination. |
| **TTL for temporary data** | If you store **typing indicators** or **presence** elsewhere (e.g., Redis), use TTL. |
| **Media storage** | Store actual files on object storage (S3, Cloudinary) and only keep a URL + metadata in `payload`. |
| **Index size** | `participants.user` in `Chat` can become large for mega‑groups; consider a separate **ChatMember** collection if groups > 10 k users. |
| **Data privacy** | Use **soft deletes** (`isDeleted`) + periodic background job to purge after a retention period if required by law. |
| **Real‑time delivery** | Use **Socket.io**, **WebSocket**, or **Server‑Sent Events**; when a message is saved, emit it to all `{ chatId }` rooms. |
| **Read receipts** | The `MessageRead` collection lets you query *unread* efficiently (`$gt` on `lastReadAt`). |
| **Testing** | Use an in‑memory MongoDB server (`mongodb-memory-server`) in your Jest/Mocha suite. |
| **Schema migrations** | When adding a new field, give it a default (`null`/`false`) and write a one‑off script to back‑fill if required. |

---

## 🎉 TL;DR – The Minimal Four Collections

```js
User     -> { email, username, avatarUrl, contacts… }
Chat     -> { type, participants[ {user, role, lastReadAt} ], lastMessage }
Message  -> { chat, sender, type, text, payload, reactions, readBy }
MessageRead (optional) -> { user, chat, lastReadAt, lastReadMessageId }
```

All three core schemas are **fully indexed**, **populated**, and come with **helper services** to:

* create/find direct or group chats
* send & paginate messages
* mark chats as read and compute unread counts
* add/remove reactions

