## 1️⃣ What was missing / could be improved?

| Area | Problem in the current code | Why it matters | What we will change |
|------|-----------------------------|----------------|---------------------|
| **Server library** | You are using `nodejs-websocket` (aka *ws2*). It is small but it does **not** give you a built‑in HTTP server, automatic binary handling, heartbeat, or easy access to the client‑IP. | Harder to add features (binary, keep‑alive, authentication). | Switch to the widely‑used **`ws`** package (the same package that powers the official Node‑WS spec). |
| **Broadcast typo** | `boardcast` – a typo that makes the code harder to read. | Minor, but it hides intent. | Rename to `broadcast`. |
| **Binary messages** | Server never distinguishes text ↔ binary → binary data is sent back as raw bytes, client will treat it as a string. | Files cannot be transferred. | Detect `isBinary` flag, forward the `ArrayBuffer` unchanged, optionally attach a tiny JSON header. |
| **Message format** | Plain string `from ip的name：msg`. No way to add extra fields later (timestamp, type, file name…). | Extending the protocol requires a full rewrite. | Use **JSON** for every text message (`{type:"text", name, ip, body, ts}`) and a **binary envelope** (`{type:"file", name, ip, fileName, ts}` + raw bytes). |
| **Client‑IP** | Front‑end pulls the *public* IP with an external HTTP call (`api.ipify.org`). That returns the *client’s* public IP, not the *socket* IP, and you have a race condition because you call `send()` before the request finishes. | Wrong IP shown, possible “undefined”. | Get the IP from the socket on the server (`req.socket.remoteAddress`) and send it back to the client as part of the welcome message. |
| **Error handling / keep‑alive** | `conn.on("error")` just echoes the error string back to the client. No ping/pong, no graceful cleanup. | Broken connections stay in memory → memory leak. | Implement a **heartbeat** (ping every 30 s) and close dead sockets. |
| **Hard‑coded host** | `socket = new WebSocket("ws://38.46.30.148:3000");` – you have to edit the code each time you move the server. | Not portable. | Use a relative URL (`new WebSocket(`ws://${location.host}`)`) or read the host from an environment variable (`process.env.WS_HOST`). |
| **HTML/JS structure** | The script re‑assigns `socket.onmessage` **twice**, overwriting the first handler. It also mixes DOM manipulation with logic. | The first handler (text) is lost; binary handling never runs. | Register **only one** `socket.onmessage` that distinguishes the data type. Keep DOM‑updates separate from protocol handling. |
| **Security** | No origin check, no rate‑limit, no size‑limit on binary. | Anyone can flood the server. | Add an origin whitelist, reject >5 MB files, throttle messages per second. |

---

## 2️⃣ Revised Backend (Node + `ws`)

> **File:** `server.js`  
> **Run:** `npm i ws` (or `npm i ws express` if you want HTTP static files) → `node server.js`

```js
/**
 *  WebSocket chat server – production ready version
 *  ------------------------------------------------
 *  Features
 *   • Text and binary (file) broadcast
 *   • Automatic timestamp & client‑IP
 *   • JSON message envelope
 *   • Heart‑beat (ping/pong) to drop dead sockets
 *   • Simple origin whitelist (change to your domain)
 *   • Max payload limits (5 MiB for binary, 2 KiB for text)
 *   • Graceful error handling & logging
 */

'use strict';
const http   = require('http');          // optional – you can attach ws to any http server
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const os = require('os');

// === Configuration =========================================================
const PORT           = process.env.PORT ?? 3000;                     // listen port
const HOST           = process.env.HOST ?? '0.0.0.0';                 // 0.0.0.0 = all interfaces
const ORIGIN_WHITELIST = [
  // e.g. 'http://mydomain.com', 'https://mydomain.com'
]; // leave empty to accept any origin (unsafe in prod)

const MAX_TEXT_BYTES = 2 * 1024;      // 2 KB per text message
const MAX_BINARY_BYTES = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MiB per file upload
const HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL = 30_000;   // 30 s

// === Helper utilities =======================================================
function now() {
  return new Date().toISOString();
}

/**
 * Broadcast a payload to all **connected** sockets, optionally excluding one.
 *
 * @param {Buffer|String} data   Data to send (binary or string)
 * @param {WebSocket} [exclude]  Socket that should *not* receive the data
 */
function broadcast(data, exclude) {
  wss.clients.forEach(client => {
    if (client !== exclude && client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
      client.send(data);
    }
  });
}

/**
 * Attach a "ping" every HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL ms and close sockets that don’t answer.
 */
function startHeartbeat(ws) {
  ws.isAlive = true;
  ws.on('pong', () => { ws.isAlive = true; });

  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    if (ws.isAlive === false) {
      console.warn(`[${now()}] No pong → terminating ${ws._socket.remoteAddress}`);
      return ws.terminate();
    }
    ws.isAlive = false;
    ws.ping();
  }, HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL);

  ws.on('close', () => clearInterval(interval));
}

// === HTTP Server (optional) =================================================
const httpServer = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  // Simple health‑check endpoint
  if (req.url === '/ping') {
    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('pong');
    return;
  }
  // In a real project you could also serve static files (your HTML) here.
  res.writeHead(404);
  res.end();
});

// === WebSocket Server =======================================================
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ server: httpServer });

wss.on('connection', (ws, req) => {
  // ---- 1️⃣ Basic validation ------------------------------------------------
  const origin = req.headers.origin;
  if (ORIGIN_WHITELIST.length && !ORIGIN_WHITELIST.includes(origin)) {
    console.warn(`[${now()}] Rejected connection from disallowed origin: ${origin}`);
    ws.close(1008, 'Origin not allowed');
    return;
  }

  // ---- 2️⃣ Store client meta ------------------------------------------------
  const clientIP = req.socket.remoteAddress; // most reliable IP (might be IPv6)
  ws.meta = {
    ip   : clientIP,
    name : null, // will be filled after first "login" message
  };

  console.log(`[${now()}] New connection from ${clientIP}`);

  // start heartbeat monitoring for this socket
  startHeartbeat(ws);

  // ---- 3️⃣ Welcome message (sent only once) --------------------------------
  const welcome = {
    type: 'welcome',
    ts  : now(),
    ip  : clientIP,
    msg : 'You are connected – please send a "login" JSON message first.'
  };
  ws.send(JSON.stringify(welcome));

  // ---- 4️⃣ Main message handler ---------------------------------------------
  ws.on('message', (data, isBinary) => {
    // --------------------------------------------------------------- Text ----------
    if (!isBinary) {
      // Guard against huge text payloads
      if (data.length > MAX_TEXT_BYTES) {
        ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'error', msg:`Text payload > ${MAX_TEXT_BYTES} bytes`}));
        return;
      }

      let payload;
      try {
        payload = JSON.parse(data);
      } catch (_) {
        // old‑school plain‑string fallback (kept for compatibility)
        payload = { type: 'text', body: data.toString() };
      }

      // ----------------------------------------------------------- Login ----------
      if (payload.type === 'login') {
        const name = String(payload.name || '').trim();
        if (!name) {
          ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'error', msg:'login: name required'}));
          return;
        }
        ws.meta.name = name;
        // broadcast system notice that a new user arrived
        const sys = {
          type: 'system',
          ts  : now(),
          msg : `${name} has entered the chat`,
        };
        broadcast(JSON.stringify(sys));
        console.log(`[${now()}] ${clientIP} logged in as "${name}"`);
        return;
      }

      // ------------------------------------------------------------ Text ----------
      if (payload.type === 'text') {
        const username = ws.meta.name || '匿名';
        const txt = {
          type : 'text',
          ts   : now(),
          ip   : ws.meta.ip,
          name : username,
          body : String(payload.body ?? '')
        };
        broadcast(JSON.stringify(txt));
        return;
      }

      // ---------------------------------------------------------- Unknown ----------
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'error', msg:`Unsupported message type "${payload.type}"`}));
      return;
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------- Binary -------
    // Binary messages are expected to be **file transfers**.
    // The first 4 bytes contain a little JSON header length (Uint32LE),
    // followed by the JSON header, then the raw file bytes.
    //
    // ┌───────┐ ┌─────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────┐
    // │ 4B   │ │ JSON header (UTF‑8) │ │   file binary data    │
    // └───────┘ └─────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘
    //
    // Example header:
    //   { "type":"file", "name":"Alice", "ip":"1.2.3.4",
    //     "fileName":"cat.png", "mime":"image/png", "ts":... }
    //
    // This structure lets us keep a *single* WebSocket.send() call
    // on the client side (no need for a separate control channel).

    if (data.length > MAX_BINARY_BYTES) {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'error', msg:`Binary payload > ${MAX_BINARY_BYTES} bytes`}));
      return;
    }

    // Parse binary envelope
    const headerLen = data.readUInt32LE(0);
    const headerBuf = data.slice(4, 4 + headerLen);
    let header;
    try {
      header = JSON.parse(headerBuf.toString('utf8'));
    } catch (e) {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'error', msg:'Invalid binary envelope header'}));
      return;
    }

    // Attach missing meta (if client didn't send a name)
    header.ip   = ws.meta.ip;
    header.name = ws.meta.name || '匿名';
    header.ts   = now();

    // Re‑build the full buffer (header length + header json + raw file)
    const newHeader = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(header), 'utf8');
    const newHeaderLenBuf = Buffer.alloc(4);
    newHeaderLenBuf.writeUInt32LE(newHeader.length, 0);
    const finalBuf = Buffer.concat([newHeaderLenBuf, newHeader, data.slice(4 + headerLen)]);

    // Broadcast to everyone (including the sender – they will receive a copy)
    broadcast(finalBuf);
  });

  // ---- 5️⃣ Connection closed -------------------------------------------------
  ws.on('close', (code, reason) => {
    const name = ws.meta.name || clientIP;
    console.log(`[${now()}] ${name} disconnected (code ${code})`);
    const sys = {
      type: 'system',
      ts  : now(),
      msg : `${name} left the chat`,
    };
    broadcast(JSON.stringify(sys));
  });

  // ---- 6️⃣ Error handling ----------------------------------------------------
  ws.on('error', err => {
    console.error(`[${now()}] Socket error from ${clientIP}:`, err);
    // The socket will be closed automatically after an error.
  });
});

// ==== Global health‑check (kill on Ctrl‑C) ===================================
process.on('SIGINT', () => {
  console.log('\nGracefully shutting down …');
  wss.clients.forEach(c => c.terminate());
  httpServer.close(() => process.exit(0));
});

// ==== Start listening =========================================================
httpServer.listen(PORT, HOST, () => {
  console.log(`[${now()}] WebSocket server listening on ws://${HOST}:${PORT}`);
});
```

### What changed in the server?

| Feature | Implementation |
|---------|-----------------|
| **JSON envelope** | Every text message is `{type:"text", name, ip, body, ts}`; binary messages start with a 4‑byte header length + JSON header. |
| **Login flow** | The client sends a first *login* message `{type:"login", name:"Alice"}`; the server stores the name and notifies everybody. |
| **Binary file support** | No `socket.send(arrayBuffer)` directly – we prepend a tiny JSON header that contains file name, mime‑type, sender, timestamp. The server just forwards the whole buffer. |
| **Heartbeat** | A ping/pong loop (`HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL = 30 s`) drops dead sockets. |
| **IP handling** | The server extracts the remote socket address (`req.socket.remoteAddress`) and injects it into all outgoing messages. The front‑end no longer needs `axios` to query a public service. |
| **Safety limits** | Payload size caps (`MAX_TEXT_BYTES`, `MAX_BINARY_BYTES`). |
| **Origin whitelist** | `ORIGIN_WHITELIST` can be filled with your domains – Example: `['https://mychat.example.com']`. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | `SIGINT` handler closes every client before exiting. |

---

## 3️⃣ Revised Front‑End (HTML + JS)

> We keep the same visual layout but **clean up the logic**, make the WebSocket **single‑source‑of‑truth**, and match the server’s JSON protocol.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="zh-CN">
