Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** that shows how you can let a visitor type any text into an HTML form and, with a tiny PHP script, write that text into a **plain‑text file** on the server.

---

## 1. The HTML part – a simple form

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Create a Text File</title>
</head>
<body>

<h2>Enter the text you want to save</h2>

<form action="save‑text.php" method="post">
    <!-- 1️⃣ The text the user writes -->
    <textarea name="content" rows="10" cols="50" placeholder="Type something…" required></textarea>
    <br><br>

    <!-- 2️⃣ Optional: let the user choose a file name (no extension) -->
    <label for="filename">File name (optional, default will be random):</label>
    <input type="text" id="filename" name="filename" pattern="[A-Za-z0-9_\-]{1,30}"
           title="Only letters, numbers, dash and underscore (max 30 chars)">
    <br><br>

    <button type="submit">Create Text File</button>
</form>

</body>
</html>
```

* `method="post"` – the text is sent via HTTP POST (safer than GET for large content).  
* `textarea` – where the user writes the custom text.  
* The optional `filename` field lets the user decide a name; otherwise a random one will be generated.

---

## 2. The PHP part – `save-text.php`

```php
<?php
/* -----------------------------------------------------------
 * save-text.php  – writes user‑supplied text to a .txt file
 * ----------------------------------------------------------- */

// 1️⃣  Configuration (adjust as needed)
$targetDir = __DIR__ . '/texts/';          // folder where files will be stored
$defaultExt = '.txt';                     // we always use .txt
$maxFileSize = 5 * 1024 * 1024;           // 5 MiB – limit for safety

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Helpers
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Clean a filename – keep only safe chars, limit length.
 */
function sanitizeFileName(string $name): string
{
    // Remove anything that is not a‑z, 0‑9, dash or underscore
    $name = preg_replace('/[^A-Za-z0-9_\-]/', '', $name);
    // Trim to a reasonable length (30 chars, you can change)
    $name = substr($name, 0, 30);
    return $name;
}

/**
 * Return a unique filename when user does not give one.
 */
function generateRandomFileName(): string
{
    // uniqid() + random_bytes for extra entropy
    return uniqid('txt_', true) . bin2hex(random_bytes(4));
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Validate POST data
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] !== 'POST') {
    http_response_code(405);
    exit('Method Not Allowed');
}

// 3a. The text that should go into the file
if (!isset($_POST['content'])) {
    exit('No content received.');
}
$content = $_POST['content'];

// Optional – enforce a maximum size before we write it
if (strlen($content) > $maxFileSize) {
    exit('Your text is too large (max '.($maxFileSize/1024/1024).' MiB).');
}

// 3b. Decide the file name
$rawName = trim($_POST['filename'] ?? '');
if ($rawName !== '') {
    $fileBase = sanitizeFileName($rawName);
    // If the user gave an empty or completely stripped name, fall back
    if ($fileBase === '') {
        $fileBase = generateRandomFileName();
    }
} else {
    $fileBase = generateRandomFileName();
}
$fileName = $fileBase . $defaultExt;

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Make sure the target folder exists and is writable
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
if (!is_dir($targetDir)) {
    // try to create it (readable+writeable for the web‑server user)
    if (!mkdir($targetDir, 0755, true)) {
        exit('Unable to create target directory.');
    }
}

// Re‑check permissions – a good safety net
if (!is_writable($targetDir)) {
    exit('Target directory is not writable.');
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  Write the file
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
$fullPath = $targetDir . $fileName;

// Use file_put_contents – it handles fopen/fwrite/fclose internally.
// The flag LOCK_EX prevents race‑conditions if several users write at once.
$result = file_put_contents($fullPath, $content, LOCK_EX);

if ($result === false) {
    exit('Failed to write the file.');
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣  Reply to the user
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>File created</title>
</head>
<body>
<h2>✅  Your file has been created!</h2>

<p>
    <strong>File name:</strong> <?= htmlspecialchars($fileName) ?><br>
    <strong>Location on server:</strong> <?= htmlspecialchars($fullPath) ?>
</p>

<p>
    <a href="download.php?file=<?= urlencode($fileName) ?>">Download the file</a>
    &nbsp;|&nbsp;
    <a href="index.html">Create another one</a>
</p>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the script does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Configuration** | `$targetDir` is the folder where the `.txt` files live (`/texts/` relative to the script). Change it to any writable directory. |
| **Sanitisation** | `sanitizeFileName()` removes every character that could be used to break out of the directory (`../`, spaces, special symbols, etc.). |
| **Random names** | If the user leaves the “file name” field empty, we generate a unique name (`txt_5f47e1a03c3c01.75237466a5c7c4b0.txt`). |
| **Safety checks** | - Only POST requests are accepted.<br>- Size limit (`$maxFileSize`).<br>- Ensure the target directory exists and is writable.<br>- Use `LOCK_EX` when writing. |
| **Writing** | `file_put_contents()` writes the content atomically (opens, locks, writes, closes). |
| **Result page** | Shows the file name and a **download link** (we’ll create a tiny `download.php` in the next section). |

---

## 3. (Optional) A tiny download script – `download.php`

If you want visitors to be able to download the file they just created, a small “proxy” script is safer than linking directly to the file system location.

```php
<?php
// download.php – streams a .txt file from the texts/ folder
$targetDir = __DIR__ . '/texts/';

if (!isset($_GET['file'])) {
    http_response_code(400);
    exit('Missing file parameter.');
}

// Basic sanitisation – allow only our generated pattern
$file = basename($_GET['file']);          // strip any directory parts
if (!preg_match('/^[A-Za-z0-9_\-\.]+$/', $file)) {
    http_response_code(400);
    exit('Invalid file name.');
}

$fullPath = $targetDir . $file;

if (!is_file($fullPath) || !is_readable($fullPath)) {
    http_response_code(404);
    exit('File not found.');
}

// Send correct headers
header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
header('Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="'.basename($file).'"');
header('Content-Length: ' . filesize($fullPath));

// Output the file
readfile($fullPath);
exit;
```

* `basename()` ensures that a malicious `../secret.txt` can’t sneak in.  
* The regular expression further guarantees the filename is only composed of safe characters.  

---

## 4. Permissions & Security Checklist

| Issue | What to do |
|-------|------------|
| **Directory write permission** | The folder (`texts/`) must be writable by the web‑server user (e.g., `www-data`, `apache`, `nginx`). `chmod 755` is enough if the owner is the web‑user. |
| **Prevent arbitrary file access** | Never take a filename from the user and prepend `../` or any other path – always use `basename()` and a whitelist regex (as shown in `download.php`). |
| **Size limits** | Set a reasonable `$maxFileSize` (5 MiB in the example) to stop a user from filling your disk. |
| **File name collisions** | Using `uniqid()` + random bytes virtually guarantees uniqueness. If you do let the user pick a name, either auto‑append a number or refuse overwriting (`if (file_exists($fullPath)) …`). |
| **Cross‑site scripting (XSS)** | Always `htmlspecialchars()` any value you echo back to the browser (file name, messages). |
| **Server‑side execution** | Store files **outside** the web‑root or give them a `.txt` extension only. Never allow `.php` or other executable extensions to be written. |
| **Rate‑limiting** | For a public site you may want to throttle the number of writes per IP (e.g., 10 per minute) – this can be done with a simple MySQL table or a memcached counter. |

---

