## 3. Database Connection (`config.php`)

We’ll use **PDO** – it gives us prepared statements, exceptions, and works with many DB engines.

```php
<?php
// config.php – include this file wherever you need DB access
declare(strict_types=1);

$host = 'localhost';
$db   = 'demo_auth';
$user = 'root';          // <-- change to your MySQL user
$pass = '';              // <-- change to your MySQL password
$charset = 'utf8mb4';

// DSN (Data Source Name)
$dsn = "mysql:host=$host;dbname=$db;charset=$charset";

$options = [
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE            => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION, // throw exceptions
    PDO::ATTR_DEFAULT_FETCH_MODE => PDO::FETCH_ASSOC,       // fetch assoc arrays
    PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES   => false,                  // use native prepares
];

try {
    $pdo = new PDO($dsn, $user, $pass, $options);
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    // In production you would log this instead of echoing
    exit('Database connection failed: ' . $e->getMessage());
}
```

> **Tip:** Keep `config.php` **outside** the web root (e.g., `../config.php`) when you go to production, or use environment variables.

