## 9. Security‑focused checklist (so you don’t forget anything)

| ✅ Item | Why it matters | How it’s implemented in the code |
|--------|----------------|---------------------------------|
| **Prepared statements (PDO)** | Prevents *SQL injection*. | All DB queries use `prepare()` + bound parameters. |
| **Password hashing (`password_hash`)** | Stores only a one‑way hash, never the plain password. | `password_hash(..., PASSWORD_DEFAULT)`. |
| **Password verification (`password_verify`)** | Constant‑time compare avoids timing attacks. | `password_verify($plain, $hash)`. |
| **CSRF tokens** | Stops cross‑site request forgery on POST forms. | Random token in session → hidden input. |
| **Session fixation protection** | Regenerate ID after log‑in/log‑out. | `session_regenerate_id(true)`. |
| **`HttpOnly` + `Secure` cookie flags** | Prevents JavaScript access and forces HTTPS. | Done in `session_start([...])`. |
| **Input validation & sanitisation** | Guarantees data shape and avoids XSS. | Server‑side checks + `htmlspecialchars` on output. |
| **Error handling** | Don’t leak DB details to the user. | Generic messages; log details with `error_log()`. |
| **Rate limiting / brute‑force protection** | (optional) stop credential stuffing. | Not covered in the simple demo – you can add a simple counter in the DB or use tools like `fail2ban`. |
| **HTTPS everywhere** | Encrypts credentials in transit. | Deploy behind TLS (e.g., Let’s Encrypt). |

